window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["imhans-2025"] = {"id": "imhans-2025", "title": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2025", "fullTitle": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2025, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, booklet 25035 A)", "sub": "75 real questions · official LBS key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "When a counsellor uses paraphrasing as an active listening skill, the primary goal is to:", "options": {"a": "Interpret the hidden meaning behind the client's statement.", "b": "Repeat the client's exact words to ensure accuracy.", "c": "Restate the client's message in the counsellor's own words to check understanding and show empathy.", "d": "Provide advice based on the client's expressed concern."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: in paraphrasing, the counsellor restates the essence of what the client said in the counsellor's own, usually fewer, words, so the client can confirm or correct it and feel heard. Paraphrasing is one of the basic listening microskills taught by Allen Ivey (1968) in microcounselling, alongside open questions, minimal encouragers, reflection of feeling and summarising. It grows out of the person-centred tradition of Carl Rogers (1951), where empathy means understanding the client's world from the inside and showing that you have understood it. A good paraphrase does three jobs: it checks the counsellor's understanding, it communicates attention and empathy, and it hands the client a clearer mirror of their own message. It stays inside the client's frame of reference and adds nothing new. Example: Meera says, \"My manager keeps loading me with work and then calls me slow.\" The counsellor replies, \"So you are given more than anyone could finish, and then blamed for not finishing it.\" Meera can answer \"Yes, exactly\" or \"Not quite, it is more that...\", and either way the counsellor learns something. Paraphrasing targets content (the facts and ideas). Reflection of feeling targets the emotion (\"You sound exhausted and unfairly treated\"). Summarising pulls together a longer stretch of talk. Option c is right because it names both purposes, checking understanding and conveying empathy, and the defining feature, the counsellor's own words. Exam trap: echoing the client's exact words is parroting. Repeating a key word or two can work as a minimal encourager, but a full verbatim repeat sounds mechanical and proves nothing about understanding. Offering a hidden meaning is interpretation, an influencing skill that moves into the counsellor's frame of reference.", "topic": "Paraphrasing in counselling"}, {"q": "Which test is most sensitive for detecting subtle frontal lobe executive dysfunction in early neurodegenerative conditions?", "options": {"a": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST)", "b": "Benton Visual Retention Test (BVRT)", "c": "Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure Test (ROCF)", "d": "Finger Tapping Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the classic, most sensitive test for subtle frontal executive dysfunction, especially loss of set shifting and abstract rule learning. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) devised it, and Robert Heaton (1981) wrote the clinical manual. The person sorts response cards under four key cards that differ in colour, form and number. The examiner never states the rule and says only \"right\" or \"wrong\". After a run of correct sorts (ten in the standard version) the rule changes without warning. To succeed, the person must work out a concept from feedback, keep it in mind, notice that it has stopped working and shift to a new one. The main scores are categories completed, perseverative errors (sticking to the old rule despite hearing \"wrong\") and failures to maintain set. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral prefrontal excisions made far more perseverative errors than patients with posterior excisions, which tied the test to the frontal lobes. Simple screens can look normal early in frontotemporal dementia, Parkinson's disease or Huntington's disease, while the WCST already shows rigidity. It is sensitive rather than perfectly specific: diffuse or non-frontal damage can also lower scores. Example: Arjun, 58, passes a memory screen, but on the WCST he keeps sorting by colour long after the rule has moved to form, even while saying he is being told \"wrong\". Option a is right because the WCST directly taxes cognitive flexibility, concept formation and use of feedback, the functions that slip first in frontal disease. Exam trap: the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure has a planning and organisation element, so it tempts, but it is mainly a test of visuoconstruction and visual memory, not of shifting set.", "topic": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test"}, {"q": "DSM-5-TR introduced expanded guidance on cultural formulation. Which tool is emphasized for systematically assessing cultural context in clinical evaluation?", "options": {"a": "Cultural Formulation Interview (CFI)", "b": "Cross-Cultural Checklist (CCC)", "c": "Multicultural Assessment Battery (MAB)", "d": "Global Functioning Scale (GFS)"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Cultural Formulation Interview (CFI) is the DSM tool for systematically assessing how culture shapes a person's problem, explanations, supports and help-seeking. A cultural formulation asks how a person's background affects what they see as the problem, what they think caused it, what stresses and supports surround them, and how they relate to the clinician. DSM-IV (1994) gave only a narrative Outline for Cultural Formulation. DSM-5 (2013) turned this into a structured, semi-structured interview of 16 questions in Section III, with an informant version and supplementary modules (for example on explanatory models, spirituality, older adults, and immigrants and refugees). DSM-5-TR (2022) kept and updated it. The 16 questions fall into four domains: the cultural definition of the problem; cultural perceptions of cause, context and support; cultural factors affecting self-coping and past help-seeking; and cultural factors affecting current help-seeking. The questions are open and use the person's own words rather than a checklist of syndromes. Example: Kabir, 22, from a village in Bihar, complains of weakness and worry about losing semen in his urine. Asked \"What do you think is causing this?\", he describes dhat. The clinician now understands his explanatory model and can frame anxiety treatment in a way Kabir accepts. Option a is right because the CFI is the only one of these that is a real DSM instrument, and it is built exactly for systematic cultural assessment. Exam trap: the stem says DSM-5-TR \"introduced\" the tool, but the CFI first appeared in DSM-5 (2013), and the Outline for Cultural Formulation goes back to DSM-IV (1994). The \"Global Functioning Scale\" is a near-miss for the GAF, Axis V of DSM-IV, which DSM-5 dropped.", "topic": "Cultural Formulation Interview"}, {"q": "The 'Crisis in psychology due to strict adherence to experimental-analytical paradigm' primarily refers to the limitations of which epistemological stance?", "options": {"a": "Social Constructionism", "b": "Positivism", "c": "Existential Phenomenology", "d": "Co-operative Enquiry"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the \"crisis\" was a critique of positivism, the stance that psychology should copy the natural sciences and accept only what can be observed, measured and tested. Positivism comes from Auguste Comte (1830s), who held that real knowledge is \"positive\", that is, built on observation and experiment, and from the logical positivists of the Vienna Circle (1920s), for whom a statement has meaning only if observation could verify it. In psychology this became the experimental-analytical paradigm: break behaviour into variables, manipulate one, measure another, control the rest, quantify everything and look for universal cause and effect laws, with a detached, value-free researcher. From the late 1960s many writers spoke of a crisis, above all in social psychology. Rom Harré and Paul Secord (1972) argued that people are agents who act for reasons and meanings, not objects pushed about by variables. Kenneth Gergen (1973) argued that social psychology is really history, because its \"laws\" shift as culture shifts. Critics added that laboratory experiments were artificial, stripped away context and power, and treated participants as passive subjects. Example: Riya studies loneliness in widows only with a rating scale and a reaction time task in the lab. She gets clean, comparable numbers but learns nothing of what being alone means to each woman, which open interviews would have shown. Option b is right because the experimental-analytical paradigm is positivism in practice, and the crisis literature attacked exactly its assumptions of objectivity, measurability and universal laws. Exam trap: social constructionism, existential phenomenology and co-operative inquiry are not the targets of the crisis; they are alternatives that grew out of it. Do not pick an option just because it sounds like a critical school.", "topic": "Positivism and the crisis"}, {"q": "In advanced statistical analysis, 'MANOVA' (Multivariate Analysis of Variance) is preferred over multiple ANOVAs when:", "options": {"a": "There is only one dependent variable", "b": "There are multiple independent variables and one dependent variable", "c": "There are multiple dependent variables that are correlated", "d": "The sample size is very small"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: MANOVA is preferred when there are two or more dependent variables (DVs) that are correlated, so it makes sense to analyse them together. Multivariate analysis of variance extends ANOVA. Instead of comparing group means on one DV, it builds a weighted combination of the DVs and asks whether the groups differ on that combination. Running a separate ANOVA for each DV has two problems. First, each test carries its own 5% risk, so the familywise Type I error grows: with three DVs tested at .05, the chance of at least one false positive is roughly 1 minus 0.95 cubed, about 14%. Second, separate ANOVAs ignore how the DVs relate, while MANOVA can detect a group difference that lies in the pattern of DVs even when no single DV differs much. The usual test statistics are Wilks' lambda (Samuel Wilks, 1932), Pillai's trace (the most robust), Hotelling's trace and Roy's largest root. If the overall test is significant, follow-up ANOVAs or a discriminant analysis show where the difference lies. Example: Kabir compares CBT, yoga and a waiting list on anxiety, depression and stress scores. These three scores correlate, so one MANOVA is better than three ANOVAs. Option c is right because several correlated DVs are exactly the situation MANOVA was built for. Exam trap: option b describes a factorial ANOVA. \"Multivariate\" refers to several dependent variables, not several independent variables. Also, MANOVA works best when DVs are moderately correlated; if two DVs correlate almost perfectly, one is redundant and the analysis becomes unstable.", "topic": "MANOVA versus multiple ANOVAs"}, {"q": "The concept of 'Effect Size' in research is crucial because:", "options": {"a": "It determines the statistical significance of a finding", "b": "It is a prerequisite for conducting a t-test", "c": "It helps in controlling for Type I errors", "d": "It indicates the practical importance or magnitude of an observed effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: effect size tells you how large, and so how practically important, an observed effect is, which a p-value cannot tell you. A significance test asks only whether an effect is probably not zero. Its p-value depends on two things at once: the size of the effect and the size of the sample. With a huge sample, even a trivial difference becomes \"significant\"; with a tiny sample, a large and useful difference can miss p < .05. Effect size separates the magnitude from the sample size. Jacob Cohen (1988) set the familiar benchmarks: for Cohen's d (the difference between two means divided by the pooled standard deviation) 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large; for the correlation r, .10, .30 and .50; for eta squared in ANOVA, .01, .06 and .14. Other effect sizes include the odds ratio and the number needed to treat. The American Psychological Association now expects effect sizes, ideally with confidence intervals, to be reported alongside p-values, and meta-analysis is built entirely on them. Example: Arjun tests a study app on 4,000 students. The app group scores half a mark higher out of 100, p < .001. The effect is real but d is tiny, so no school should spend money on it. Meera's pilot of 20 students finds a large d of 0.8 with p = .08; it deserves a bigger trial, not the bin. Option d is right because effect size is defined as a standardised index of the magnitude of an effect, the basis for judging practical or clinical importance. Exam trap: effect size does not decide significance (that is the p-value against alpha). It is used before a study, in power analysis, to plan sample size, but it is not a prerequisite for running a t-test.", "topic": "Effect size and practical importance"}, {"q": "The 'Humanistic' concept of 'Unconditional Positive Regard' in therapy assumes that:", "options": {"a": "The therapist must approve of all the client's behaviors", "b": "The therapist's complete acceptance and non-judgmental attitude towards the client is essential for therapeutic growth", "c": "The client must earn the therapist's respect", "d": "Therapy should focus on confronting the client's negative traits"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: unconditional positive regard means the therapist accepts and values the client as a person, without judgement and without conditions, and this acceptance is held to be essential for growth. Carl Rogers (1957) named three therapist core conditions as necessary and sufficient for change: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm, non-possessive acceptance or \"prizing\") and accurate empathic understanding. Rogers credited the term itself to his student Stanley Standal (1954). The logic comes from Rogers's theory of the self. Children who receive only conditional regard (\"I love you when you are good\") absorb conditions of worth. They then deny or distort experiences that clash with those conditions, and a gap opens between the self-concept and actual experience, which Rogers called incongruence. In therapy, regard that does not depend on what the client says or does lets the client stop defending, admit denied feelings into awareness, and let the actualising tendency resume. Accepting the person is not approving every act. The therapist can privately disagree with a behaviour while still valuing the human being who did it. Example: Kabir, 19, admits in his third session that he has been stealing money from his mother's purse. The counsellor neither praises nor condemns him. She conveys, \"I still value you and want to understand what is happening for you.\" Feeling safe, Kabir starts to talk about the shame and anger he has been hiding. Option b is right because it captures both parts of the concept: complete, non-judgemental acceptance and its role in therapeutic growth. Exam trap: \"unconditional\" is about the person's worth, not about agreeing with behaviour, so option a is the classic over-reading. Option c is the conditional regard that, in Rogers's theory, causes the problem.", "topic": "Unconditional positive regard"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The diathesis-stress model explains the onset of schizophrenia as an interaction between genetic predisposition and environmental triggers. Reason (R): Because schizophrenia develops only in individuals who have no genetic vulnerability but are exposed to extreme stressors.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because the diathesis-stress model needs a genetic (or other biological) vulnerability to be present, not absent. Step 1, the Assertion: true. The diathesis-stress model, set out for schizophrenia by Paul Meehl (1962) and developed as the vulnerability-stress model by Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977), says that illness appears when a pre-existing vulnerability (the diathesis) meets enough stress. Neither is usually sufficient on its own. The diathesis can be genes, obstetric complications or early brain development; the stress can be cannabis use, migration, childhood adversity, a hostile family climate or major life events. The evidence fits: the lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population but nearly 50% for the identical co-twin of a patient, as Irving Gottesman (1991) summarised. That high figure shows genes matter; the fact that it is not 100% shows that environment matters too. Step 2, the Reason: false. It says schizophrenia develops only in people who have no genetic vulnerability. That reverses the model. Without a diathesis, even extreme stress usually does not produce schizophrenia. Step 3: since R is false, the question of whether R explains A does not arise. Example: Arjun's mother has schizophrenia, so he carries raised genetic risk. He stays well through school, but after heavy cannabis use and a stressful move to a hostel, he develops psychosis at 20. His roommate Kabir, with no family history, goes through the same stresses without becoming psychotic. Option c is right because A describes the model correctly and R contradicts it. Exam trap: in Assertion-Reason items, examiners often plant an extreme word such as \"only\", \"never\" or \"always\" in R. Here \"only\" plus \"no genetic vulnerability\" turns an interaction model into a pure stress model.", "topic": "Diathesis-stress model, schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Which of the following best explains the role of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) in the regulation of sleep?", "options": {"a": "It promotes REM sleep by directly activating pontine cholinergic neurons.", "b": "It inhibits arousal-promoting regions (e.g., locus coeruleus, tuberomammillary nucleus) through GABAergic projections.", "c": "It generates circadian rhythms by acting as the master pacemaker.", "d": "It integrates light input from the retina to synchronize the sleep-wake cycle."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) switches on sleep by sending inhibitory GABA (and galanin) projections that silence the brain's arousal centres. Wakefulness is kept up by several ascending arousal systems: noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus, serotonin from the raphe nuclei, acetylcholine from the pons and basal forebrain, and orexin (hypocretin) from the lateral hypothalamus. The VLPO, a cluster of sleep-active neurons in the anterior hypothalamus, fires mainly during sleep and inhibits these systems. The arousal systems in turn inhibit the VLPO. Clifford Saper, Thomas Chou and Thomas Scammell (2001) called this mutual inhibition a \"flip-flop switch\": the brain is pushed firmly into either waking or sleep, with few in-between states, and orexin acts like a finger that holds the switch in the wake position. Adenosine, which builds up the longer we stay awake, is one signal that excites the VLPO and drives sleep pressure. The idea of an anterior hypothalamic sleep centre goes back to Constantin von Economo (1930), who saw that patients with encephalitis lethargica and damage to the anterior hypothalamus had severe insomnia. Example: Riya takes an old cough syrup containing an antihistamine and soon feels heavy and sleepy. The drug blocks histamine, the very arousal signal the VLPO turns down each night. Option b is right because the VLPO's defining job is GABAergic inhibition of the arousal-promoting regions. Exam trap: the VLPO is not the clock. The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is the master circadian pacemaker that receives light from the retina; the VLPO is the switch that the clock and sleep pressure act on. REM itself is generated in the pons.", "topic": "VLPO and the sleep switch"}, {"q": "The somatic marker hypothesis (proposed by Antonio Damasio) suggests that decision-making under emotional conditions depends primarily on:", "options": {"a": "The amygdala's role in fear conditioning.", "b": "Hypothalamic regulation of autonomic responses.", "c": "The insula's interoceptive awareness of visceral signals.", "d": "The ventromedial prefrontal cortex linking bodily states with cognitive evaluation."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: Antonio Damasio's (1994) somatic marker hypothesis gives the central role to the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), which links bodily emotional states to the options being weighed. When we face a choice, past outcomes have left traces in the body: a knot in the stomach, a quickened heart, a sense of ease. Damasio called these somatic markers. The vmPFC (including the orbitofrontal cortex) learns which bodily state went with which option and reactivates it when that option comes up again, as a \"gut feeling\". This narrows the field before slow, deliberate reasoning begins, so decisions under uncertainty become faster and wiser. The evidence came from patients. Paul Eslinger and Antonio Damasio (1985) described patient E.V.R., who kept a superior IQ after removal of an orbitofrontal tumour yet made ruinous personal and financial decisions. Antoine Bechara and colleagues (1994) designed the Iowa Gambling Task: players draw from four decks, two giving big wins but bigger losses, two giving small wins and smaller losses. Healthy players drift to the safe decks and show anticipatory skin conductance responses before picking a risky one. Patients with vmPFC damage keep choosing the risky decks and show no such warning response. Phineas Gage (1848) is the historical forerunner. Example: Arjun, after a road accident damaging his vmPFC, scores normally on intelligence tests and can explain the risks of a loan, yet keeps signing guarantees for unreliable friends. His knowledge is intact; the bodily signal that should stop him is missing. Option d is right because the hypothesis is, at its core, a theory of vmPFC function in linking body states with cognitive evaluation. Exam trap: the amygdala, insula and somatosensory cortex are part of the network (they trigger and map body states), so they tempt, but the integrating hub of the hypothesis is the vmPFC.", "topic": "Somatic marker hypothesis"}, {"q": "A newly developed psychological test shows high internal consistency (e.g., high Cronbach's Alpha) but consistently fails to predict real-world behavior it is designed to measure. This suggests a problem with:", "options": {"a": "Reliability", "b": "Norms", "c": "Validity", "d": "Standardization"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a test that is internally consistent but does not predict the behaviour it is meant to measure has a validity problem. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same, stable result? Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is one form of reliability, internal consistency, which shows how strongly the items hang together, as if they measure one common thing. Validity is accuracy of meaning: does the test measure what it claims, and do its scores support the decisions we make with them? Predicting a real-world criterion, now or in the future, is criterion-related validity (concurrent or predictive), one strand of the unified idea of construct validity set out by Samuel Messick (1989). The two are linked in one direction only. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. An unreliable test cannot be valid, because its scores are mostly noise. But a highly reliable test can still measure the wrong thing very consistently. A bathroom scale that reads 2 kg heavy every single time is reliable but not valid. Example: Riya builds a 30-item \"sales aptitude\" scale. Alpha is .92, so the items clearly agree with each other. Yet scores do not correlate with the actual sales figures of new recruits after six months. The items may be tapping general confidence or social desirability rather than selling skill. Her test needs better construct and predictive validity, not more reliability. Option c is right because failure to predict the criterion is, by definition, a failure of criterion-related validity, whatever the alpha. Exam trap: a high alpha tempts students to say the test is \"good\". Alpha tells you only that items are consistent with each other; a very high alpha can even signal redundant, near-identical items that cover the construct too narrowly.", "topic": "Reliability without validity"}, {"q": "Damage to the 'Broca's Area' of the brain is typically associated with difficulties in:", "options": {"a": "Language comprehension", "b": "Auditory processing", "c": "Visual processing", "d": "Language production"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: damage to Broca's area typically produces difficulty with language production, that is, slow, effortful, non-fluent speech. Broca's area lies in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the motor cortex region that controls the face, tongue and larynx. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Victor Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because that was almost the only syllable he could produce, although he understood much of what was said to him. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe, the first strong evidence that language is localised and lateralised. Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia shows halting, telegraphic speech with content words but few grammatical words or endings (agrammatism), poor repetition, word-finding pauses, and often a right-sided weakness because the nearby motor cortex is also damaged. Comprehension of everyday conversation is relatively preserved, and patients know their speech is wrong, so they are often frustrated. Contrast Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874) in the left posterior superior temporal gyrus: damage there gives fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension. Example: Kabir, 62, has a left frontal stroke. Asked about his weekend he says, \"Sunday... walk... dog... park,\" and shakes his head in frustration. When his daughter asks him to point to the window, he does so at once. Option d is right because the defining deficit after Broca's area damage is in producing speech, not in hearing or seeing. Exam trap: Broca's patients are not perfectly normal in comprehension; they struggle with grammatically complex sentences such as \"The dog was chased by the cat.\" Even so, the textbook hallmark of Broca's aphasia is production, while comprehension is the hallmark of Wernicke's.", "topic": "Broca's area and aphasia"}, {"q": "The neurophysiological method which allows for the direct measurement of single neuron activity in living animals:", "options": {"a": "EEG", "b": "fMRI", "c": "Microelectrode studies", "d": "PET scan"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: microelectrode studies, also called single-unit recording, directly measure the electrical activity of individual neurons in living animals. A microelectrode is a very fine wire or glass pipette with a tip of a few micrometres. Lowered into the brain of an anaesthetised or freely moving animal, an extracellular microelectrode picks up the action potentials (\"spikes\") of one or a few neurons near its tip. An intracellular microelectrode, inserted into a single cell, records its membrane potential, including small postsynaptic potentials. Because the recording is taken from the neuron itself, with millisecond timing, this is the most direct way to link the firing of a single cell to a stimulus or a behaviour. Classic discoveries came this way. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959) recorded single cells in the cat's visual cortex and found neurons tuned to lines of a particular orientation, work that won a Nobel Prize in 1981. John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one part of its environment. Example: Meera, a neuroscience student, implants a microelectrode in a rat's hippocampus. Each time the rat runs into one corner of the box, the speaker connected to the amplifier crackles with spikes; elsewhere it is quiet. She has found a single place cell. Option c is right because only microelectrode recording measures single-neuron activity directly; the other three methods measure the summed or indirect activity of millions of cells. Exam trap: EEG also records electrical activity, so it tempts, but scalp electrodes pick up the summed postsynaptic potentials of large populations of cortical neurons, never one cell. fMRI and PET are even more indirect, tracking blood oxygen, blood flow or metabolism.", "topic": "Single-unit microelectrode recording"}, {"q": "In 'Instrumental Learning', 'Partial Reinforcement Schedules' lead to behavior that is:", "options": {"a": "Acquired more quickly but extinguished more rapidly", "b": "Acquired more slowly but extinguished more slowly", "c": "Acquired and extinguished at the same rate as continuous reinforcement", "d": "Not acquired at all"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: behaviour learnt under partial (intermittent) reinforcement is acquired more slowly than under continuous reinforcement but is far more resistant to extinction. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. On continuous reinforcement (CRF) every correct response is rewarded, so the link between response and reward is easy to detect and learning is fastest. On a partial schedule only some responses are rewarded, by number of responses (ratio) or by time (interval), fixed or variable, the four schedules that Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped. Learning is slower because the link is less consistent. But once reward stops, partially reinforced behaviour keeps going much longer. This is the partial reinforcement extinction effect (PREE), first shown by Lloyd Humphreys (1939) with conditioned eyeblinks. Variable ratio schedules produce the most persistent responding of all. Why does it happen? One account is discrimination: after CRF, the first unrewarded response is a sharp change, so the person notices quickly that the rules have changed; after partial reinforcement, a run of non-rewards looks like more of the same. Abram Amsel's (1958) frustration theory adds that partially reinforced animals learn to keep responding while frustrated. Example: when a vending machine fails once, Kabir stops feeding it coins, because it has always worked before (continuous reinforcement). At a slot machine, which pays off unpredictably, he keeps playing through long losing runs (variable ratio). Option b is right because it gives both halves of the effect: slower acquisition and slower extinction. Exam trap: option a is the exact reverse and describes continuous reinforcement. Trainers therefore use continuous reinforcement to establish a new behaviour quickly, then thin it to a partial schedule so the behaviour lasts.", "topic": "Partial reinforcement extinction effect"}, {"q": "The 'Flynn Effect' refers to the phenomenon of:", "options": {"a": "A decline in average IQ scores over generations", "b": "The impact of genetics on intelligence", "c": "The stability of IQ scores throughout an individual's lifespan", "d": "A gradual increase in average IQ scores over generations"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Flynn effect is the steady rise in average IQ test scores from one generation to the next across the twentieth century. James Flynn (1984) compared how people of different eras scored on the same tests and showed that American scores rose by about 3 IQ points per decade; he later (1987) found similar gains in many countries. The gains were largest on fluid reasoning tests such as Raven's Progressive Matrices and smaller on vocabulary and general knowledge. Richard Herrnstein and Charles Murray (1994) gave the effect its name in The Bell Curve. Because the rise is far too fast for genetic change, it must be environmental. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and longer schooling, smaller families, a more visually and cognitively complex world, and growing familiarity with abstract, test-like thinking (Flynn's \"scientific spectacles\"). Since the 1990s some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, have shown a slowing or a reversal. The practical result is that norms go stale. If a test was normed 20 years ago, today's children will score about 6 points too high against it, so tests such as the Wechsler scales must be restandardised regularly. This matters most near cut-offs, for example in diagnosing intellectual disability. Example: Meera's grandmother took a reasoning test in 1960 and scored 100 against the norms of her day. If Meera, of the same ability relative to her own generation, took that test today and was scored against the 1960 norms, she would score well above 100. Option d is right because the Flynn effect is defined as a gradual increase in average IQ scores over generations. Exam trap: option a describes the recent \"reverse\" or negative Flynn effect seen in a few countries, the opposite of the classic effect.", "topic": "The Flynn effect"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In Major Depressive Disorder, neuroimaging studies often show hypoactivity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Reason (R): Because the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is primarily involved in regulating affective salience of threat-related stimuli.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) is an executive control region, not the region that tags threat as emotionally salient. Step 1, the Assertion: true. Many imaging studies of major depressive disorder find reduced activity and metabolism in the DLPFC, especially on the left. Helen Mayberg (1997) built this into her limbic-cortical dysregulation model: dorsal cortical regions such as the DLPFC are underactive, while ventral limbic regions such as the subgenual anterior cingulate and the amygdala are overactive. Low DLPFC activity fits the poor concentration, slowed thinking and indecision of depression. It is also why repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) for depression is given at high frequency over the left DLPFC, a treatment approved in the United States in 2008. Step 2, the Reason: false. The DLPFC's main jobs are working memory, planning, set shifting and top-down control, including deliberately reappraising an emotion. Detecting threat and assigning it emotional salience is chiefly the work of the amygdala, together with the ventromedial prefrontal cortex and the anterior cingulate cortex. In depression the amygdala tends to over-respond to sad or threatening faces. Step 3: because R is false, it cannot explain A. Example: Riya, 34, has been depressed for a year. She cannot hold a phone number in mind or plan her week, signs of low DLPFC function, so her psychiatrist offers rTMS over the left DLPFC. Her habit of noticing every critical look, by contrast, reflects an overactive amygdala. Option c is right because A is a well-replicated finding while R gives the DLPFC a function that belongs mainly to other structures. Exam trap: the DLPFC does regulate emotion indirectly, through reappraisal, so R sounds half right; but \"primarily\" and \"affective salience of threat\" point to the amygdala.", "topic": "DLPFC hypoactivity in depression"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) has been linked with abnormalities in cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuitry. Reason (R): Because this circuit is responsible for error detection, habit formation, and inhibitory control.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the Reason explains the Assertion, because the functions of the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) circuit are exactly the functions that go wrong in OCD. Step 1, the Assertion: true. The CSTC loop runs from the orbitofrontal cortex and anterior cingulate cortex to the striatum (especially the caudate nucleus), then through the globus pallidus to the thalamus and back to the cortex. Lewis Baxter and colleagues (1987, 1992) used PET to show raised glucose metabolism in the orbitofrontal cortex and caudate in OCD, and later found that caudate activity fell in patients who improved with either fluoxetine or behaviour therapy. Neurosurgery and deep brain stimulation for severe, treatment-resistant OCD target this same circuit. Step 2, the Reason: true. The orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex signal that something is wrong (error and conflict detection); the striatum builds habits and normally filters out unimportant signals; and the loop as a whole supports stopping an action once it is no longer needed (inhibitory control). Step 3: R explains A. In OCD the error signal fires even when nothing is wrong, giving an intrusive \"not right\" feeling (the obsession). The caudate fails to filter it, the thalamus loops it back, and a repeated action hardens into a habit that the person cannot inhibit (the compulsion). William Gehring and colleagues (2000) found an enlarged error-related brain potential in OCD, and Claire Gillan and colleagues (2011) showed over-reliance on habits. Example: Arjun locks the door, then feels a nagging sense of error. He checks, feels brief relief, and checks again, ten times; each check strengthens the habit. Option a is right because R names the very functions whose breakdown produces obsessions and compulsions. Exam trap: choose b only when R is true but irrelevant to A. Here R is the mechanism itself, not a side fact.", "topic": "CSTC circuit in OCD"}, {"q": "Which following finding would most strongly support a genetic explanation for schizophrenia based on adoption studies?", "options": {"a": "Higher rates of schizophrenia in adopted children whose biological parents had schizophrenia, compared to those whose adoptive parents had schizophrenia.", "b": "Equal rates of schizophrenia in adopted children whose biological vs. adoptive parents had schizophrenia.", "c": "Higher rates of schizophrenia in adopted children if raised in stressful adoptive environments, regardless of biological parent diagnosis.", "d": "No significant difference in schizophrenia rates between adopted children of affected vs. non-affected biological parents."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the strongest genetic evidence from adoption studies is that schizophrenia follows the biological parents, who passed on the genes, rather than the adoptive parents, who provided the upbringing. Family members share both genes and environment, so a disorder that runs in families could be inherited or learnt. An adoption study pulls the two apart. A child adopted away at birth gets genes from the biological parents and rearing from the adoptive parents. If the child's risk tracks the biological parent's diagnosis, genes are implicated; if it tracks the adoptive home, environment is. Leonard Heston (1966) followed children of mothers with schizophrenia who had been adopted away soon after birth. About 1 in 10 developed schizophrenia, while none of the control adoptees, whose biological mothers were well, did. The Danish adoption studies of Seymour Kety, David Rosenthal and Paul Wender (from 1968) found schizophrenia spectrum disorders concentrated in the biological, not the adoptive, relatives of adoptees with schizophrenia. Cross-fostering data added that children of healthy biological parents raised by a parent with schizophrenia did not show a raised risk. Later, Pekka Tienari and colleagues (2004) in Finland found that high-risk adoptees became ill most often when raised in disturbed adoptive families, a gene-environment interaction. Example: Meera was born to a mother with schizophrenia and adopted as a newborn by a healthy family. Kabir was born to healthy parents and raised by an adoptive father who developed schizophrenia. If children like Meera show higher rates than children like Kabir, the risk travelled with the genes. Option a is right because it is the exact comparison that separates genetic from rearing influence and finds the genetic side stronger. Exam trap: option c sounds like real evidence, and stressful rearing does matter, but \"regardless of biological parent diagnosis\" makes it a purely environmental finding.", "topic": "Adoption studies of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Fine motor control during skilled behaviors (e.g., writing or playing a musical instrument) primarily depends on:", "options": {"a": "Corticospinal tract projections from the primary motor cortex to alpha motor neurons.", "b": "Reticulospinal tract activation of proximal limb muscles.", "c": "Extrapyramidal system control via the basal ganglia without cortical involvement.", "d": "Autonomic innervation of intrafusal muscle fibers regulating tone."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: skilled fine movements of the fingers depend on the corticospinal tract, which carries commands from the primary motor cortex down to the alpha motor neurons that drive the muscles. The primary motor cortex lies in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) and is mapped as a motor homunculus in which the hand and fingers take up a very large area. Its axons, with those from premotor areas, form the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract. About 85 to 90% of the fibres cross at the pyramids of the medulla and descend as the lateral (dorsolateral) corticospinal tract to the ventral horn of the spinal cord. There they synapse on alpha motor neurons, in primates often directly, and these contract the muscle fibres. Direct cortex-to-motor-neuron connections are densest for the hand and allow fingers to move independently. Donald Lawrence and Hans Kuypers (1968) cut the pyramidal tracts in monkeys. The animals could still walk, climb and grasp with the whole hand, but they permanently lost the ability to move single fingers, for example to pick food out of a small hole. Other systems have other jobs. The ventromedial pathways (reticulospinal, vestibulospinal, tectospinal) control posture and the trunk and proximal limb muscles. The basal ganglia and cerebellum shape movement, mainly through loops back to the motor cortex. Example: Riya, a veena player, has a small stroke in the internal capsule, where corticospinal fibres are tightly packed. She can still lift her arm, but her fingers cannot pick out separate notes and her handwriting turns clumsy. Option a is right because the corticospinal route from primary motor cortex to alpha motor neurons is the pathway for fractionated, skilled hand movement. Exam trap: \"extrapyramidal\" tempts because the basal ganglia do matter in skilled movement, but they work through the cortex, never \"without cortical involvement\".", "topic": "Corticospinal tract and fine movement"}, {"q": "Which of the following best illustrates the permissive effect of hormones on behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Cortisol directly initiating the fight-or-flight response during acute stress.", "b": "Thyroid hormones enabling the normal functioning of catecholamines in arousal regulation.", "c": "Oxytocin triggering uterine contractions during childbirth.", "d": "Testosterone immediately producing aggressive behavior in experimental animals."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: thyroid hormones enabling catecholamines to work normally is the textbook permissive effect, in which one hormone does not cause a response itself but must be present for another hormone to act fully. Hormones can interact in three main ways. In a synergistic effect, two hormones together produce more than the sum of each alone. In an antagonistic effect, one opposes the other, as insulin lowers blood glucose and glucagon raises it. In a permissive effect, hormone A has little visible effect on its own but prepares the target tissue so that hormone B can act. Thyroid hormones (thyroxine and triiodothyronine) increase the number of beta-adrenergic receptors on heart, muscle and fat cells. With enough receptors, adrenaline and noradrenaline produce their normal rise in heart rate, alertness and energy release. With too little thyroid hormone, the same catecholamines have a weak effect; with too much, tissues become over-responsive. Example: Meera develops hyperthyroidism. She feels jittery and anxious, with a racing heart and tremor, as if constantly over-aroused. Her catecholamine levels are not especially high; her tissues are simply over-sensitive to them. That is why her doctor gives propranolol, a beta-blocker, for quick relief while the thyroid itself is treated. Option b is right because the thyroid hormones here do not create arousal themselves; they enable catecholamines to regulate it. Exam trap: the other three options each describe a hormone directly producing a response, the opposite of permissiveness. Cortisol is in fact permissive for some actions of catecholamines, so its name may tempt, but option a says it directly initiates fight-or-flight. That response is launched by the sympathetic nervous system and adrenaline, as Walter Cannon (1915) described, while cortisol rises more slowly through the HPA axis.", "topic": "Permissive effect of hormones"}, {"q": "'Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)' incorporates elements from:", "options": {"a": "Sufism", "b": "Integral Yoga", "c": "Buddhism", "d": "Bhagavad Gita"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) draws mainly on Buddhist meditation, especially the insight (vipassana) and Zen traditions, taught in a secular, medical form. Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979) founded MBSR at the Stress Reduction Clinic of the University of Massachusetts Medical Center, for patients with chronic pain and stress-related illness whom usual care had not helped. He had trained in Zen and insight meditation and deliberately removed religious language so the practices could be taught in a hospital. The programme runs for eight weeks: a weekly group class of about two and a half hours, one full day of silent practice, and around 45 minutes of daily home practice. Its core exercises are the body scan, sitting meditation on the breath, gentle mindful hatha yoga and mindful everyday activities. Kabat-Zinn (1994) defined mindfulness as paying attention in a particular way: on purpose, in the present moment and non-judgementally. The idea is that automatic reactions and rumination magnify stress; watching sensations and thoughts without fighting them breaks the cycle. MBSR later inspired mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002), which reduces relapse in recurrent depression. Example: Arjun, an IT worker in Bengaluru with chronic back pain, joins an MBSR group. During the body scan he learns to notice the ache as shifting sensations rather than a catastrophe, and his distress falls even though the pain does not vanish. Option c is right because MBSR's mindfulness practices come straight from Buddhist meditation, even though they are taught without religious framing. Exam trap: MBSR includes yoga postures, so \"Integral Yoga\" tempts; but the yoga element is generic mindful hatha yoga, and the defining source of the programme is Buddhist mindfulness (sati). MBSR borrows Buddhist practices, not Buddhist beliefs.", "topic": "Roots of MBSR"}, {"q": "'Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)' is a third-wave behavioral therapy that emphasizes:", "options": {"a": "Directly challenging irrational thoughts", "b": "Changing problematic behaviors through classical conditioning", "c": "Psychological flexibility and values-driven action", "d": "Exploring early childhood experiences"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims at psychological flexibility, the ability to stay in contact with the present moment, unwanted thoughts and feelings included, while acting in line with one's chosen values. ACT (said as the word \"act\") was set out by Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999), and Steven Hayes (2004) placed it in the \"third wave\" of behaviour therapy, after classical behaviour therapy and cognitive therapy. Its basic science is relational frame theory, an account of how language lets us link events to each other, which explains why a thought such as \"I am a failure\" can feel as real as an event. ACT holds that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance (trying to escape or control unwanted inner experiences) and cognitive fusion (treating thoughts as literal truths). Six core processes, often drawn as a \"hexaflex\", build flexibility: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. ACT does not try to change the content of thoughts; it changes the person's relationship to them. Example: Meera, a junior lawyer, avoids speaking in meetings because she thinks, \"I will sound stupid.\" Her ACT therapist does not dispute the thought. Meera practises saying, \"I notice I am having the thought that I will sound stupid,\" clarifies that she values becoming a strong advocate, and commits to speaking once in every meeting, anxiety and all. Option c is right because psychological flexibility and values-driven action are the stated goals of ACT. Exam trap: option a describes Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy and Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy, the second wave. ACT deliberately does not challenge irrational thoughts; it defuses from them and makes room for them.", "topic": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy"}, {"q": "'Psychoneuroimmunology' research has shown a strong link between chronic stress and:", "options": {"a": "Enhanced immune function", "b": "Suppression of the immune system", "c": "Increased neurogenesis", "d": "Improved cognitive function"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoneuroimmunology research shows that chronic stress suppresses the immune system, leaving people more prone to infection and slower to heal. Psychoneuroimmunology studies how the mind, nervous system, endocrine system and immune system influence each other. It began with Robert Ader and Nicholas Cohen (1975), who gave rats saccharin-flavoured water together with cyclophosphamide, a drug that suppresses immunity. Later, saccharin alone suppressed the rats' immune response: the immune system could be classically conditioned, so the brain must be talking to it. The main stress route runs through the HPA axis and the sympathetic nervous system. Prolonged stress keeps cortisol and catecholamines high, and these reduce the number and activity of lymphocytes and natural killer cells. Human studies show the effect. Janice Kiecolt-Glaser and colleagues (1984) found that medical students' immune measures fell during examinations, and later (1995) that caregivers of relatives with dementia took about nine days longer than controls to heal a small standard skin wound. Sheldon Cohen and colleagues (1991) gave volunteers cold viruses in nasal drops and found that the more stressed people were, the more likely they were to develop a cold. Suzanne Segerstrom and Gregory Miller (2004), in a meta-analysis of about 300 studies, concluded that brief stressors can briefly boost some first-line defences, but chronic stressors suppress immunity broadly. Example: Kabir works night shifts while caring for his father after a stroke. Over the year he catches every passing cold, and a cut on his hand takes weeks to close. Option b is right because immune suppression under chronic stress is the central, repeatedly replicated finding of the field. Exam trap: option a is a half-truth that holds only for short, acute stress. The stem says chronic, and chronic stress suppresses immunity.", "topic": "Psychoneuroimmunology and chronic stress"}, {"q": "The effectiveness of 'Parental mediation of Digital Usage' in mitigating negative impacts on children implicitly assumes that:", "options": {"a": "Children are incapable of self-regulation regarding digital media", "b": "All digital content is equally appropriate for all ages.", "c": "Digital media is inherently harmful to children", "d": "Parental involvement can significantly influence a child's digital habits and outcomes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: parental mediation can only work if parents can actually shape how children use digital media and what effects that use has, so this is the assumption built into the idea. Parental mediation means the ways parents manage and interpret media for their children. It began in television research. Patti Valkenburg and colleagues (1999) described three styles: restrictive mediation (rules about time and content), instructive or active mediation (talking with the child about what they see, for example explaining adverts or violence) and social co-viewing (watching together without much comment). Sonia Livingstone and Ellen Helsper (2008) extended the work to the internet, adding technical restrictions (filters, parental controls) and monitoring (checking history or apps). The implicit premise is that the family is a powerful microsystem, in the terms of Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), through which outside influences reach the child, so what parents do can increase benefits and reduce risks. Research broadly supports this, with nuance. Active mediation is linked with better critical understanding of content and fewer negative effects such as aggression after violent content. Restrictive mediation reduces exposure and some online risks, but it also cuts online opportunities, and with adolescents heavy restriction can backfire. This mirrors the finding of Diana Baumrind (1966) that warm, explanatory, authoritative parenting tends to work better than control alone. Example: Arjun's mother lets eight-year-old Riya use a tablet in the living room, with a one-hour limit. Sometimes she watches with her and asks, \"Why do you think that advert says the toy will make you popular?\" Riya starts to spot persuasion on her own. Option d is right because, if parents had no real influence, none of these strategies could reduce harm. Exam trap: the wrong options are extreme claims. Mediation theory assumes media bring both risks and opportunities, and it aims to build children's own self-regulation, not to deny that they have any.", "topic": "Parental mediation of digital media"}, {"q": "'Grounded Theory' as a qualitative research method emphasizes:", "options": {"a": "Testing pre-existing hypotheses", "b": "Developing theory from systematically gathered and analyzed data", "c": "Quantifying social phenomena", "d": "Conducting large-scale surveys"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: grounded theory develops a theory from data that are systematically gathered and analysed, rather than testing a theory chosen in advance. Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967) set out the method in The Discovery of Grounded Theory, after their study of how hospital staff and dying patients managed awareness of dying. They argued that sociology had become too focused on testing a few grand theories, and that new theory should be \"grounded\" in what people actually do and say. The method is inductive and cyclical: data collection and analysis run together. The researcher codes data line by line (open coding), links codes into categories and relationships (axial coding, in the version of Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin, 1990), and then organises everything around a core category (selective coding). Constant comparison checks each new incident against earlier ones. Theoretical sampling chooses the next participants to test and develop the emerging categories, and sampling stops at theoretical saturation, when new data add nothing new. Memos record ideas as the theory forms. Versions differ: Glaser stressed letting theory emerge, Strauss and Corbin gave more structured procedures, and Kathy Charmaz (2006) developed a constructivist grounded theory in which researcher and participants co-construct the theory. Example: Meera interviews mothers caring for adult sons with schizophrenia in Kerala. Early codes include \"hiding the illness\" and \"waiting for marriage\". Comparing cases, she deliberately samples mothers from different communities and builds a theory of \"protective concealment\". Option b is right because generating theory from systematically gathered and analysed data is the defining aim of grounded theory. Exam trap: grounded theory ends in a theory, so students may link it with hypothesis testing; it moves in the opposite, inductive direction, from data to theory.", "topic": "Grounded theory"}, {"q": "The scenario which best demonstrates the unique advantage of invasive methods over noninvasive neuroimaging is using:", "options": {"a": "fMRI to detect changes in BOLD response during memory recall tasks.", "b": "PET scans to measure glucose metabolism in cortical regions during problem-solving.", "c": "Single-cell recording in the hippocampus to track firing patterns of place cells in real time.", "d": "EEG to assess event-related potentials in response to emotional stimuli."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: single-cell recording of hippocampal place cells shows what only an invasive method can, the firing of one identified neuron, spike by spike, while the animal behaves. Invasive methods put something into brain tissue: a microelectrode, a lesion or a stimulating probe. Noninvasive methods (fMRI, PET, EEG, MEG) record from outside the skull and always see the pooled activity of very many neurons. In single-unit recording a fine microelectrode sits next to one neuron and picks up each action potential it fires, so it has the best spatial resolution (one cell) and the best temporal resolution (single spikes, in milliseconds) at the same time. The classic example is John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky (1971), who recorded from the rat hippocampus and found place cells, each firing only when the rat was in one particular spot; O'Keefe shared the 2014 Nobel Prize for this work. Suppose Meera, a neuroscience student, wants to know whether a single hippocampal neuron fires only when her rat reaches the left corner of a maze. No scanner can answer that. An fMRI voxel a few millimetres across holds hundreds of thousands of neurons, and the BOLD signal lags seconds behind their firing; EEG is fast but blurred across centimetres of cortex. Only an electrode inside the hippocampus can reveal one neuron's code. Option c is right because it is the only scenario that needs, and exploits, the cellular and real-time precision that only electrodes placed in the brain can give. Exam trap: two decoys borrow half of this advantage. PET involves an injection, so students call it invasive, but the tracer measures regional metabolism averaged over minutes. EEG has millisecond timing, so \"real time\" tempts, but its electrodes stay on the scalp and it cannot isolate a single neuron.", "topic": "Invasive versus noninvasive brain methods"}, {"q": "Damage to the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) most consistently leads to:", "options": {"a": "Severe anterograde amnesia with preserved implicit learning.", "b": "Heightened aggression with reduced autonomic responses to threat.", "c": "Hyperorality and indiscriminate sexual behavior typical of Kluver-Bucy syndrome.", "d": "Impaired conflict monitoring and reduced affective experience of pain."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: damage to the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) most consistently impairs conflict monitoring and blunts the emotional, unpleasant side of pain. The ACC lies on the medial surface of the frontal lobe, curving around the front of the corpus callosum; James Papez (1937) placed the cingulate gyrus in his emotion circuit. It does two jobs that examiners like. First, it watches for conflict and errors. In the Stroop task, naming the ink colour of the word RED printed in green creates response conflict, and the ACC becomes active. The conflict monitoring theory of Matthew Botvinick and colleagues (2001) holds that the ACC detects such conflict and signals the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex to apply more control. Second, it codes how much pain hurts, not where it is. Pierre Rainville and colleagues (1997) used hypnosis to change how unpleasant hot water felt on the hand without changing its felt intensity; ACC activity tracked the unpleasantness, while somatosensory cortex did not. Patients given a cingulotomy for intractable pain often say the pain is still there but no longer bothers them. Picture Kabir after a bilateral cingulate stroke: he is slow and error-prone on the Stroop test, does not notice his own mistakes, and calls a pinprick \"sharp, but it does not upset me\". Large lesions can add apathy and even akinetic mutism. Option d is right because it names exactly these two ACC functions, detecting cognitive conflict and giving pain its affective sting. Exam trap: the cingulate belongs to the limbic system, so examiners pair it with limbic syndromes. Klüver-Bucy syndrome and loss of fear belong to the amygdala and anterior temporal lobes, and dense anterograde amnesia belongs to the hippocampus; none of these is the ACC's signature.", "topic": "Anterior cingulate cortex functions"}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered the greatest methodological risk in computer-adaptive psychological testing compared to traditional paper-pencil testing?", "options": {"a": "Ceiling and floor effects in the test design.", "b": "Reduced standardization across administrations.", "c": "Item exposure and compromised test security due to adaptive algorithms.", "d": "Increased examiner bias in scoring responses."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in computerised adaptive testing (CAT) the greatest methodological risk is item exposure, which compromises test security. A CAT rests on item response theory (IRT), associated with Georg Rasch (1960) and Frederic Lord (1980). Every item in a large bank has known parameters, such as difficulty and discrimination. The computer starts with a medium item, re-estimates the person's ability (theta) after each answer, and picks the next item that gives the most information at that level: right answers bring harder items, wrong answers easier ones. The test stops when the estimate is precise enough, often after far fewer items than a paper form needs, and every score lands on the same theta scale even though each person saw different items. The weakness comes from the same algorithm. Because the computer always seeks the most informative item, a small set of highly discriminating items is chosen again and again while much of the bank sits unused. Those overused items are seen by thousands of candidates, remembered, and passed on through coaching centres and websites. Imagine Riya sits an adaptive entrance test and recognises five items that a senior described to her last month: her score now reflects memory, not ability, and validity collapses. Test makers fight back with exposure control rules, very large and frequently refreshed banks, and item rotation, but the risk is built into the method. Option c is right because overexposure is created by the adaptive selection rule itself, whereas a fixed paper form can be sealed until the day and replaced by a fresh form at the next sitting. Exam trap: students list the familiar weaknesses of fixed tests (ceiling and floor effects, uneven standardisation, examiner bias), but CAT actually reduces all three; the risk peculiar to CAT is security, not precision.", "topic": "Computerised adaptive testing risks"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Conversion Disorder symptoms are intentionally produced for external incentives. Reason (R): Because patients with conversion disorder consciously feign symptoms to obtain tangible rewards.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, and d is the only option that rejects it. Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5-TR (2022), means real symptoms of altered movement or sensation (weakness, non-epileptic seizures, blindness, loss of speech) that do not fit any recognised neurological disease, often shown by positive signs such as Hoover's sign. The person is not pretending. Step 1, the Assertion: false. Conversion symptoms are not intentionally produced. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the old formal criterion requiring the clinician to rule out feigning, because feigning is so hard to prove, but the disorder is still understood as involuntary. Psychodynamic writers spoke of primary gain (relief from an unconscious conflict) and secondary gain (attention, escape from duties), yet neither is conscious faking. Step 2, the Reason: also false. Consciously feigning symptoms to obtain tangible rewards is malingering, which DSM-5-TR lists as a V/Z code, a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention, not a mental disorder. Intentional production with no external reward, only the wish to occupy the sick role, is factitious disorder (the Munchausen syndrome named by Richard Asher, 1951). Step 3, whether R explains A, does not arise. Compare Meera, whose legs give way the week her engagement is broken off and who is frightened that she cannot walk, with Arjun, who fakes back pain to win an insurance claim: Meera has conversion disorder, Arjun is malingering. Option d is right because it is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested. Exam trap: examiners swap these three terms; ask two questions, is it intentional, and is there an external reward? Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no \"both false\" option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question for exactly this reason, so no one was marked on it.", "topic": "Conversion disorder versus malingering"}, {"q": "When employing 'Structural Equation Modeling (SEM)' in research, a fundamental assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "All variables are directly observable", "b": "Causal relationships can be inferred from correlational data alone", "c": "The model perfectly fits the data without any error", "d": "Latent (unobserved) variables can be measured indirectly through observed indicators"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: structural equation modelling (SEM) assumes that latent, unobserved variables can be measured indirectly through observed indicators. A latent variable is a construct you cannot see directly, such as depression, intelligence or social support; you only see its indicators, such as questionnaire scores, ratings and test results. SEM joins two older tools. The measurement model is a confirmatory factor analysis: each latent variable is defined by several indicators, and each indicator gets a loading plus its own error term. The structural model is a path analysis, the method Sewall Wright (1921) devised for testing a diagram of directional links, now drawn between the latent variables. Karl Jöreskog (1973) set out the general estimation method, and his LISREL program made SEM practical. Suppose Kabir studies whether social support protects college students against depression. He measures support with three scales and depression with the Beck Depression Inventory, a clinician rating and a sleep questionnaire. SEM treats support and depression as latent factors, separates true construct variance from measurement error in each indicator, estimates the path between the factors, and then asks whether the whole model reproduces the observed covariance matrix, judged by fit indices such as the comparative fit index (CFI) and the root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA). Option d is right because the latent variable and indicator measurement model is what sets SEM apart from ordinary regression or path analysis on raw scores. Exam trap: SEM is often called \"causal modelling\", so option b tempts. The causal arrows come from theory and design, not from the data; with cross-sectional correlations several different models can fit equally well, so a good fit shows that the data are consistent with the model, not that the causes are proven.", "topic": "Structural equation modelling assumptions"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) is particularly effective for Borderline Personality Disorder. Reason (R): Because DBT balances acceptance strategies with change-oriented interventions.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the balance of acceptance and change is exactly why dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) works for borderline personality disorder (BPD). Step 1, the Assertion: true. Marsha Linehan (1993) built DBT for chronically suicidal women, most with BPD. In her first randomised trial (1991) DBT clients made fewer and less dangerous self-harm attempts, spent fewer days in hospital and dropped out less than those given treatment as usual, and a 2006 trial against expert community therapists still halved suicide attempts. Step 2, the Reason: true. The word dialectical means holding two opposites and finding a synthesis. DBT's acceptance side (validation, core mindfulness drawn from Zen practice, radical acceptance) is paired with a change side from behaviour therapy (chain analysis of each self-harm episode, skills training, problem solving). Step 3, the link: R explains A. Linehan found that standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients, who then quit, while acceptance alone left the self-harm untouched. Her biosocial theory sees BPD as emotion dysregulation arising when a biologically sensitive child meets an invalidating environment, so a treatment that first validates and then teaches regulation targets the disorder's very roots. Take Riya, 22, who cuts herself after fights with her partner. Her therapist says her pain makes sense given what happened (acceptance), then walks through the chain of events and coaches her in distress tolerance skills (change). Option a is right because the acceptance and change dialectic is the mechanism Linehan designed to keep BPD clients in treatment and to reduce self-harm. Exam trap: option b tempts students who think DBT works simply because it teaches four skills modules; the skills are the change half of the dialectic, not a separate reason.", "topic": "DBT for borderline personality"}, {"q": "The 'Semantic Differential Scale' measures:", "options": {"a": "Attitudes based on agreement/disagreement with statements", "b": "Attitudes based on ratings along bipolar adjective pairs", "c": "Personality traits through self-report", "d": "Intelligence through problem-solving tasks"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the semantic differential measures attitudes, and the meaning a concept has for a person, through ratings along bipolar adjective pairs. Charles Osgood, George Suci and Percy Tannenbaum introduced it in The Measurement of Meaning (1957). The respondent sees a concept at the top of the page, such as \"mental hospital\", and rates it on a row of seven-point scales whose ends are opposite adjectives: good to bad, strong to weak, active to passive, clean to dirty. Factor analysis of thousands of such ratings across many concepts and cultures kept finding the same three dimensions of connotative meaning, known by the initials EPA: evaluation (good to bad), potency (strong to weak) and activity (fast to slow). Evaluation carries most of the variance and is the attitude component proper, so a person's attitude score is usually the mean of the evaluative scales. The method is quick, works for any concept, and can compare concepts on the same profile. For example, Arjun, a psychology student, gives the semantic differential to two groups of college students before and after an anti-stigma talk. They rate \"person with schizophrenia\" on good to bad, safe to dangerous and strong to weak, and the talk shifts the evaluative ratings towards the positive pole. Option b is right because rating a concept between pairs of opposite adjectives is the defining format of the semantic differential. Exam trap: option a describes the Likert scale of Rensis Likert (1932), where people agree or disagree with statements; both look like rows of numbered boxes, but Likert scales rate statements while the semantic differential rates a concept between two adjectives. Remember: no statements, only adjective pairs.", "topic": "Osgood's semantic differential"}, {"q": "The 'Nature-Nurture controversy' in behavioral genetics, particularly when using twin studies, assumes that:", "options": {"a": "Identical twins raised apart share identical environments", "b": "Environmental influences are negligible in shaping behavior", "c": "Differences between fraternal twins raised together are solely due to environmental factors", "d": "Differences between identical twins raised apart are primarily due to genetic factors"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: the twin design rests on comparing genetic similarity with resemblance, and identical twins reared apart are its sharpest test. Monozygotic (MZ) twins come from one fertilised egg and share all their genes; dizygotic (DZ) twins share about 50 percent of the genes that vary, like ordinary siblings. If MZ pairs resemble each other more than DZ pairs on a trait, genes are implicated; Douglas Falconer (1960) turned this into a rough heritability estimate, twice the difference between the MZ and DZ correlations. Twins reared together, however, share a home, so their likeness could be environmental. MZ twins separated early and raised apart remove that shared home: any similarity between them cannot come from a common upbringing, so it is attributed to their identical genes. From 1979 Thomas Bouchard and colleagues ran the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart; their 1990 report in Science found that MZ twins raised in different families were about as alike in intelligence and personality as MZ twins raised together. Imagine identical twins Meera and Kavya, adopted at birth by families in Kochi and Delhi: at 30 they have similar IQs and similar levels of anxiety, which points to genes. Whatever differences remain between them must come from their different environments. Option d is right because it is the only option about the reared-apart identical twin design, which isolates genetic influence. Exam trap: twin studies never assume that environment is negligible; models split variance into genes, shared environment and non-shared environment. Key note: as printed, option d says \"differences\" between identical twins reared apart are due to genes, which is backwards (identical twins share all their genes, so their differences must be environmental); the setter clearly meant the similarities of identical twins reared apart, and d is the only option about that design, so d is used.", "topic": "Twin studies and heritability"}, {"q": "'Behavior Modification' techniques, rooted in operant conditioning, implicitly assume that:", "options": {"a": "All behavior is a result of unconscious conflicts", "b": "Behavior is primarily learned through its consequences (reinforcement and punishment)", "c": "Cognitive processes are the sole determinants of behavior", "d": "Innate drives are the primary motivators of behavior"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: behaviour modification assumes that behaviour is learned, and kept going or weakened, by its consequences. The idea goes back to the puzzle-box experiments of Edward Thorndike (1898) and his law of effect: responses followed by satisfying results are stamped in, those followed by discomfort are stamped out. B. F. Skinner (1938) recast this as operant conditioning. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, and both increase a behaviour; positive punishment adds something unpleasant and negative punishment removes something pleasant, and both decrease it; extinction, withholding the reinforcer that maintained a behaviour, makes it fade. Behaviour modification applies these principles to real problems. It starts with a functional analysis of antecedents, behaviour and consequences, then changes the consequences. The assumption underneath is environmental: maladaptive behaviour is learned in the same lawful way as adaptive behaviour, so it can be unlearned without digging for hidden causes. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) showed this on psychiatric wards with the token economy, where patients earned tokens for self-care and work and exchanged them for privileges. Take Kabir, 7, who throws tantrums at homework time. His parents stop giving in to the tantrums (extinction) and give him a star for every completed page, exchangeable for cricket time (token reinforcement); within weeks the tantrums drop. Option b is right because reinforcement and punishment, the consequences of behaviour, are the core of operant theory and of every behaviour modification technique. Exam trap: option c tempts because of \"cognitive behaviour modification\", the term Donald Meichenbaum (1977) used for self-instructional training; even that approach treats thoughts as one influence among many, never the sole determinant, and classic behaviour modification sets cognition aside.", "topic": "Assumptions of behaviour modification"}, {"q": "'Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT)' assumes that psychological distress is primarily caused by:", "options": {"a": "Early childhood trauma", "b": "Irrational beliefs and cognitive distortions", "c": "Unmet needs for self-actualization", "d": "Biological imbalances"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) holds that psychological distress comes mainly from irrational beliefs, not from events themselves. Albert Ellis (1955) began rational therapy after psychoanalysis disappointed him; it became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. Its core is the ABC model. A is the activating event, B the person's beliefs about A, and C the emotional and behavioural consequences. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis says A contributes, but B is the main cause. An irrational belief is a rigid, absolute demand that does not fit reality: musts and shoulds (demandingness), awfulising (\"this is the worst thing possible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global rating of self or others (\"I am worthless\"). A rational belief is a flexible preference. Therapy adds D, vigorous disputing of the irrational belief, which produces E, an effective new belief. Take Riya, who fails a mock entrance test (A). If she thinks \"I must always score at the top, or I am a failure\" (B), she becomes depressed and stops studying (C). Her therapist disputes the belief: where is the evidence that one mark makes a whole person worthless? She arrives at \"I would prefer to do well, and one mock does not decide my future\" (E), feels disappointed rather than depressed, and returns to her books. Option b is right because Ellis placed the cause of disturbance in the B of the ABC model, the person's irrational beliefs. Exam trap: \"cognitive distortions\" (catastrophising, overgeneralisation and so on) is strictly the language of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1967); Ellis spoke of irrational beliefs. Examiners mix the two vocabularies, but both point to the same cognitive cause, so b still fits.", "topic": "Ellis's ABC model of REBT"}, {"q": "According to John Holland's RIASEC model, a mismatch between personality type and occupational environment is most likely to result in:", "options": {"a": "Increased crystallized intelligence but reduced fluid intelligence.", "b": "No measurable effects, since career outcomes are determined primarily by socioeconomic status.", "c": "Enhanced adaptability due to forced skill diversification.", "d": "Lower vocational satisfaction and greater likelihood of career instability."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: in John Holland's theory a poor fit between personality type and work environment predicts lower vocational satisfaction and more career instability. John L. Holland (1959; set out fully in 1973) proposed that both people and work environments can be described by six types, known by the initials RIASEC: Realistic (hands-on, mechanical), Investigative (scientific, analytical), Artistic (creative, unstructured), Social (helping, teaching), Enterprising (persuading, leading) and Conventional (orderly, data-keeping). Each person gets a three-letter code from tests such as his Self-Directed Search (1971), and each occupation gets a code from the people who work in it and the tasks it demands. The types sit on a hexagon: neighbours are alike (Realistic and Investigative), opposites least alike (Realistic and Social). The central prediction is congruence. People seek environments that let them use their skills and express their values, and the closer the match between their type and their environment, the greater their satisfaction, stability and achievement. A mismatch, called incongruence, brings dissatisfaction, poorer performance and a pull towards changing jobs or fields until a better fit is found. Take Arjun, whose code is Artistic-Social, working in a bank's accounts department, a Conventional environment of rules and ledgers. He is bored, keeps applying elsewhere, and finally retrains as an art teacher, where he settles. Research supports the prediction, though the link between congruence and satisfaction is real but modest. Option d is right because lower satisfaction and job instability are exactly what Holland's congruence principle predicts from a poor fit between person and environment. Exam trap: option c borrows the language of career development theories (adaptability, growth through challenge), but Holland's model is a matching model: misfit produces strain and change, not enhanced adaptability.", "topic": "Holland's congruence hypothesis"}, {"q": "The statement best illustrates the distinction between directive and nondirective counselling approaches:", "options": {"a": "Directive counseling focuses on emotional catharsis, whereas nondirective counseling emphasizes problem-solving.", "b": "Directive counseling is associated with client autonomy, whereas nondirective counseling minimizes client decision-making.", "c": "Directive counseling involves counselor-led interpretation and advice-giving, whereas nondirective counseling emphasizes the client's self-exploration and personal growth.", "d": "Directive counseling is rooted in existential therapy, whereas nondirective counseling originates from psychoanalysis."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: directive counselling is counsellor-led, built on diagnosis, interpretation and advice, while nondirective counselling lets the client explore and decide, trusting the client's capacity for growth. Directive counselling grew from the trait-factor approach of Frank Parsons (1909), who taught vocational guidance as knowing yourself, knowing the world of work and matching the two. Edmund G. Williamson (1939) of the University of Minnesota turned it into a method in How to Counsel Students. The counsellor collects data, often test scores, and moves through six steps: analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. The counsellor is the expert who interprets the evidence and recommends a course of action, and the focus is intellectual problem solving. Nondirective counselling came from Carl Rogers (1942) in Counseling and Psychotherapy. The counsellor does not advise, interpret or steer; he listens, reflects feelings and accepts the client, believing that a person who feels understood will find his own direction. Responsibility for the choice stays with the client, and the focus is on feelings and self-understanding. Rogers later renamed the approach client-centred, and Frederick Thorne (1950) argued for an eclectic middle path. Consider Meera, a Class 12 student torn between engineering and design. A directive counsellor reviews her aptitude and interest scores and advises design. A nondirective counsellor reflects her excitement when she talks about sketching and her fear of disappointing her father, until she reaches her own decision. Option c is right because it captures the core contrast: who leads the session, with interpretation and advice on one side and self-exploration and growth on the other. Exam trap: options a and b take the right ideas and swap them between the two schools; autonomy and feelings belong to Rogers, problem solving and counsellor control to Williamson.", "topic": "Directive versus nondirective counselling"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In Person-Centered Therapy, the therapist's unconditional positive regard fosters self-actualization. Reason (R): Because clients experience reduced incongruence when accepted without judgment.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and reduced incongruence is the route by which unconditional positive regard frees self-actualisation. Carl Rogers (1951, 1959) held that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow and fulfil potential. Growth is blocked by conditions of worth: as children we learn that we are loved only when we think, feel or act in approved ways, so we deny or distort experiences that clash with the approved self-concept. The resulting mismatch between self-concept and actual experience is incongruence, which Rogers saw as the root of anxiety and maladjustment. Step 1, the Assertion: true. In his 1957 paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change, Rogers named three core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (UPR) and empathy. UPR, a term coined by Stanley Standal (1954), means valuing the client without conditions, and Rogers held that it frees the actualising tendency. Step 2, the Reason: true. When feelings meet acceptance rather than judgement, the client no longer needs to defend against them, so denied experiences can enter awareness and the self-concept widens to include them; incongruence falls. Step 3, the link: R explains A. Falling incongruence is the mechanism that lets the client move towards being a fully functioning person. Take Kabir, who believes \"a good son never feels angry with his father\". In therapy his anger meets warm acceptance, not shock. He admits it, comes to see himself as a loving son who is sometimes angry, and stops spending energy hiding it. Option a is right because Rogers explicitly links acceptance, falling incongruence and renewed growth in one causal chain. Exam trap: \"congruence\" names both a therapist condition (genuineness) and a client state (self matching experience); examiners blur the two to make b look tempting.", "topic": "Rogers: acceptance and incongruence"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is equally effective across all cultural contexts. Reason (R): Because maladaptive cognitive schemas are universal and unaffected by cultural variations.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, and d is the only option that rejects it. Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) has a large evidence base, but most of its trials were run with Western, educated, industrialised, rich and democratic samples, the WEIRD populations described by Joseph Henrich, Steven Heine and Ara Norenzayan (2010). Step 1, the Assertion: false. CBT is not equally effective in every cultural context. Meta-analyses of culturally adapted interventions, such as that of Derek Griner and Timothy Smith (2006), find that treatments adapted in language, metaphors, content and goals do better for minority and non-Western clients than unadapted ones. The culturally adapted CBT trials led by Farooq Naeem in Pakistan, which fitted the therapy's language, examples and family involvement to local culture, are a well-known example. Step 2, the Reason: also false. Aaron Beck (1967) described schemas as core beliefs formed from early experience, and that experience is cultural. What counts as an irrational belief, how much weight family obligations carry, whether the self is defined individually or collectively: all of these vary across cultures. Step 3, the link, does not arise. Consider Arjun, a 24-year-old in Kochi, who believes \"I must follow my parents' choice of career\". An unadapted therapist might dispute this as a rigid \"should\"; in his joint family it is a shared value, and attacking it alienates him. An adapted therapist works with the family's goals instead. Option d is right because it is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is what the item tests. Exam trap: \"evidence-based\" does not mean \"universally effective\"; strong efficacy in one population does not transfer automatically to another. Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no \"both false\" option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question, so no one was marked on it.", "topic": "Culture and CBT effectiveness"}, {"q": "'Transactional Analysis' as a psychotherapy approach focuses on:", "options": {"a": "Unconscious conflicts and defense mechanisms", "b": "Analyzing ego states (Parent, Adult, Child) and life scripts", "c": "Client-centered empathy and unconditional positive regard", "d": "Challenging irrational beliefs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: transactional analysis (TA) analyses the ego states Parent, Adult and Child, the transactions between them, and the life scripts people follow. Eric Berne (1961) founded TA in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and popularised it in Games People Play (1964). Berne, trained as a psychoanalyst, borrowed the term ego state from psychoanalytic ego psychology but made it observable: an ego state is a consistent pattern of feeling and behaviour that you can see and hear. The Parent holds attitudes copied from parents and parental figures, critical or nurturing. The Adult processes present reality objectively. The Child holds feelings and responses from childhood, free or adapted. TA works at four levels. Structural analysis identifies which ego state is active. Transactional analysis proper studies exchanges: complementary transactions run in parallel and talk flows, crossed ones stop it. Game analysis looks at repeated ulterior transactions with a predictable bad-feeling payoff, such as \"Why Don't You, Yes But\". Script analysis uncovers the life script, an unconscious life plan decided in childhood under parental messages such as \"Don't be close\". People also need strokes, units of recognition, and prefer negative strokes to none. Picture Meera asking her husband Rahul, \"Where are my keys?\" (Adult to Adult). He replies, \"Why do you always blame me?\" (Child to Parent). The transaction is crossed and an argument begins; a TA therapist helps both notice which ego state is speaking. Option b is right because ego states and scripts are the defining units of analysis in TA. Exam trap: the three ego states loosely echo the superego, ego and id of Sigmund Freud (1923), so option a tempts; but Berne studied observable social transactions, not unconscious conflict and defences.", "topic": "Berne's transactional analysis"}, {"q": "When using 'Single-Subject Designs' (e.g., ABAB reversal design) in research, a key assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "The treatment effect is irreversible", "b": "The behavior will return to baseline levels when the intervention is withdrawn", "c": "Generalizability to a larger population is the primary goal", "d": "Multiple participants are required for statistical validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: an ABAB reversal design assumes that the target behaviour will return towards baseline when the intervention is withdrawn. Single-subject (single-case) designs study one person, or a few, measured repeatedly over time. Murray Sidman (1960) set out their logic in Tactics of Scientific Research, and Alan Kazdin (1982) systematised them for clinical work. Phase A is the baseline: the behaviour is recorded, by frequency, duration or time intervals, until it is stable, which predicts what would happen without treatment. Phase B introduces the treatment. In the second A the treatment is withdrawn, and in the second B it is reinstated. The logic is replication within one person. If the behaviour improves each time the treatment starts and worsens each time it stops, coincidence, maturation or outside events become very unlikely explanations, and the treatment is shown to control the behaviour. That logic only works if the behaviour can reverse. Take Arjun, 8, who hits classmates about 12 times a day at baseline. With token reinforcement for calm play, hitting falls to 3 a day; when the tokens stop it climbs back to 10; when they return it drops to 2. That rise and fall is the evidence. If the treatment had taught a lasting skill, such as reading, the behaviour would not reverse and the design would fail, and withdrawing an effective treatment for self-injury would be unethical. In such cases the multiple-baseline design, which starts treatment at staggered times across behaviours, settings or people, is used instead. Option b is right because the return to baseline during withdrawal is the very event that demonstrates experimental control. Exam trap: option a states the reverse of the assumption; an irreversible effect is exactly the situation in which ABAB cannot be used.", "topic": "ABAB reversal design logic"}, {"q": "The concept of 'Neuroplasticity' in the context of recovery from brain injury implicitly assumes that:", "options": {"a": "The brain has the capacity to reorganize and compensate for damage.", "b": "The brain cannot recover any lost function", "c": "All brain damage is permanent and irreversible", "d": "Only very young brains exhibit plasticity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the idea of neuroplasticity in recovery assumes that the brain can reorganise itself and compensate for damage. Neuroplasticity is the nervous system's capacity to change its structure and function with experience, learning and injury. After a stroke or head injury some early recovery has simpler causes: swelling subsides, blood flow returns to tissue around the lesion, and distant areas stunned by the sudden loss of their inputs start working again (diaschisis, a term of Constantin von Monakow, 1914). Longer-term recovery depends on plasticity proper. Surviving axons send branches into vacated synapses (collateral sprouting), silent connections are unmasked, and neighbouring cortex, or the matching area in the other hemisphere, takes over lost territory (cortical reorganisation). Randolph Nudo and colleagues (1996) showed in monkeys that after a small motor cortex lesion, retraining the hand preserved, and sometimes enlarged, the hand area in nearby cortex. Constraint-induced movement therapy, developed by Edward Taub (1993), restrains a stroke patient's good arm so that the weak arm must be used intensively, enlarging its cortical map. Take Kabir, 45, whose left-hemisphere stroke weakened his right hand. Weeks of daily training with his left hand in a mitt improve his grip and writing, and scans show more activity in motor areas around the damage. Dead neurons are rarely replaced, but function can be rerouted. Recovery is better with small lesions, intensive practice and high cognitive reserve. Option a is right because every rehabilitation programme rests on the premise that the brain can rewire and compensate. Exam trap: option d echoes the Kennard principle of Margaret Kennard (1936), that early lesions are better tolerated; youth helps, but adult brains are plastic too, as stroke rehabilitation shows.", "topic": "Neuroplasticity and brain recovery"}, {"q": "In hypothesis testing, which situation best illustrates a Type II error?", "options": {"a": "Concluding that a new therapy is effective when in fact it is not.", "b": "Failing to reject the null hypothesis when the null is actually true.", "c": "Using a significance level (alpha) smaller than the actual probability of committing a Type I error.", "d": "Concluding that a new therapy is not effective when in fact it is effective."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a Type II error is concluding that a therapy does not work when it actually does, that is, failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) framed hypothesis testing as a decision with two possible errors. The null hypothesis (H0) says there is no effect; the alternative says there is one. A Type I error is rejecting a true H0, a false alarm; its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets, usually .05. A Type II error is retaining a false H0, a miss; its probability is beta. Power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of detecting a real effect, and Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested aiming for at least .80. Type II errors become likely when the sample is small, the true effect is small, measurement is noisy, or alpha is very strict, because a stricter alpha lowers Type I risk at the cost of raising Type II risk. Take Riya, who tests a new mindfulness programme for exam anxiety with only 12 students. The programme genuinely helps, but with so few participants her p value is .20, so she retains H0 and reports that the programme is ineffective. That is a Type II error, caused by low power; with 100 students the same effect would probably have reached significance. Option d is right because it describes a real effect being missed, a false H0 that was not rejected. Exam trap: option b borrows the phrase \"failing to reject the null hypothesis\" from the definition of a Type II error but adds \"when the null is actually true\", which turns it into a correct decision. Ask two questions every time: what was decided, and what is the truth? Type I is a false positive, Type II a false negative.", "topic": "Type I and Type II errors"}, {"q": "A test item shows a high difficulty index (p = 0.85) and a negative discrimination index. What is the most likely interpretation?", "options": {"a": "The item is too easy and better distinguishes between high- and low-performing examinees.", "b": "The item is too difficult and should be retained to reduce test variance.", "c": "The item is moderately difficult but has excellent discrimination.", "d": "The item is too easy and may be miskeyed, since high scorers are more likely to answer it incorrectly."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: p = 0.85 means the item is easy, and a negative discrimination index means high scorers fail it more often than low scorers, which usually signals a miskeyed or flawed item. The difficulty index, p, is simply the proportion of examinees who answer the item correctly. It is really an easiness index: the higher the p, the easier the item, so at p = .85, 85 percent passed. Items of middle difficulty (around .50, a little higher for multiple-choice items because of guessing) spread scores best. The discrimination index, D, compares the top and bottom groups on the total test, classically the upper and lower 27 percent recommended by Truman Kelley (1939): D is the proportion correct in the upper group minus the proportion correct in the lower group. D runs from minus 1 to plus 1. A positive D, ideally .30 or more, means the item separates strong from weak candidates; a D near zero means it does not discriminate. A negative D means weaker students do better than stronger ones, the reverse of what a good item does. The usual causes are a scoring error (the wrong option marked as correct), an ambiguous stem, two defensible answers, or a trick that misleads students who know the subject in depth. Suppose Meera analyses her class test. On item 12, 70 percent of the top group but 95 percent of the bottom group answered correctly, so D = .70 minus .95 = minus .25. She checks and finds that the scoring sheet says b when the correct answer is c. Option d is right because it reads both numbers correctly: an easy item with reversed discrimination, so check the scoring and revise or drop it. Exam trap: \"high difficulty index\" sounds like \"very difficult\", but p counts passes, so a high p means easy.", "topic": "Item difficulty and discrimination"}, {"q": "Which feature most differentiates military selection tests from civilian aptitude tests?", "options": {"a": "Reliance on measures of crystallized intelligence to predict long-term learning.", "b": "Emphasis on group-administered timed tests for large-scale screening and quick deployment.", "c": "Heavy dependence on projective techniques to assess unconscious motives.", "d": "Prioritization of vocational interest inventories over general cognitive ability."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: what most sets military selection tests apart is their reliance on group-administered, timed tests that can screen huge numbers of recruits quickly. Group testing itself was born of military need. When the United States entered the First World War, Robert Mearns Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, led a committee that built the Army Alpha and Army Beta. Alpha was a verbal, paper-and-pencil test for literate recruits, with items on following directions, arithmetic, analogies and general information. Beta was a pictorial, non-verbal test for recruits who were illiterate or did not speak English, explained by mime and demonstration. About 1.75 million men were tested, many in large halls at once, with strict time limits and quick scoring stencils. The results sorted recruits into ranks, training streams and officer candidates within days, something no individually given Binet-style test could do. The same logic continues in the Armed Services Vocational Aptitude Battery (ASVAB) used by the US military today. Civilian aptitude tests usually serve guidance and selection for education or particular jobs, where a detailed profile of the individual matters more than speed of deployment. Imagine Rohan sitting a services entrance test in a hall with 400 other candidates: identical printed booklets, a stopwatch, machine-scored answer sheets and results within days. Option b is right because mass, fast, standardised group screening is the defining feature that military testing introduced and still emphasises. Exam trap: Indian students know that the Services Selection Board (SSB) uses a picture-story test and word association for officer candidates, so option c can tempt; those projective-style tests come after group screening and are not what distinguishes military testing as a whole.", "topic": "Military group testing origins"}, {"q": "During the propagation of an action potential, which event ensures the unidirectional flow of the neural impulse along the axon?", "options": {"a": "The absolute refractory period caused by voltage-gated sodium channel inactivation.", "b": "The relative refractory period caused by the hyperpolarization of the membrane.", "c": "The saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier in myelinated axons.", "d": "The temporal summation of excitatory postsynaptic potentials at the axon hillock."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the absolute refractory period, caused by inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels, ensures that an action potential travels only one way along the axon. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out the ionic basis of the action potential from the squid giant axon. When the membrane at one point reaches threshold, voltage-gated Na+ channels open and sodium rushes in, driving the inside positive. Within about a millisecond these channels inactivate: an inactivation gate plugs them, and they cannot reopen until the membrane has repolarised. For roughly 1 to 2 milliseconds after a spike begins, no stimulus, however strong, can fire that patch again. This is the absolute refractory period. Now follow the impulse. Current from the active patch spreads passively in both directions. Ahead lies resting membrane, which is pushed to threshold and fires. Behind lies the patch that has just fired, whose Na+ channels are still inactivated, so the spreading current cannot trigger it. The spike therefore moves forward only, from axon hillock to terminal buttons, like a flame along a Diwali fuse that cannot burn back over the part already burnt. The same period also limits how fast a neuron can fire. If an axon is stimulated artificially in the middle, spikes run in both directions, which shows that direction depends on where firing starts and on refractoriness, not on any one-way property of the axon itself. Option a is right because sodium channel inactivation makes the membrane just behind the spike temporarily unexcitable. Exam trap: the relative refractory period also follows the spike, so option b tempts; but during it a strong enough stimulus can still fire the membrane, so it cannot guarantee one-way travel, and it reflects open potassium channels rather than inactivated sodium channels.", "topic": "Refractory period and one-way conduction"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims to increase psychological flexibility. Reason (R): Because ACT focuses on eliminating distressing thoughts through direct cognitive restructuring.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) does not try to eliminate distressing thoughts or restructure them. Step 1, the Assertion: true. Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999) developed ACT, a third-wave behaviour therapy, and its stated aim is psychological flexibility: staying in contact with the present moment and acting on one's values whatever thoughts and feelings are present. It builds this through six processes, drawn as the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment contact, self-as-context, values and committed action. Step 2, the Reason: false. ACT rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of language, and argues that the struggle to get rid of unwanted thoughts and feelings, called experiential avoidance, is itself the problem. Thought suppression backfires, as the white bear studies of Daniel Wegner (1987) showed: people told not to think of a white bear kept thinking of it, and thought of it even more afterwards. So ACT changes the person's relationship to a thought, not its content. Defusion teaches clients to see a thought as just words, for instance by saying \"I am having the thought that I will fail\". Direct cognitive restructuring, examining the evidence for a thought and replacing it with a more balanced one, belongs to the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1979). Step 3, the link, does not arise because R is false. Take Meera, who thinks \"I will embarrass myself\" before every seminar. A cognitive therapist would test the evidence for that prediction; an ACT therapist helps her notice the thought, make room for the anxiety, and present anyway because teaching matters to her. Option c is right because A states ACT's real aim while R wrongly gives ACT the method of Beck's therapy. Exam trap: both therapies sit under the CBT umbrella, so examiners swap their techniques; elimination and disputing belong to the second wave, acceptance and defusion to the third.", "topic": "ACT and psychological flexibility"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In a repeated measures ANOVA, violation of the sphericity assumption can bias results. Reason (R): Because violation of sphericity inflates the Type I error rate.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the inflated Type I error rate is exactly how a sphericity violation biases a repeated measures ANOVA. In a repeated measures design the same people are tested under three or more conditions, and the univariate F test assumes sphericity: the variances of the difference scores between every pair of conditions are equal. Step 1, the Assertion: true. When sphericity fails, the F ratio no longer follows the F distribution with the usual degrees of freedom, so its p values are wrong. John Mauchly (1940) gave the standard test for it. Step 2, the Reason: true. The F test becomes too liberal: with alpha set at .05, the real chance of a false positive can be noticeably higher. Step 3, the link: R explains A. The bias the Assertion speaks of is precisely this positive bias in F and the extra Type I errors it causes. That is why corrections shrink the degrees of freedom by an estimate called epsilon: the Greenhouse-Geisser correction (Samuel Greenhouse and Seymour Geisser, 1959), which is more conservative, and the Huynh-Feldt correction (Huynh Huynh and Leonard Feldt, 1976), which is less so. A multivariate approach (MANOVA), which does not assume sphericity, is another route. Take Arjun, who measures anxiety in the same clients at baseline, week 4, week 8 and month 6. Differences between time points far apart vary more than differences between adjacent ones, so Mauchly's test is significant. His uncorrected p is .04, but after the Greenhouse-Geisser correction it is .09; reporting the first would risk a false positive. Option a is right because Type I inflation is the mechanism behind the bias. Exam trap: with only two conditions there is just one set of difference scores, so sphericity is automatically met; and Bartlett's test of sphericity (Maurice Bartlett, 1950), used before factor analysis, is a different test altogether.", "topic": "Sphericity in repeated measures ANOVA"}, {"q": "Which mechanism best explains synaptic facilitation observed during high-frequency stimulation?", "options": {"a": "Increased probability of vesicle release due to residual presynaptic calcium accumulation.", "b": "Upregulation of postsynaptic receptor density through long-term potentiation.", "c": "Decreased presynaptic neurotransmitter release caused by autoreceptor activation.", "d": "Postsynaptic desensitization leading to temporary hyperexcitability."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: synaptic facilitation during high-frequency stimulation is explained by residual calcium in the presynaptic terminal, which raises the probability that vesicles will be released. Ordinary transmission starts when an action potential reaches the terminal button and opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; calcium entry makes vesicles fuse with the membrane and release transmitter (exocytosis). Release is probabilistic: each spike releases only some of the vesicles that are ready. After the spike, calcium is pumped out and buffered, but that takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds. If a second spike arrives before clearance is complete, its fresh calcium adds to what is left over, so the calcium level near the release sites is higher than after the first spike, and more vesicles are released. Bernard Katz and Ricardo Miledi (1968) proposed this residual calcium hypothesis from work at the frog neuromuscular junction. With two stimuli close together the effect is called paired-pulse facilitation; during a high-frequency train it builds up and is joined by augmentation and post-tetanic potentiation, which last seconds to minutes. This is short-term plasticity: presynaptic, fast and transient. Suppose Riya, in a physiology practical, stimulates a nerve twice, 20 milliseconds apart. The second postsynaptic potential is clearly larger than the first, though nothing has changed postsynaptically; that is facilitation. At synapses that already release most of their ready vesicles, the opposite, depression from vesicle depletion, can dominate instead. Option a is right because the extra release comes from accumulated presynaptic calcium, not from any change in receptors. Exam trap: option b names long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973), which also follows high-frequency stimulation; but LTP lasts hours to weeks and depends mainly on postsynaptic NMDA receptor activation and added AMPA receptors, whereas facilitation fades within about a second.", "topic": "Short-term synaptic facilitation"}, {"q": "A key limitation of retrospective cohort studies compared to prospective cohort studies is:", "options": {"a": "Inability to study rare exposures.", "b": "Greater susceptibility to recall bias and incomplete records.", "c": "Lack of ability to measure incidence rates.", "d": "Higher financial and time costs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: retrospective cohort studies rely on records made in the past, often for other purposes, so data may be incomplete or inconsistent, and any exposure information gathered from memory brings recall bias. A cohort study classifies people who do not yet have the outcome as exposed or unexposed and compares how many in each group develop it, which yields incidence and relative risk. In a prospective cohort the researcher identifies the groups now and follows them into the future, measuring exposure in a planned, uniform way. The British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill (1951) and the Framingham Heart Study (from 1948) are classics. In a retrospective (historical) cohort the researcher starts today but uses existing records to identify who was exposed years ago, then traces what happened up to the present. The reasoning still runs from exposure to outcome; only the data collection looks back. The price is data quality. The researcher cannot choose what was measured, so key variables and confounders may be missing, recorded differently by different staff, or lost; and if exposure has to be filled in by interviewing people, their memories may be coloured by what has since happened to them. Take Kabir, who uses a hospital's 2010 to 2015 neonatal records to compare children who had severe newborn jaundice with those who did not, then checks school records for learning difficulties. Many files lack birth weight or treatment details, and parents asked to recall feeding problems may remember selectively. Option b is right because incomplete records and recall bias are the characteristic weaknesses of looking back. Exam trap: recall bias is classically tied to case-control studies, so students hesitate; it also threatens retrospective cohorts whenever exposure data come from memory.", "topic": "Retrospective versus prospective cohorts"}, {"q": "According to the Geneplore Model (Finke, Ward, & Smith), the creative process involves:", "options": {"a": "Alternating phases of divergent thinking (idea generation) and convergent thinking (idea evaluation).", "b": "Neural activation of the right hemisphere exclusively during novel problem-solving.", "c": "Direct transfer of unconscious imagery into creative products without cognitive mediation.", "d": "Pre-inventive structures generated in the generative phase, which are then explored and refined."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the Geneplore model, creative thinking cycles between a generative phase that builds rough mental 'pre-inventive structures' and an exploratory phase that interprets and refines them. Ronald Finke, Thomas Ward and Steven Smith (1992) set out the model in their book Creative Cognition. Its name joins 'generate' and 'explore'. In the generative phase a person retrieves, associates, combines and transforms ideas to form a pre-inventive structure: a half-formed shape, a mental blueprint, an odd word combination or a category example that is not yet an invention. In the exploratory phase the person studies that structure, finds new attributes in it, imagines what it could be used for, shifts it into new contexts and tests its limits. If the result fails, the person goes back and generates again, so the two phases alternate until a product emerges. Constraints (for example, 'it must be furniture') can be added at either phase. In Ronald Finke's creative imagery experiments, people mentally combined a few simple shapes into a form and only then interpreted it as an invention; forms interpreted this way were often judged more creative than ones built with the purpose fixed in advance. Example: Kabir, a design student, mentally joins a cylinder, a hook and a flat disc, then explores the shape and sees a hanging lamp. When it looks unstable, he goes back and builds a new form. Option d is right because it names the model's own two phases and its central term, pre-inventive structures. Exam trap: option a describes the divergent and convergent thinking of Joy Paul Guilford (1950), which sounds like 'generate, then evaluate' but uses Guilford's terms, not the Geneplore model's. Geneplore is a cognitive account, so brain-only and unconscious-only explanations do not fit it.", "topic": "Geneplore model of creativity"}, {"q": "Which observation most specifically reflects a thought process abnormality rather than a thought content abnormality in MSE?", "options": {"a": "The patient reports hearing voices commanding them to act.", "b": "The patient believes neighbors are plotting against them.", "c": "The patient's speech is tangential, moving from one topic to another without completing ideas.", "d": "The patient expresses persistent thoughts of worthlessness and guilt."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: tangential speech is a disorder of thought process (the form or stream of thought), because it shows how ideas are linked, not what they are about. The mental status examination (MSE) splits thinking into two parts. Thought process, also called the form or stream of thought, describes how ideas connect and flow: their speed, their logical links and whether they reach a goal. Its disorders include flight of ideas, loosening of associations, tangentiality, circumstantiality, thought block and perseveration. Thought content describes what the person is thinking about: delusions, obsessions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations, suicidal ideas and themes such as guilt or worthlessness. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, made the split between form and content a basic tool of descriptive psychopathology. Nancy Andreasen (1979) later defined tangentiality as replying to a question in an oblique or irrelevant way, so the answer drifts off and never returns to the point. It differs from circumstantiality, in which the speaker takes long detours but finally reaches the goal. Example: Meera is asked, 'Where do you live?' She replies, 'Near the beach; the sea reminds me of my school trip; the teachers were very strict then; discipline is important for children...' and never names her town. The clinician records this under thought process, whatever the topic happens to be. Option c is right because tangentiality is defined by the broken path between ideas, not by any belief or theme. Exam trap: hallucinations look like a 'thought' symptom but belong to perception, while delusions and depressive ideas belong to thought content; examiners also swap tangentiality (never reaches the goal) with circumstantiality (gets there slowly).", "topic": "MSE: thought form versus content"}, {"q": "According to Rowe and Kahn's model of successful aging, which of the following best captures the three central criteria?", "options": {"a": "Avoidance of disease, maintenance of high physical/cognitive function, and active engagement with life.", "b": "Acceptance of physical decline, spiritual fulfillment, and intergenerational continuity.", "c": "Genetic resilience, cognitive plasticity, and environmental enrichment.", "d": "Economic independence, physical vitality, and low emotional distress."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: John Rowe and Robert Kahn (1997) defined successful ageing by three criteria: low probability of disease and disability, high cognitive and physical functioning, and active engagement with life. Rowe, a physician, and Kahn, a social psychologist, led the MacArthur Foundation research network on ageing. In 1987, in the journal Science, they argued that research had studied only 'usual' ageing and wrongly treated common losses as inevitable. In 1997 they set out the full model: successful ageing needs (1) avoiding disease and disease-related disability, (2) maintaining high mental and physical function, and (3) active engagement with life, meaning close relationships with others and productive activity, paid or unpaid. The three overlap, and successful ageing lies where all three meet. They stressed that lifestyle factors such as exercise, diet and social ties shape late life more than people assume, so much decline is not fixed by age alone. Critics replied that the model labels most people with chronic illness as 'unsuccessful', ignores what older people themselves value and overlooks poverty and social inequality. Paul Baltes and Margret Baltes (1990) offered a process alternative, selective optimisation with compensation. Example: Arjun's grandfather, 74, has no diabetes or heart disease, walks five kilometres daily, solves crosswords and teaches village children twice a week. He meets all three criteria. His neighbour, equally active and cheerful but living with arthritis, fails the first criterion, which is exactly the critics' point. Option a is right because it lists Rowe and Kahn's three components in their own order and wording. Exam trap: examiners mix in Erik Erikson's (1950) integrity, Lars Tornstam's (1989) gerotranscendence or the Baltes model, all real ideas about ageing that are not Rowe and Kahn's criteria.", "topic": "Rowe and Kahn successful ageing"}, {"q": "According to Murray Bowen's Family Systems Theory, which of the following best represents the concept of differentiation of self?", "options": {"a": "The ability of family members to maintain emotional independence while staying connected to others.", "b": "The process by which unresolved parental conflicts are projected onto children.", "c": "The cycle in which marital stress contributes to child dysfunction.", "d": "The degree to which family members rigidly adhere to enmeshed boundaries."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: differentiation of self is the capacity to keep one's own thinking, feelings and identity while staying emotionally connected to the family. Murray Bowen (1913 to 1990) built family systems theory from his 1950s work at the National Institute of Mental Health, where whole families of patients with schizophrenia lived on the ward. He saw the family as an emotional unit in which anxiety passes from member to member. Differentiation of self, his master concept, has two sides. Inside the person, it is the ability to separate thinking from feeling, so that one can choose a response instead of reacting automatically. Between people, it is the ability to stay oneself while in close contact: neither fused with the family (absorbing its anxiety and opinions) nor cut off from it (cold distance). Bowen pictured a scale from low to high differentiation; people low on it are reactive, seek approval and pull others into triangles under stress. His other concepts include triangles, the nuclear family emotional process, the family projection process, multigenerational transmission, emotional cutoff, sibling position and societal emotional process. A Bowenian therapist stays calm, avoids being drawn into triangles, coaches each member to take 'I-positions' and maps three generations in a genogram. Example: Riya, 25, wants to study psychology while her parents want medicine. She explains her view calmly, listens to their worries and keeps visiting as usual; she neither storms out nor gives in to keep the peace. That is high differentiation. Option a is right because it captures both halves of the concept: autonomy and continued connection. Exam trap: emotional distance looks like independence but is emotional cutoff, a sign of poor differentiation, and 'enmeshed boundaries' is a term from the structural family therapy of Salvador Minuchin (1974), not Bowen.", "topic": "Bowen differentiation of self"}, {"q": "'Item Response Theory (IRT)' in psychological testing is an advanced approach that aims to:", "options": {"a": "Calculate a single score for each test taker", "b": "Model the relationship between an individual's ability and their probability of answering an item correctly", "c": "Determine the reliability of a test using Cronbach's Alpha", "d": "Establish norms for a test based on a representative sample"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: item response theory models how a person's level on a latent trait (ability) relates to the probability of answering each item correctly. Classical test theory (CTT), going back to Charles Spearman (1904), treats an observed score as true score plus error (X = T + E) and describes items by indices such as difficulty (p, the proportion passing) that depend on the sample tested. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, models each item instead. Frederic Lord (1952) laid its foundations, and Georg Rasch (1960) published the one-parameter Rasch model. The core tool is the item characteristic curve: an S-shaped curve plotting the probability of a correct response against ability, written θ (theta). The one-parameter model uses item difficulty (b, the ability at which the chance of success is 0.50); the two-parameter model adds discrimination (a, the steepness of the curve); the three-parameter model adds guessing (c, the lower floor of the curve). Because item parameters are, in principle, independent of the sample, and ability estimates independent of the particular items, IRT supports test equating, item banks, checks for item bias and computerised adaptive testing. Example: in an adaptive test, Meera answers a medium item correctly, so the computer gives her a harder one; she fails it, so the next is slightly easier. After about 15 items her θ is estimated about as precisely as a much longer fixed test would manage. Option b is right because modelling the link between ability and the probability of success on each item is the definition of IRT. Exam trap: examiners offer CTT ideas (a total score, Cronbach's alpha, norms) as if they were IRT aims; IRT does yield a score, but its aim is the item-level model.", "topic": "Item response theory"}, {"q": "When conducting a 'Phenomenological Study', the researcher's 'bracketing' or 'epoche' is crucial because it assumes that:", "options": {"a": "The researcher's preconceived notions will enhance the objectivity of the study", "b": "The researcher must set aside their own biases and assumptions to genuinely understand the lived experiences of participants", "c": "The researcher should actively impose their interpretations on the data", "d": "The researcher's personal experiences are irrelevant to the research process."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: bracketing (epoché) means the researcher sets aside their own assumptions so that participants' lived experience can be understood as they live it. Phenomenology studies how a phenomenon appears in a person's own experience: what it is like to grieve, to hear voices or to care for a parent with dementia. Edmund Husserl (1913) gave the method its key step, the epoché, from the Greek for suspension or holding back. The researcher first becomes aware of their beliefs, theories and personal history about the topic, then suspends them, like putting them in brackets in an equation, so that the description is not forced into a ready-made frame. Clark Moustakas (1994) turned this into a research procedure: the researcher writes out their own experience of the topic, collects participants' accounts, treats every statement as equally important at first (horizontalisation), groups statements into themes and writes a textural description (what was experienced) and a structural description (how it was experienced) that lead to the essence. Martin Heidegger (1927) replied that no one can fully step out of their own world, so interpretative versions such as Jonathan Smith's interpretative phenomenological analysis (1996) ask instead for honest reflexivity about the researcher's influence. Example: Riya, whose father died when she was 18, studies young widows in Kerala. She notes in a journal her belief that grief is mostly sadness, holds it aside in interviews, and hears relief, anger and money fears she might otherwise have missed. Option b is right because suspending one's own biases in order to grasp participants' lived experience is exactly what bracketing is for. Exam trap: option d also sounds like 'setting aside', but bracketing does not say the researcher's experience is irrelevant; it says that experience is influential enough to be named and held in check.", "topic": "Phenomenology and bracketing"}, {"q": "The 'Social Readjustment Rating Scale' (Holmes & Rahe) is a tool used to measure:", "options": {"a": "Personality traits", "b": "Life stress and its potential impact on health", "c": "Social support networks", "d": "Emotional intelligence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Social Readjustment Rating Scale measures life stress, as the amount of life change a person has faced, and was built to predict later illness. Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe (1967) started from the idea that any major change, pleasant or unpleasant, demands readjustment, and that heavy readjustment strains the body. They asked hundreds of people to rate 43 life events against marriage, which was fixed at 50. Each event received a value in life change units (LCU): death of a spouse 100, divorce 73, marriage 50, change in residence 20, holiday 13 and minor violations of the law 11. A person ticks the events of the past year and the units are summed. Later work by Holmes and colleagues linked totals of about 300 or more with a much higher chance of illness in the following year or two, and Rahe found similar links in studies of naval crews, though the correlations were modest. Critics noted that the scale mixes good and bad events, ignores how a person appraises an event (the transactional model of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman, 1984), relies on memory, and confuses causes with consequences, since illness can itself cause events such as job loss. Allen Kanner, Lazarus and colleagues (1981) found that daily hassles predicted symptoms better than major life events. Example: in one year Kabir marries (50), moves house (20) and takes a holiday (13), a total of 83 LCU, even though two of these events made him happy. Option b is right because the SRRS quantifies stressful life change in order to estimate its health impact. Exam trap: the SRRS counts events, not personality, support or coping, and because change itself is the stressor, positive events such as marriage and holidays carry scores too.", "topic": "Holmes and Rahe SRRS"}, {"q": "The 'Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)' approach to treating anxiety disorders implicitly assumes that:", "options": {"a": "Anxiety is solely a result of unconscious conflicts", "b": "Maladaptive thought patterns and avoidance behaviors play a central role in maintaining anxiety", "c": "Anxiety is a purely a thought reaction that parallels a physiological phenomenon", "d": "Exposure to feared situations should always be avoided"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: CBT for anxiety assumes that anxious thoughts (overestimating danger, underestimating coping) and avoidance behaviours keep anxiety going. Aaron Beck, with Gary Emery and Ruth Greenberg (1985), described anxiety as a response to perceived threat: the anxious person overestimates the probability and cost of danger and underestimates their ability to cope. David Clark (1986) applied this to panic disorder: the person misreads normal bodily sensations, such as a racing heart, as signs of catastrophe, such as a heart attack, which raises fear and the sensations further in a vicious circle. Behaviour then keeps the cycle alive. O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory explained that fear is learnt by classical conditioning and maintained by avoidance, because escape brings relief (negative reinforcement). Paul Salkovskis (1991) added that subtle safety behaviours, such as gripping a chair 'to stop fainting', stop the person from discovering that the feared outcome would not have happened. CBT therefore targets both: cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments test the thoughts, and graded exposure ends avoidance until fear falls and the feared prediction is disconfirmed. Example: Meera fears she will faint during a seminar. She thinks 'my pounding heart means I will collapse', holds the desk, avoids eye contact and skips seminars when she can. Her therapist helps her weigh the evidence and then present without holding the desk; she does not faint, and her belief weakens. Option b is right because the maintaining role of maladaptive thoughts and avoidance is the central working assumption of CBT for anxiety. Exam trap: absolute words ('solely', 'purely', 'always') mark the distractors, and option d reverses CBT's logic, since avoidance is the problem and exposure the cure.", "topic": "CBT model of anxiety"}, {"q": "'Motivational Interviewing' is a client-centered counseling style for eliciting behavior change by helping clients to explore and resolve:", "options": {"a": "Unconscious conflicts", "b": "Irrational beliefs", "c": "Past traumas", "d": "Ambivalence"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: motivational interviewing helps clients explore and resolve ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting to change at the same time. William Miller (1983) first described motivational interviewing (MI) for problem drinkers and developed it with Stephen Rollnick in their book Motivational Interviewing (1991). Its starting point is that most people stuck in a harmful habit are not simply 'in denial'; they hold two sets of reasons at once. When a counsellor argues for change (the 'righting reflex'), the client defends the other side and talks themselves further into staying the same. MI instead draws out the client's own reasons. The counsellor uses OARS skills (open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries), develops discrepancy between present behaviour and the client's deeper goals, rolls with resistance and supports self-efficacy. The aim is to strengthen change talk (desire, ability, reasons and need to change, then commitment) and soften sustain talk. Its spirit is partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation. MI fits the stages of change of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983), especially for people in precontemplation and contemplation, and is widely used for alcohol, drugs, treatment adherence and health behaviour. Example: Arjun says, 'I know drinking is hurting my liver, but it is the only way I relax after work.' The counsellor reflects both sides: 'Part of you needs a way to unwind, and part of you is worried about your health.' Arjun then lists his own reasons to cut down, which carry more weight than any lecture. Option d is right because resolving ambivalence is the defining purpose of MI in Miller and Rollnick's own description. Exam trap: MI is client-centred yet directive, and it belongs to Miller and Rollnick, not to Prochaska and DiClemente, whose model only describes the stages of readiness.", "topic": "Motivational interviewing"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A non-significant result in hypothesis testing always means that the null hypothesis is true. Reason (R): Because the absence of statistical significance indicates absolute absence of an effect in the population.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, because a non-significant result only means we failed to reject the null hypothesis (H0), not that H0 is true. Step 1, the Assertion on its own: a test asks how likely data this extreme would be if H0 were true. If p is above alpha (say .05), the data are compatible with H0, so we 'fail to reject' or 'retain' it. That is not proof. Ronald Fisher (1935) wrote that the null hypothesis is never proved or established, but is possibly disproved. A real effect can be missed because the sample is small, the measure is noisy or the effect is modest: this is a Type II error, with probability beta. Jacob Cohen (1962) showed that the power (1 minus beta) of many published psychology studies was low. So A is false. Step 2, the Reason on its own: 'absence of significance indicates absolute absence of an effect' repeats the same error. Douglas Altman and Martin Bland (1995) put it simply: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. To claim no meaningful effect, a researcher needs a confidence interval narrow enough to exclude important effects, or an equivalence test. So R is also false. Step 3: since neither is true, the question of explanation does not arise. Example: Kabir tests a yoga programme on 12 students and gets p = .20; the confidence interval runs from a small harm to a large benefit, so the study cannot tell. Option d is right because it is the only option that rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested. Exam trap: 'retain H0' is easily misread as 'accept H0 as true'. Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no 'both false' option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question for exactly this reason, so no one was marked on it.", "topic": "Interpreting non-significant results"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Factor analysis helps in identifying underlying latent variables from a large set of observed variables. Reason (R): Because it maximizes within-factor variance and minimizes between-factor variance.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, because factor analysis does uncover latent variables from many observed ones, but the Reason misdescribes how it works. Step 1, the Assertion on its own: true. Factor analysis starts from the correlations among many measured variables and asks how few underlying factors could produce them. Charles Spearman (1904) invented it to argue for a general intelligence factor, g, and Louis Thurstone (1931) extended it to several factors, aiming at simple structure, in which each variable loads highly on one factor and near zero on the rest. A factor loading is a variable's correlation with a factor; a factor's eigenvalue is the variance it explains. Factor analysis models the shared (common) variance among variables, so variables that correlate highly end up on the same factor. Step 2, the Reason on its own: false. 'Maximising within-factor variance and minimising between-factor variance' borrows the language of separating groups of cases, as in cluster analysis or ANOVA-style comparisons (and even there the aim runs the other way: small spread within groups, large differences between them). Factor analysis does not sort people into groups. The confusion probably comes from varimax rotation (Henry Kaiser, 1958), which maximises the variance of the squared loadings on each factor to sharpen simple structure; that is a rotation rule, not the reason factors are found. Step 3: since R is false, it cannot explain A. Example: Riya gives a 20-item anxiety scale to 400 students. Racing heart, sweating and trembling correlate with one another and load on a 'somatic' factor, while worry and dread items load on a 'cognitive' factor. Option c is right because A is true and R is false. Exam trap: examiners borrow cluster and ANOVA vocabulary to make a wrong reason sound technical.", "topic": "Factor analysis logic"}, {"q": "The 'Biological Basis of Motivation' (e.g., hunger, thirst) implicitly assumes that:", "options": {"a": "All motivation is learned through environmental conditioning", "b": "Physiological needs and homeostatic mechanisms play a fundamental role in driving certain behaviors", "c": "Cognitive factors are the sole determinants of motivation", "d": "Motivation is entirely independent of bodily states"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: biological theories of motivation assume that physiological needs and homeostatic mechanisms drive certain basic behaviours such as eating and drinking. Claude Bernard (1813 to 1878) described the milieu intérieur, the stable internal environment of the body. Walter Cannon (1926) named the process that keeps it stable homeostasis, and explained it in The Wisdom of the Body (1932): like a thermostat, the body detects a departure from a set point (temperature, blood glucose, fluid balance) and triggers corrections. Clark Hull (1943) built drive-reduction theory on this idea: a tissue need creates a drive, an unpleasant state of arousal that pushes the organism to act, and any behaviour that reduces the drive is reinforced. Primary drives such as hunger and thirst come from such needs; secondary drives, such as learned fear, are acquired. Hunger is regulated by the hypothalamus, which reads signals such as blood glucose, ghrelin from the stomach and leptin from fat cells. Thirst has two triggers: osmotic thirst, when salt concentration rises and osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus respond, and hypovolaemic thirst, when blood volume falls, as after sweating or bleeding. The word 'certain' matters: the biological view does not claim that all motives are biological, and learning, culture and thought still shape what, when and how much we eat. Example: after a cricket match in the Kozhikode heat, Arjun has lost fluid through sweating. Rising salt concentration and falling blood volume trigger thirst; he drinks, the drive falls and drinking is reinforced. Option b is right because it states the homeostatic assumption without overclaiming. Exam trap: the distractors use 'all', 'sole' and 'entirely'; an absolute statement about motivation is almost always wrong.", "topic": "Biological basis of motivation"}, {"q": "When conducting a meta-analysis, a crucial assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "All studies included in the analysis are of equal methodological quality", "b": "The effects observed in individual studies are independent of each other", "c": "There is a true underlying effect size that can be estimated by combining results from multiple studies", "d": "Publication bias does not exist"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: meta-analysis rests on the idea that there is a true underlying effect (or a mean of true effects) that combining many studies can estimate more precisely than any single study. Gene Glass (1976) coined the term meta-analysis for the statistical pooling of results from many studies. Each study's result is converted to a common effect size, such as Cohen's d or a correlation, each is weighted (usually by the inverse of its variance, so larger and more precise studies count more), and a pooled estimate with a confidence interval is computed. Mary Lee Smith and Gene Glass (1977) pooled 375 psychotherapy studies and found an average effect size of about 0.68. The fixed-effect model assumes one true effect size shared by all studies, so they differ only by sampling error. The random-effects model of Rebecca DerSimonian and Nan Laird (1986) relaxes this: true effects vary from study to study, and the analysis estimates the mean of their distribution and the spread between studies. Heterogeneity is checked with Cochran's Q and the I-squared statistic. Either way, the logic assumes there is a meaningful effect to estimate. Example: Meera pools 12 trials of CBT for social anxiety in college students. If all the trials estimate one common effect, a fixed-effect model suits; if populations and formats differ, a random-effects model estimates the average effect and how much it varies. Option c is right because estimating a true underlying effect by combining studies is the premise that defines meta-analysis. Exam trap: publication bias and unequal study quality are known problems that meta-analysts test for, not assumptions they make. Key note: b, independence of the effect sizes (for example, no sample counted twice), is also a real technical assumption of standard meta-analysis; c is the conceptual premise examiners mean, so c stands.", "topic": "Meta-analysis assumptions"}, {"q": "The 'Phi Coefficient' is a measure of association used when:", "options": {"a": "Both variables are continuous", "b": "Both variables are dichotomous", "c": "One variable is continuous and the other is dichotomous", "d": "One variable is ordinal and the other is nominal"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the phi coefficient measures the association between two dichotomous variables, each with only two categories. Phi (φ) is simply the product-moment correlation of Karl Pearson (1896) applied to two variables coded 0 and 1. The data fit a 2 by 2 table with cells a, b, c and d, and φ = (ad minus bc) divided by the square root of the product of the four marginal totals. It is also tied to chi-square: φ equals the square root of chi-square divided by N. Like r, it runs from minus 1 to plus 1 and is read the same way, though its maximum shrinks when the two variables have very different splits. Phi is meant for true (natural) dichotomies, such as alive or dead, smoker or non-smoker. The right coefficient depends on the type of each variable: Pearson's r for two continuous variables; point-biserial for one true dichotomy and one continuous variable; biserial for an artificial dichotomy (pass or fail cut from marks) with a continuous variable; tetrachoric for two artificial dichotomies; and Cramér's V (Harald Cramér, 1946) for larger nominal tables. Example: Kabir records 100 patients as smokers or non-smokers and as relapsed or not relapsed after treatment. Both are natural two-category variables, so he computes phi from the 2 by 2 table. Option b is right because phi is defined for the case where both variables are dichotomous. Exam trap: the tetrachoric coefficient also uses two dichotomies, but artificial ones with an assumed continuous, normal variable underneath; and the point-biserial, with one dichotomous and one continuous variable, is the classic near-miss.", "topic": "Phi coefficient"}, {"q": "'Neuroplasticity' is most evident in which of the following scenarios?", "options": {"a": "The fixed structure of the adult brain", "b": "The brain's ability to recover function after injury or adapt to new experiences", "c": "The complete inability of the brain to form new connections after childhood", "d": "The unchanging nature of neural pathways throughout life"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: neuroplasticity is seen most clearly when the brain recovers function after injury or rewires itself to fit new experience. Neuroplasticity is the brain's ability to change its structure and function throughout life in response to experience, learning and damage. For much of the twentieth century the adult brain was thought to be fixed; Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1852 to 1934) described adult nerve pathways as fixed and unable to regenerate. Evidence overturned this. Donald Hebb (1949) proposed that connections between neurons strengthen when the neurons are active together, and Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) found such long-term potentiation in the hippocampus. Mark Rosenzweig and colleagues in the 1960s showed that rats reared in enriched cages grew a thicker cortex. Eleanor Maguire and colleagues (2000) found that London taxi drivers had a larger posterior hippocampus, related to their years of navigating. Peter Eriksson and colleagues (1998) confirmed that new neurons form in the adult human hippocampus. After injury, neighbouring axons sprout into vacant synapses and nearby cortex takes over lost functions. Plasticity is greatest in childhood critical periods, as David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1963) showed in kittens deprived of sight in one eye, but it never switches off. Example: Arjun, 56, has a stroke that weakens his right arm. In constraint-induced movement therapy (Edward Taub, 1993) his good arm is restrained and the weak arm is trained for hours daily; its area in the motor cortex expands and use returns. Option b is right because recovery and adaptation are the observable signs of plasticity. Exam trap: critical periods mean plasticity is greater early in life, not that it is absent later; options a, c and d all restate the outdated fixed-brain view.", "topic": "Neuroplasticity and recovery"}, {"q": "Which of the following datasets has the highest coefficient of variation (CV)? Dataset A: Mean = 50, Standard Deviation = 5. Dataset B: Mean = 20, Standard Deviation = 8. Dataset C: Mean = 100, Standard Deviation = 10. Dataset D: Mean = 10, Standard Deviation = 2.", "options": {"a": "Dataset A", "b": "Dataset B", "c": "Dataset C", "d": "Dataset D"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: Dataset B has the highest coefficient of variation, 40 percent. The coefficient of variation (CV), introduced by Karl Pearson (1896), expresses the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD / mean) x 100. Because it removes both the units and the size of the mean, it compares relative spread across groups with very different means or units, such as reaction time in milliseconds and body weight in kilograms. It needs ratio-scale data with a true zero. The working: Dataset A = 5 / 50 x 100 = 10 percent. Dataset B = 8 / 20 x 100 = 40 percent. Dataset C = 10 / 100 x 100 = 10 percent. Dataset D = 2 / 10 x 100 = 20 percent. Option b is right because 40 percent is the largest relative spread: B's standard deviation is two fifths of its mean. Exam trap: Dataset C has the largest raw SD (10), which tempts anyone who compares SDs directly, but its mean is also the largest, so its relative spread is only 10 percent.", "topic": "Coefficient of variation"}, {"q": "When conducting 'Ethnography' as a research method, a core assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "The researcher should remain completely detached from the community being studied", "b": "Deep understanding of a culture or social group can be gained through prolonged immersion and observation within their natural setting", "c": "Quantitative data is always superior for understanding cultural phenomena", "d": "The researcher's presence has no impact on the observed behavior"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: ethnography assumes that a culture or group is best understood from the inside, through prolonged immersion and observation in its natural setting. Ethnography means 'writing about a people' and grew from anthropology. Bronislaw Malinowski (1884 to 1942) lived in the Trobriand Islands from 1915 to 1918, learned the language and joined daily life; his Argonauts of the Western Pacific (1922) set the goal of grasping 'the native's point of view'. The main method is participant observation: the researcher lives or works alongside the group for months or years, takes part in everyday life, keeps detailed field notes and talks with key informants. Interviews, documents and objects add to this. Kenneth Pike (1954) contrasted emic categories (insiders' own meanings) with etic ones (an outsider's comparative categories); ethnography starts from the emic. Clifford Geertz (1973) called its aim thick description: recording behaviour together with the context that gives it meaning, so that a wink can be told from a twitch. Ethnographers accept that their presence changes what they see (reactivity), so they stay long enough for people to get used to them and reflect openly on their own role (reflexivity). Example: Riya spends a year in a fishing village near Kozhikode to understand how families talk about alcohol and 'tension'. Only after months do the women tell her things they never say to visiting doctors. Option b is right because prolonged immersion in the natural setting is the defining assumption of the method. Exam trap: complete detachment is the 'complete observer' role in the typology of Raymond Gold (1958), not the ethnographic ideal, and ethnographers never assume their presence has no effect.", "topic": "Ethnography and immersion"}, {"q": "When using 'ANCOVA' (Analysis of Covariance) in research, a key assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "The covariate is highly correlated with the independent variable", "b": "The sample sizes are unequal", "c": "The dependent variable is categorical", "d": "The covariate is linearly related to the dependent variable and is independent of the treatment effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ANCOVA assumes that the covariate is linearly related to the dependent variable and is not itself affected by the treatment. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA), developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), blends ANOVA with regression. It compares group means on a dependent variable (DV) after statistically removing the influence of a continuous covariate, such as a pre-test score. This adjusts the group means for chance differences at baseline and shrinks error variance, so the test of the treatment gains power. Beyond the usual ANOVA assumptions (normality, equal variances, independent observations), ANCOVA adds several of its own. First, the covariate must be linearly related to the DV, because the adjustment is a straight-line regression. Second, homogeneity of regression slopes: the covariate-DV slope must be the same in every group, so there is no covariate by treatment interaction. Third, the covariate must be independent of the treatment: measured before the treatment, or at least not changed by it; otherwise adjusting for it removes part of the real treatment effect. The covariate should also be measured reliably. Example: Kabir compares three ways of teaching statistics to postgraduate students. Because they start with different maths ability, he uses a pre-course maths score as the covariate. It predicts the final exam score in a straight line, the slope is similar in all three groups, and it was measured before teaching began, so ANCOVA suits his data. Option d is right because linearity with the DV and independence from the treatment effect are core ANCOVA assumptions. Exam trap: option a reverses the logic; the covariate should correlate with the DV, not with the independent variable, because a covariate tied to the treatment confounds the comparison.", "topic": "ANCOVA assumptions"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a 'Non-parametric test' used for comparing more than two independent groups when the data is ordinal?", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "Kruskal-Wallis H test", "c": "Mann-Whitney U test", "d": "Wilcoxon signed rank test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Kruskal-Wallis H test is the non-parametric test for comparing three or more independent groups on ordinal data. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) designed it as a one-way ANOVA by ranks. All scores from all groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest, and the ranks are summed for each group. If the groups come from the same population, their mean ranks should be similar; the H statistic grows as the mean ranks pull apart. With reasonable group sizes, H is read against the chi-square distribution with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. Because it works on ranks, it needs no assumption of normality and suits ordinal data such as ratings or badly skewed scores, though it still assumes independent observations, and similarly shaped distributions if the result is to be read as a difference in medians. A significant H says only that at least one group differs; follow-up pairwise comparisons, such as Dunn's test (Olive Jean Dunn, 1964), show which. Example: Meera compares nurses' ratings of patient agitation (1 = calm to 5 = very agitated) across three wards. The ratings are ordinal and each ward holds different patients, so she uses the Kruskal-Wallis H test. Option b is right because it is the rank-based test built for more than two independent groups. Exam trap: match the test to two questions, how many groups and whether they are independent or related: Mann-Whitney U covers two independent groups, Wilcoxon signed-rank two related samples, Friedman three or more related samples, and ANOVA is the parametric version.", "topic": "Kruskal-Wallis H test"}, {"q": "When conducting 'Case Studies' in research, a primary assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "The findings are directly generalizable to the entire population", "b": "In-depth investigation of a single individual, group, or event can provide rich, detailed insights that may not be captured by other methods", "c": "Quantitative data is always preferred over qualitative data", "d": "The researcher's objectivity is not important"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a case study assumes that in-depth investigation of one individual, group or event can yield rich, detailed insights that other methods would miss. A case study is an intensive, usually long-term study of a single case, drawing on several sources: interviews, observation, records, tests and reports from others. Gordon Allport (1937) called this the idiographic approach, the study of the unique individual, as opposed to the nomothetic search for general laws across many people. Case studies have shaped psychology far beyond their size. Sigmund Freud's (1909) account of Little Hans fed his theory of the Oedipus complex. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) described patient H.M., whose severe amnesia after removal of both medial temporal lobes showed that these structures are needed to form new long-term memories. Phineas Gage's change in personality after his 1848 injury pointed to the role of the frontal lobes. Case studies suit rare conditions, new hypotheses and the detailed study of process. Their weaknesses are just as clear: no control, possible researcher bias, reliance on memory and limited generalisation. Robert Yin (1984) argued that a case generalises to theory (analytic generalisation), not to a population (statistical generalisation). Example: Arjun, a clinical psychology trainee, writes a case study of a 9-year-old girl with selective mutism, combining parent interviews, school reports, play observation and test results to trace how her silence developed. Option b is right because depth and detail on one case is the assumption that justifies the method. Exam trap: generalisability is the classic weakness of case studies, so option a turns their main limitation into an assumption.", "topic": "Case study method"}, {"q": "When interpreting the results of a 'Cross-Cultural Study' on psychological phenomena, a crucial assumption for avoiding ethnocentrism is that:", "options": {"a": "Psychological constructs are universally defined and experienced in the same way across all cultures", "b": "Cultural context significantly shapes the expression and meaning of psychological phenomena, and researchers must be sensitive to these differences", "c": "Western psychological theories are universally applicable", "d": "Cultural differences are merely superficial"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: avoiding ethnocentrism in cross-cultural research rests on the assumption that culture shapes how psychological phenomena are expressed and what they mean, so researchers must stay sensitive to those differences. Ethnocentrism, a term William Graham Sumner (1906) defined in Folkways, means judging other groups by the standards of one's own. In research it appears when a theory, test or category built in one culture is applied elsewhere as if it were universal. Kenneth Pike (1954) contrasted emic concepts (meanings inside a culture) with etic ones (categories used to compare cultures). John Berry (1969) warned against an 'imposed etic': taking a Western concept and treating it as a universal yardstick. Joseph Henrich, Steven Heine and Ara Norenzayan (2010) showed that most psychology samples are WEIRD (Western, educated, industrialised, rich, democratic) and often unusual by world standards. Indian work makes the point: Joan Miller (1984) found that American adults explained behaviour mainly by personality, while Hindu adults in Mysore gave more situational explanations, and the gap grew with age, so attribution style is learned from culture. Good cross-cultural studies therefore check construct, linguistic and measurement equivalence and use careful back-translation. Example: Meera adapts a Western depression scale for women in rural Kerala. Focus groups show that distress is often voiced as 'tension', tiredness or a sinking heart, so she adds these local idioms before comparing scores. Option b is right because cultural sensitivity is the safeguard against judging all cultures by one culture's norms. Exam trap: universalism in cross-cultural psychology means shared basic processes with culturally varied expression; it does not mean identical experience everywhere, which is the absolutist assumption in option a.", "topic": "Cross-cultural research and ethnocentrism"}, {"q": "The 'Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)' measures intelligence through:", "options": {"a": "A single overall score", "b": "Creativity and divergent thinking", "c": "Emotional intelligence only", "d": "Verbal and Performance (or Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory, Processing Speed) subscales"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale measures intelligence through part scores: Verbal and Performance scales in its earlier editions and, from the WAIS-IV, four index scores (Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed). David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale because the Stanford-Binet suited children and leaned heavily on verbal tasks. He added a Performance scale of non-verbal tasks beside the Verbal scale and replaced the mental-age ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15 within each age group). The WAIS (1955), WAIS-R (1981) and WAIS-III (1997) gave a Verbal IQ, a Performance IQ and a Full Scale IQ. The WAIS-IV (2008; ages 16 to 90) dropped the Verbal and Performance IQs and reports a Full Scale IQ from 10 core subtests plus four indexes: Verbal Comprehension (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information), Perceptual Reasoning (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), Working Memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic) and Processing Speed (Symbol Search, Coding). Wechsler defined intelligence as a global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment, yet he measured it through many different tasks because a profile tells more than one number. Example: after a road accident, Kabir, 30, scores 108 on Verbal Comprehension but 78 on Processing Speed. His Full Scale IQ alone would hide the slowed processing that matters for his rehabilitation. Option d is right because it names both the classic Verbal and Performance structure and the modern index scores. Exam trap: the Verbal and Performance IQs belong to the WAIS up to the WAIS-III, and 'Perceptual Organisation' was the WAIS-III index name; the WAIS-IV term is Perceptual Reasoning.", "topic": "WAIS structure"}, {"q": "When using 'Repeated Measures Design' in experimental research, a key assumption is that:", "options": {"a": "The order in which treatments are presented does not affect the outcome (or order effects are counterbalanced)", "b": "Participants are randomly assigned to different treatment groups", "c": "Each participant receives only one treatment condition", "d": "The measurements taken at different time points are independent"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a repeated measures design assumes that the order of treatments does not distort the results, or that order effects are controlled by counterbalancing. In a repeated measures (within-subjects) design every participant receives every condition and serves as their own control. Stable individual differences in ability or personality drop out of the error term, so fewer participants are needed and the test is more powerful. The price is order effects. Practice effects make later conditions look better, fatigue or boredom effects make them look worse, and carry-over effects occur when one condition leaves something behind, such as a drug still acting or a strategy still in use. If order is confounded with condition, any difference could be due to order rather than treatment. Researchers therefore counterbalance: half the participants do A then B and half B then A (or ABBA within each person), and with several conditions a Latin square places each condition once in every position. Where carry-over is severe, a washout period or a between-subjects design is used instead. Statistically, scores from the same person are correlated, and the analysis must respect this; repeated measures ANOVA also assumes sphericity, checked by John Mauchly's (1940) test and corrected by the adjustment of Samuel Greenhouse and Seymour Geisser (1959). Example: Riya tests recall of word lists in silence, with music and with traffic noise. Each of her 24 students does all three, with orders rotated by a Latin square, so no condition always comes first. Option a is right because the design is valid only if order effects are absent or balanced. Exam trap: random assignment to separate groups and one condition per person describe between-subjects designs, and 'independent measurements' is the opposite of what repeated measures produce.", "topic": "Repeated measures design"}, {"q": "'Observational Learning' (Bandura) involves which of the following processes?", "options": {"a": "Attention, Retention, Reproduction, Motivation", "b": "Encoding, Storage, Retrieval", "c": "Stimulus, Response, Consequence", "d": "Id, Ego, Superego"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Albert Bandura's observational learning works through four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) showed that people learn by watching others, called models, without being reinforced themselves. In the Bobo doll study of Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the model's specific actions and words, while children who saw a calm model did not. In a 1965 follow-up, children who saw the model punished imitated less, until they were offered a reward for showing what the model had done, when they reproduced it as well as the others. This separated learning from performance. The four processes are attention (noticing the model; we attend more to models who are similar, competent or high in status), retention (storing the behaviour as images and verbal codes), motor reproduction (being physically able to turn the memory into action, improving with practice and feedback) and motivation (a reason to perform, from external, vicarious or self-reinforcement). The first three govern learning; the fourth governs whether the behaviour is shown. Bandura later widened this into social cognitive theory (1986). Example: Kabir watches his elder sister roll chapatis. He watches her hands closely (attention), remembers 'press from the centre, turn a quarter' (retention), tries it with lumpy results at first (reproduction) and keeps practising because his mother praises him (motivation). Option a is right because these are Bandura's four named processes, in his order. Exam trap: 'Encoding, Storage, Retrieval' sounds close because retention involves memory, but it is the three-stage model of memory, not Bandura's model of modelling.", "topic": "Bandura observational learning"}, {"q": "Match the following. a. Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test, b. Kruskal-Wallis Test, c. Eta-squared, d. Friedman Test. 1. Non-parametric equivalent of Repeated Measures ANOVA, 2. Effect size in ANOVA, 3. Non-parametric equivalent of One-way ANOVA, 4. Non-parametric test for two related samples", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1", "b": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1", "c": "a-1, b-4, c-2, d-3", "d": "a-4, b-1, c-3, d-2"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the correct matches are Wilcoxon signed-rank with two related samples (a-4), Kruskal-Wallis with one-way ANOVA (b-3), eta-squared with effect size in ANOVA (c-2) and Friedman with repeated measures ANOVA (d-1). Non-parametric tests replace raw scores with ranks, so they need no normality assumption and suit ordinal or skewed data. Each has a parametric partner. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) is for two related samples, such as the same patients before and after therapy. It ranks the size of each person's difference and reattaches the signs, so it uses both direction and magnitude; it is the rank-based partner of the paired t-test. The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) pools and ranks the scores of three or more independent groups and compares their mean ranks, the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA. The Friedman test, devised by the economist Milton Friedman (1937), ranks each person's scores across three or more conditions and compares the rank totals, the non-parametric equivalent of repeated measures ANOVA. Eta-squared is not a test at all: it is an effect size for ANOVA, the proportion of total variance explained by the factor (sum of squares between groups divided by the total sum of squares). Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested about .01 as small, .06 as medium and .14 as large. Example: Riya rates anxiety in the same 15 patients at weeks 0, 4 and 8 on an ordinal scale, so she uses the Friedman test. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairs correct. Exam trap: a-4 appears in both a and d, so decide on b: Kruskal-Wallis is the independent-groups (one-way) equivalent, which rules out d, where it is wrongly matched to repeated measures.", "topic": "Non-parametric tests matching"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"imhans-2025": [{"q": "When a counsellor uses paraphrasing as an active listening skill, the primary goal is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: in paraphrasing, the counsellor restates the essence of what the client said in the counsellor's own, usually fewer, words, so the client can confirm or correct it and feel heard. Paraphrasing is one of the basic listening microskills taught by Allen Ivey (1968) in microcounselling, alongside open questions, minimal encouragers, reflection of feeling and summarising. It grows out of the person-centred tradition of Carl Rogers (1951), where empathy means understanding the client's world from the inside and showing that you have understood it. A good paraphrase does three jobs: it checks the counsellor's understanding, it communicates attention and empathy, and it hands the client a clearer mirror of their own message. It stays inside the client's frame of reference and adds nothing new. Example: Meera says, \"My manager keeps loading me with work and then calls me slow.\" The counsellor replies, \"So you are given more than anyone could finish, and then blamed for not finishing it.\" Meera can answer \"Yes, exactly\" or \"Not quite, it is more that...\", and either way the counsellor learns something. Paraphrasing targets content (the facts and ideas). Reflection of feeling targets the emotion (\"You sound exhausted and unfairly treated\"). Summarising pulls together a longer stretch of talk. Option c is right because it names both purposes, checking understanding and conveying empathy, and the defining feature, the counsellor's own words. Exam trap: echoing the client's exact words is parroting. Repeating a key word or two can work as a minimal encourager, but a full verbatim repeat sounds mechanical and proves nothing about understanding. Offering a hidden meaning is interpretation, an influencing skill that moves into the counsellor's frame of reference.", "traps": {"a": "Bringing out a hidden meaning is interpretation, an influencing skill rooted in psychodynamic therapy that offers a new frame from the counsellor's theory; a paraphrase adds no meaning the client did not express.", "b": "Repeating the exact words is parroting (or, for a word or two, a minimal encourager); it sounds mechanical and does not show that the counsellor has processed the message, which is the whole point of paraphrasing.", "d": "Giving advice is a directive influencing response that solves the problem for the client; it is not a listening skill at all, and offered early it shuts down the client's own exploration."}}, {"q": "Which test is most sensitive for detecting subtle frontal lobe executive dysfunction in early neurodegenerative conditions?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the classic, most sensitive test for subtle frontal executive dysfunction, especially loss of set shifting and abstract rule learning. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) devised it, and Robert Heaton (1981) wrote the clinical manual. The person sorts response cards under four key cards that differ in colour, form and number. The examiner never states the rule and says only \"right\" or \"wrong\". After a run of correct sorts (ten in the standard version) the rule changes without warning. To succeed, the person must work out a concept from feedback, keep it in mind, notice that it has stopped working and shift to a new one. The main scores are categories completed, perseverative errors (sticking to the old rule despite hearing \"wrong\") and failures to maintain set. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral prefrontal excisions made far more perseverative errors than patients with posterior excisions, which tied the test to the frontal lobes. Simple screens can look normal early in frontotemporal dementia, Parkinson's disease or Huntington's disease, while the WCST already shows rigidity. It is sensitive rather than perfectly specific: diffuse or non-frontal damage can also lower scores. Example: Arjun, 58, passes a memory screen, but on the WCST he keeps sorting by colour long after the rule has moved to form, even while saying he is being told \"wrong\". Option a is right because the WCST directly taxes cognitive flexibility, concept formation and use of feedback, the functions that slip first in frontal disease. Exam trap: the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure has a planning and organisation element, so it tempts, but it is mainly a test of visuoconstruction and visual memory, not of shifting set.", "traps": {"b": "The Benton Visual Retention Test (Arthur Benton, first described 1945) has the person copy or draw designs from memory; it measures visual memory, visual perception and visuoconstruction, not set shifting.", "c": "The Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure (André Rey, 1941; Paul-Alexandre Osterrieth, 1944) is a copy and delayed recall task for visuoconstruction and visual memory; its organisation score tempts, but it is not the standard probe of executive flexibility.", "d": "The Finger Tapping Test, part of the Halstead-Reitan battery, measures simple motor speed of each hand and helps lateralise motor damage; it says nothing about abstract reasoning or set shifting."}}, {"q": "DSM-5-TR introduced expanded guidance on cultural formulation. Which tool is emphasized for systematically assessing cultural context in clinical evaluation?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Cultural Formulation Interview (CFI) is the DSM tool for systematically assessing how culture shapes a person's problem, explanations, supports and help-seeking. A cultural formulation asks how a person's background affects what they see as the problem, what they think caused it, what stresses and supports surround them, and how they relate to the clinician. DSM-IV (1994) gave only a narrative Outline for Cultural Formulation. DSM-5 (2013) turned this into a structured, semi-structured interview of 16 questions in Section III, with an informant version and supplementary modules (for example on explanatory models, spirituality, older adults, and immigrants and refugees). DSM-5-TR (2022) kept and updated it. The 16 questions fall into four domains: the cultural definition of the problem; cultural perceptions of cause, context and support; cultural factors affecting self-coping and past help-seeking; and cultural factors affecting current help-seeking. The questions are open and use the person's own words rather than a checklist of syndromes. Example: Kabir, 22, from a village in Bihar, complains of weakness and worry about losing semen in his urine. Asked \"What do you think is causing this?\", he describes dhat. The clinician now understands his explanatory model and can frame anxiety treatment in a way Kabir accepts. Option a is right because the CFI is the only one of these that is a real DSM instrument, and it is built exactly for systematic cultural assessment. Exam trap: the stem says DSM-5-TR \"introduced\" the tool, but the CFI first appeared in DSM-5 (2013), and the Outline for Cultural Formulation goes back to DSM-IV (1994). The \"Global Functioning Scale\" is a near-miss for the GAF, Axis V of DSM-IV, which DSM-5 dropped.", "traps": {"b": "There is no DSM instrument called the Cross-Cultural Checklist; the name is built to sound plausible. The DSM approach is an open interview in the person's own words, not a checklist.", "c": "Multicultural Assessment Battery is not a DSM instrument either; DSM cultural assessment is done by interview, not by a battery of tests.", "d": "This is a near-miss for the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), the 0 to 100 rating on Axis V of DSM-IV; it rated overall functioning, not culture, and DSM-5 dropped it, recommending the WHODAS 2.0 instead."}}, {"q": "The 'Crisis in psychology due to strict adherence to experimental-analytical paradigm' primarily refers to the limitations of which epistemological stance?", "concept": "The answer is b: the \"crisis\" was a critique of positivism, the stance that psychology should copy the natural sciences and accept only what can be observed, measured and tested. Positivism comes from Auguste Comte (1830s), who held that real knowledge is \"positive\", that is, built on observation and experiment, and from the logical positivists of the Vienna Circle (1920s), for whom a statement has meaning only if observation could verify it. In psychology this became the experimental-analytical paradigm: break behaviour into variables, manipulate one, measure another, control the rest, quantify everything and look for universal cause and effect laws, with a detached, value-free researcher. From the late 1960s many writers spoke of a crisis, above all in social psychology. Rom Harré and Paul Secord (1972) argued that people are agents who act for reasons and meanings, not objects pushed about by variables. Kenneth Gergen (1973) argued that social psychology is really history, because its \"laws\" shift as culture shifts. Critics added that laboratory experiments were artificial, stripped away context and power, and treated participants as passive subjects. Example: Riya studies loneliness in widows only with a rating scale and a reaction time task in the lab. She gets clean, comparable numbers but learns nothing of what being alone means to each woman, which open interviews would have shown. Option b is right because the experimental-analytical paradigm is positivism in practice, and the crisis literature attacked exactly its assumptions of objectivity, measurability and universal laws. Exam trap: social constructionism, existential phenomenology and co-operative inquiry are not the targets of the crisis; they are alternatives that grew out of it. Do not pick an option just because it sounds like a critical school.", "traps": {"a": "Social constructionism (Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann, 1966; Kenneth Gergen, 1985 in psychology) holds that knowledge and categories are built through language and social interaction; it is one of the critiques of positivism, not the stance being criticised.", "c": "Existential phenomenology, from Edmund Husserl and applied to psychology by Amedeo Giorgi (1970), studies lived experience and meaning; it was offered as a human-science alternative to the experimental paradigm.", "d": "Co-operative inquiry, developed by John Heron and Peter Reason from the 1970s, is a participatory method in which participants are co-researchers; it is a response to the crisis, not the stance under attack."}}, {"q": "In advanced statistical analysis, 'MANOVA' (Multivariate Analysis of Variance) is preferred over multiple ANOVAs when:", "concept": "The answer is c: MANOVA is preferred when there are two or more dependent variables (DVs) that are correlated, so it makes sense to analyse them together. Multivariate analysis of variance extends ANOVA. Instead of comparing group means on one DV, it builds a weighted combination of the DVs and asks whether the groups differ on that combination. Running a separate ANOVA for each DV has two problems. First, each test carries its own 5% risk, so the familywise Type I error grows: with three DVs tested at .05, the chance of at least one false positive is roughly 1 minus 0.95 cubed, about 14%. Second, separate ANOVAs ignore how the DVs relate, while MANOVA can detect a group difference that lies in the pattern of DVs even when no single DV differs much. The usual test statistics are Wilks' lambda (Samuel Wilks, 1932), Pillai's trace (the most robust), Hotelling's trace and Roy's largest root. If the overall test is significant, follow-up ANOVAs or a discriminant analysis show where the difference lies. Example: Kabir compares CBT, yoga and a waiting list on anxiety, depression and stress scores. These three scores correlate, so one MANOVA is better than three ANOVAs. Option c is right because several correlated DVs are exactly the situation MANOVA was built for. Exam trap: option b describes a factorial ANOVA. \"Multivariate\" refers to several dependent variables, not several independent variables. Also, MANOVA works best when DVs are moderately correlated; if two DVs correlate almost perfectly, one is redundant and the analysis becomes unstable.", "traps": {"a": "With only one DV there is nothing multivariate to analyse; that is an ordinary one-way or factorial ANOVA, or a t-test for two groups.", "b": "Several independent variables with one DV is a factorial ANOVA (for example a 2 x 3 design); MANOVA is defined by multiple DVs, not multiple IVs.", "d": "MANOVA needs more data, not less: each cell should have more cases than there are DVs, and its assumptions (multivariate normality, equal covariance matrices) are harder to meet in small samples."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'Effect Size' in research is crucial because:", "concept": "The answer is d: effect size tells you how large, and so how practically important, an observed effect is, which a p-value cannot tell you. A significance test asks only whether an effect is probably not zero. Its p-value depends on two things at once: the size of the effect and the size of the sample. With a huge sample, even a trivial difference becomes \"significant\"; with a tiny sample, a large and useful difference can miss p < .05. Effect size separates the magnitude from the sample size. Jacob Cohen (1988) set the familiar benchmarks: for Cohen's d (the difference between two means divided by the pooled standard deviation) 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large; for the correlation r, .10, .30 and .50; for eta squared in ANOVA, .01, .06 and .14. Other effect sizes include the odds ratio and the number needed to treat. The American Psychological Association now expects effect sizes, ideally with confidence intervals, to be reported alongside p-values, and meta-analysis is built entirely on them. Example: Arjun tests a study app on 4,000 students. The app group scores half a mark higher out of 100, p < .001. The effect is real but d is tiny, so no school should spend money on it. Meera's pilot of 20 students finds a large d of 0.8 with p = .08; it deserves a bigger trial, not the bin. Option d is right because effect size is defined as a standardised index of the magnitude of an effect, the basis for judging practical or clinical importance. Exam trap: effect size does not decide significance (that is the p-value against alpha). It is used before a study, in power analysis, to plan sample size, but it is not a prerequisite for running a t-test.", "traps": {"a": "Statistical significance is decided by the p-value compared with alpha, which depends on sample size as well as effect size; a tiny effect can be significant and a large one non-significant.", "b": "A t-test can be run without any effect size; the near-miss is that an expected effect size is needed for an a priori power analysis to plan sample size, not to run the test itself.", "c": "Type I error is controlled by the alpha level and by corrections for multiple tests such as the Bonferroni correction, not by the effect size."}}, {"q": "The 'Humanistic' concept of 'Unconditional Positive Regard' in therapy assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is b: unconditional positive regard means the therapist accepts and values the client as a person, without judgement and without conditions, and this acceptance is held to be essential for growth. Carl Rogers (1957) named three therapist core conditions as necessary and sufficient for change: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm, non-possessive acceptance or \"prizing\") and accurate empathic understanding. Rogers credited the term itself to his student Stanley Standal (1954). The logic comes from Rogers's theory of the self. Children who receive only conditional regard (\"I love you when you are good\") absorb conditions of worth. They then deny or distort experiences that clash with those conditions, and a gap opens between the self-concept and actual experience, which Rogers called incongruence. In therapy, regard that does not depend on what the client says or does lets the client stop defending, admit denied feelings into awareness, and let the actualising tendency resume. Accepting the person is not approving every act. The therapist can privately disagree with a behaviour while still valuing the human being who did it. Example: Kabir, 19, admits in his third session that he has been stealing money from his mother's purse. The counsellor neither praises nor condemns him. She conveys, \"I still value you and want to understand what is happening for you.\" Feeling safe, Kabir starts to talk about the shame and anger he has been hiding. Option b is right because it captures both parts of the concept: complete, non-judgemental acceptance and its role in therapeutic growth. Exam trap: \"unconditional\" is about the person's worth, not about agreeing with behaviour, so option a is the classic over-reading. Option c is the conditional regard that, in Rogers's theory, causes the problem.", "traps": {"a": "This confuses acceptance of the person with approval of behaviour; Rogers's therapist values the client unconditionally but need not endorse every action, and pretending to would break congruence.", "c": "Regard that must be earned is conditional regard, the source of conditions of worth and incongruence in Rogers's theory; it is the opposite of unconditional positive regard.", "d": "Confronting negative traits belongs to directive or confrontational approaches, such as the disputing of beliefs in Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy; person-centred therapy is non-directive and accepting."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The diathesis-stress model explains the onset of schizophrenia as an interaction between genetic predisposition and environmental triggers. Reason (R): Because schizophrenia develops only in individuals who have no genetic vulnerability but are exposed to extreme stressors.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because the diathesis-stress model needs a genetic (or other biological) vulnerability to be present, not absent. Step 1, the Assertion: true. The diathesis-stress model, set out for schizophrenia by Paul Meehl (1962) and developed as the vulnerability-stress model by Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977), says that illness appears when a pre-existing vulnerability (the diathesis) meets enough stress. Neither is usually sufficient on its own. The diathesis can be genes, obstetric complications or early brain development; the stress can be cannabis use, migration, childhood adversity, a hostile family climate or major life events. The evidence fits: the lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population but nearly 50% for the identical co-twin of a patient, as Irving Gottesman (1991) summarised. That high figure shows genes matter; the fact that it is not 100% shows that environment matters too. Step 2, the Reason: false. It says schizophrenia develops only in people who have no genetic vulnerability. That reverses the model. Without a diathesis, even extreme stress usually does not produce schizophrenia. Step 3: since R is false, the question of whether R explains A does not arise. Example: Arjun's mother has schizophrenia, so he carries raised genetic risk. He stays well through school, but after heavy cannabis use and a stressful move to a hostel, he develops psychosis at 20. His roommate Kabir, with no family history, goes through the same stresses without becoming psychotic. Option c is right because A describes the model correctly and R contradicts it. Exam trap: in Assertion-Reason items, examiners often plant an extreme word such as \"only\", \"never\" or \"always\" in R. Here \"only\" plus \"no genetic vulnerability\" turns an interaction model into a pure stress model.", "traps": {"a": "This would need R to be true; but R claims schizophrenia arises only without genetic vulnerability, which contradicts the diathesis-stress model that A correctly describes.", "b": "This also requires R to be true; R is false, because the model requires a vulnerability to be present before stress can trigger illness.", "d": "A is not false: an interaction of genetic predisposition and environmental triggers is exactly what the diathesis-stress model proposes; and R, not A, is the false statement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best explains the role of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) in the regulation of sleep?", "concept": "The answer is b: the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) switches on sleep by sending inhibitory GABA (and galanin) projections that silence the brain's arousal centres. Wakefulness is kept up by several ascending arousal systems: noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus, serotonin from the raphe nuclei, acetylcholine from the pons and basal forebrain, and orexin (hypocretin) from the lateral hypothalamus. The VLPO, a cluster of sleep-active neurons in the anterior hypothalamus, fires mainly during sleep and inhibits these systems. The arousal systems in turn inhibit the VLPO. Clifford Saper, Thomas Chou and Thomas Scammell (2001) called this mutual inhibition a \"flip-flop switch\": the brain is pushed firmly into either waking or sleep, with few in-between states, and orexin acts like a finger that holds the switch in the wake position. Adenosine, which builds up the longer we stay awake, is one signal that excites the VLPO and drives sleep pressure. The idea of an anterior hypothalamic sleep centre goes back to Constantin von Economo (1930), who saw that patients with encephalitis lethargica and damage to the anterior hypothalamus had severe insomnia. Example: Riya takes an old cough syrup containing an antihistamine and soon feels heavy and sleepy. The drug blocks histamine, the very arousal signal the VLPO turns down each night. Option b is right because the VLPO's defining job is GABAergic inhibition of the arousal-promoting regions. Exam trap: the VLPO is not the clock. The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is the master circadian pacemaker that receives light from the retina; the VLPO is the switch that the clock and sleep pressure act on. REM itself is generated in the pons.", "traps": {"a": "REM sleep is generated by pontine circuits, including cholinergic neurons of the laterodorsal and pedunculopontine tegmental nuclei; the VLPO's main role is non-REM sleep onset by inhibition, not direct excitation of these neurons.", "c": "The master circadian pacemaker is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus, whose cells keep a roughly 24-hour rhythm even in a dish; the VLPO receives clock signals but does not generate them.", "d": "Light reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract from melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells; integrating light to entrain the cycle is the SCN's job, not the VLPO's."}}, {"q": "The somatic marker hypothesis (proposed by Antonio Damasio) suggests that decision-making under emotional conditions depends primarily on:", "concept": "The answer is d: Antonio Damasio's (1994) somatic marker hypothesis gives the central role to the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), which links bodily emotional states to the options being weighed. When we face a choice, past outcomes have left traces in the body: a knot in the stomach, a quickened heart, a sense of ease. Damasio called these somatic markers. The vmPFC (including the orbitofrontal cortex) learns which bodily state went with which option and reactivates it when that option comes up again, as a \"gut feeling\". This narrows the field before slow, deliberate reasoning begins, so decisions under uncertainty become faster and wiser. The evidence came from patients. Paul Eslinger and Antonio Damasio (1985) described patient E.V.R., who kept a superior IQ after removal of an orbitofrontal tumour yet made ruinous personal and financial decisions. Antoine Bechara and colleagues (1994) designed the Iowa Gambling Task: players draw from four decks, two giving big wins but bigger losses, two giving small wins and smaller losses. Healthy players drift to the safe decks and show anticipatory skin conductance responses before picking a risky one. Patients with vmPFC damage keep choosing the risky decks and show no such warning response. Phineas Gage (1848) is the historical forerunner. Example: Arjun, after a road accident damaging his vmPFC, scores normally on intelligence tests and can explain the risks of a loan, yet keeps signing guarantees for unreliable friends. His knowledge is intact; the bodily signal that should stop him is missing. Option d is right because the hypothesis is, at its core, a theory of vmPFC function in linking body states with cognitive evaluation. Exam trap: the amygdala, insula and somatosensory cortex are part of the network (they trigger and map body states), so they tempt, but the integrating hub of the hypothesis is the vmPFC.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala's role in fear conditioning (studied by Joseph LeDoux (1996)) is a different topic; in Damasio's scheme the amygdala triggers emotional body states, but it does not link them to choices among options.", "b": "The hypothalamus organises autonomic and endocrine responses, part of the body loop, but it does no cognitive evaluation of options; that integration is credited to the vmPFC.", "c": "The insula maps interoceptive signals from the viscera and is part of the network that represents body states, so it is a near-miss; the hypothesis places the decision-guiding link between body state and option in the vmPFC."}}, {"q": "A newly developed psychological test shows high internal consistency (e.g., high Cronbach's Alpha) but consistently fails to predict real-world behavior it is designed to measure. This suggests a problem with:", "concept": "The answer is c: a test that is internally consistent but does not predict the behaviour it is meant to measure has a validity problem. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same, stable result? Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is one form of reliability, internal consistency, which shows how strongly the items hang together, as if they measure one common thing. Validity is accuracy of meaning: does the test measure what it claims, and do its scores support the decisions we make with them? Predicting a real-world criterion, now or in the future, is criterion-related validity (concurrent or predictive), one strand of the unified idea of construct validity set out by Samuel Messick (1989). The two are linked in one direction only. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. An unreliable test cannot be valid, because its scores are mostly noise. But a highly reliable test can still measure the wrong thing very consistently. A bathroom scale that reads 2 kg heavy every single time is reliable but not valid. Example: Riya builds a 30-item \"sales aptitude\" scale. Alpha is .92, so the items clearly agree with each other. Yet scores do not correlate with the actual sales figures of new recruits after six months. The items may be tapping general confidence or social desirability rather than selling skill. Her test needs better construct and predictive validity, not more reliability. Option c is right because failure to predict the criterion is, by definition, a failure of criterion-related validity, whatever the alpha. Exam trap: a high alpha tempts students to say the test is \"good\". Alpha tells you only that items are consistent with each other; a very high alpha can even signal redundant, near-identical items that cover the construct too narrowly.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability is the part that is working here: a high Cronbach's alpha shows good internal consistency. The test measures something consistently; it just is not the right thing.", "b": "Norms are the scores of a reference group used to interpret an individual's score (percentiles, standard scores); out-of-date or wrong norms distort interpretation but do not stop a test from predicting a criterion.", "d": "Standardisation means uniform administration, scoring and norming procedures; poor standardisation mainly adds error and lowers reliability, which is not the problem in this stem."}}, {"q": "Damage to the 'Broca's Area' of the brain is typically associated with difficulties in:", "concept": "The answer is d: damage to Broca's area typically produces difficulty with language production, that is, slow, effortful, non-fluent speech. Broca's area lies in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the motor cortex region that controls the face, tongue and larynx. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Victor Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because that was almost the only syllable he could produce, although he understood much of what was said to him. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe, the first strong evidence that language is localised and lateralised. Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia shows halting, telegraphic speech with content words but few grammatical words or endings (agrammatism), poor repetition, word-finding pauses, and often a right-sided weakness because the nearby motor cortex is also damaged. Comprehension of everyday conversation is relatively preserved, and patients know their speech is wrong, so they are often frustrated. Contrast Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874) in the left posterior superior temporal gyrus: damage there gives fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension. Example: Kabir, 62, has a left frontal stroke. Asked about his weekend he says, \"Sunday... walk... dog... park,\" and shakes his head in frustration. When his daughter asks him to point to the window, he does so at once. Option d is right because the defining deficit after Broca's area damage is in producing speech, not in hearing or seeing. Exam trap: Broca's patients are not perfectly normal in comprehension; they struggle with grammatically complex sentences such as \"The dog was chased by the cat.\" Even so, the textbook hallmark of Broca's aphasia is production, while comprehension is the hallmark of Wernicke's.", "traps": {"a": "Impaired language comprehension is the hallmark of Wernicke's aphasia, after damage to Wernicke's area in the left posterior superior temporal gyrus (Carl Wernicke, 1874), not of Broca's area damage.", "b": "Basic auditory processing depends on the primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus) in the temporal lobe; Broca's area is in the frontal lobe and hearing is intact after damage there.", "c": "Visual processing is handled by the occipital lobe (primary visual cortex, area 17) and the visual streams that run from it; it is not affected by a left inferior frontal lesion."}}, {"q": "The neurophysiological method which allows for the direct measurement of single neuron activity in living animals:", "concept": "The answer is c: microelectrode studies, also called single-unit recording, directly measure the electrical activity of individual neurons in living animals. A microelectrode is a very fine wire or glass pipette with a tip of a few micrometres. Lowered into the brain of an anaesthetised or freely moving animal, an extracellular microelectrode picks up the action potentials (\"spikes\") of one or a few neurons near its tip. An intracellular microelectrode, inserted into a single cell, records its membrane potential, including small postsynaptic potentials. Because the recording is taken from the neuron itself, with millisecond timing, this is the most direct way to link the firing of a single cell to a stimulus or a behaviour. Classic discoveries came this way. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959) recorded single cells in the cat's visual cortex and found neurons tuned to lines of a particular orientation, work that won a Nobel Prize in 1981. John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one part of its environment. Example: Meera, a neuroscience student, implants a microelectrode in a rat's hippocampus. Each time the rat runs into one corner of the box, the speaker connected to the amplifier crackles with spikes; elsewhere it is quiet. She has found a single place cell. Option c is right because only microelectrode recording measures single-neuron activity directly; the other three methods measure the summed or indirect activity of millions of cells. Exam trap: EEG also records electrical activity, so it tempts, but scalp electrodes pick up the summed postsynaptic potentials of large populations of cortical neurons, never one cell. fMRI and PET are even more indirect, tracking blood oxygen, blood flow or metabolism.", "traps": {"a": "The EEG (Hans Berger, 1929) records the summed postsynaptic activity of millions of cortical neurons through scalp electrodes; it has excellent timing but cannot isolate a single neuron.", "b": "fMRI measures the blood oxygen level dependent (BOLD) signal, a slow haemodynamic response to the activity of large groups of neurons; it is indirect and resolves millimetres and seconds, not single cells.", "d": "PET tracks a radioactive tracer (for example labelled glucose or a receptor ligand) to show metabolism, blood flow or receptor binding over regions; it is indirect and far too coarse for single neurons."}}, {"q": "In 'Instrumental Learning', 'Partial Reinforcement Schedules' lead to behavior that is:", "concept": "The answer is b: behaviour learnt under partial (intermittent) reinforcement is acquired more slowly than under continuous reinforcement but is far more resistant to extinction. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. On continuous reinforcement (CRF) every correct response is rewarded, so the link between response and reward is easy to detect and learning is fastest. On a partial schedule only some responses are rewarded, by number of responses (ratio) or by time (interval), fixed or variable, the four schedules that Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped. Learning is slower because the link is less consistent. But once reward stops, partially reinforced behaviour keeps going much longer. This is the partial reinforcement extinction effect (PREE), first shown by Lloyd Humphreys (1939) with conditioned eyeblinks. Variable ratio schedules produce the most persistent responding of all. Why does it happen? One account is discrimination: after CRF, the first unrewarded response is a sharp change, so the person notices quickly that the rules have changed; after partial reinforcement, a run of non-rewards looks like more of the same. Abram Amsel's (1958) frustration theory adds that partially reinforced animals learn to keep responding while frustrated. Example: when a vending machine fails once, Kabir stops feeding it coins, because it has always worked before (continuous reinforcement). At a slot machine, which pays off unpredictably, he keeps playing through long losing runs (variable ratio). Option b is right because it gives both halves of the effect: slower acquisition and slower extinction. Exam trap: option a is the exact reverse and describes continuous reinforcement. Trainers therefore use continuous reinforcement to establish a new behaviour quickly, then thin it to a partial schedule so the behaviour lasts.", "traps": {"a": "Quick acquisition with rapid extinction describes continuous reinforcement, where every response is rewarded and the end of reward is easy to notice; it is the reverse of the partial reinforcement pattern.", "c": "Partial and continuous schedules do not behave alike; the partial reinforcement extinction effect (Lloyd Humphreys, 1939) is precisely the difference in how fast responses extinguish.", "d": "Partial reinforcement does produce learning, just more slowly; gambling and checking a phone for messages are everyday behaviours kept going entirely by intermittent rewards."}}, {"q": "The 'Flynn Effect' refers to the phenomenon of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Flynn effect is the steady rise in average IQ test scores from one generation to the next across the twentieth century. James Flynn (1984) compared how people of different eras scored on the same tests and showed that American scores rose by about 3 IQ points per decade; he later (1987) found similar gains in many countries. The gains were largest on fluid reasoning tests such as Raven's Progressive Matrices and smaller on vocabulary and general knowledge. Richard Herrnstein and Charles Murray (1994) gave the effect its name in The Bell Curve. Because the rise is far too fast for genetic change, it must be environmental. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and longer schooling, smaller families, a more visually and cognitively complex world, and growing familiarity with abstract, test-like thinking (Flynn's \"scientific spectacles\"). Since the 1990s some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, have shown a slowing or a reversal. The practical result is that norms go stale. If a test was normed 20 years ago, today's children will score about 6 points too high against it, so tests such as the Wechsler scales must be restandardised regularly. This matters most near cut-offs, for example in diagnosing intellectual disability. Example: Meera's grandmother took a reasoning test in 1960 and scored 100 against the norms of her day. If Meera, of the same ability relative to her own generation, took that test today and was scored against the 1960 norms, she would score well above 100. Option d is right because the Flynn effect is defined as a gradual increase in average IQ scores over generations. Exam trap: option a describes the recent \"reverse\" or negative Flynn effect seen in a few countries, the opposite of the classic effect.", "traps": {"a": "A decline over generations is the recent reverse (negative) Flynn effect seen in some Scandinavian data since the 1990s; the Flynn effect itself is a rise.", "b": "The genetic contribution to intelligence is studied by heritability research (twin and adoption studies); the Flynn effect points the other way, because gains this fast must be environmental.", "c": "The stability of an individual's IQ across the lifespan is a separate question about longitudinal test-retest consistency; the Flynn effect concerns shifts in population averages between generations."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In Major Depressive Disorder, neuroimaging studies often show hypoactivity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Reason (R): Because the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is primarily involved in regulating affective salience of threat-related stimuli.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) is an executive control region, not the region that tags threat as emotionally salient. Step 1, the Assertion: true. Many imaging studies of major depressive disorder find reduced activity and metabolism in the DLPFC, especially on the left. Helen Mayberg (1997) built this into her limbic-cortical dysregulation model: dorsal cortical regions such as the DLPFC are underactive, while ventral limbic regions such as the subgenual anterior cingulate and the amygdala are overactive. Low DLPFC activity fits the poor concentration, slowed thinking and indecision of depression. It is also why repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) for depression is given at high frequency over the left DLPFC, a treatment approved in the United States in 2008. Step 2, the Reason: false. The DLPFC's main jobs are working memory, planning, set shifting and top-down control, including deliberately reappraising an emotion. Detecting threat and assigning it emotional salience is chiefly the work of the amygdala, together with the ventromedial prefrontal cortex and the anterior cingulate cortex. In depression the amygdala tends to over-respond to sad or threatening faces. Step 3: because R is false, it cannot explain A. Example: Riya, 34, has been depressed for a year. She cannot hold a phone number in mind or plan her week, signs of low DLPFC function, so her psychiatrist offers rTMS over the left DLPFC. Her habit of noticing every critical look, by contrast, reflects an overactive amygdala. Option c is right because A is a well-replicated finding while R gives the DLPFC a function that belongs mainly to other structures. Exam trap: the DLPFC does regulate emotion indirectly, through reappraisal, so R sounds half right; but \"primarily\" and \"affective salience of threat\" point to the amygdala.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to be true; but appraising the affective salience of threat is mainly the amygdala's role (with ventromedial prefrontal and anterior cingulate cortex), while the DLPFC handles executive control, so R fails and cannot explain A.", "b": "This also needs R to be true; R wrongly assigns threat salience to the DLPFC, so 'both true' fails at the second statement.", "d": "A is not false: left DLPFC hypoactivity in depression is a well-replicated imaging finding and the basis of rTMS targeting; R, not A, is the false statement."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) has been linked with abnormalities in cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuitry. Reason (R): Because this circuit is responsible for error detection, habit formation, and inhibitory control.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the Reason explains the Assertion, because the functions of the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) circuit are exactly the functions that go wrong in OCD. Step 1, the Assertion: true. The CSTC loop runs from the orbitofrontal cortex and anterior cingulate cortex to the striatum (especially the caudate nucleus), then through the globus pallidus to the thalamus and back to the cortex. Lewis Baxter and colleagues (1987, 1992) used PET to show raised glucose metabolism in the orbitofrontal cortex and caudate in OCD, and later found that caudate activity fell in patients who improved with either fluoxetine or behaviour therapy. Neurosurgery and deep brain stimulation for severe, treatment-resistant OCD target this same circuit. Step 2, the Reason: true. The orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex signal that something is wrong (error and conflict detection); the striatum builds habits and normally filters out unimportant signals; and the loop as a whole supports stopping an action once it is no longer needed (inhibitory control). Step 3: R explains A. In OCD the error signal fires even when nothing is wrong, giving an intrusive \"not right\" feeling (the obsession). The caudate fails to filter it, the thalamus loops it back, and a repeated action hardens into a habit that the person cannot inhibit (the compulsion). William Gehring and colleagues (2000) found an enlarged error-related brain potential in OCD, and Claire Gillan and colleagues (2011) showed over-reliance on habits. Example: Arjun locks the door, then feels a nagging sense of error. He checks, feels brief relief, and checks again, ten times; each check strengthens the habit. Option a is right because R names the very functions whose breakdown produces obsessions and compulsions. Exam trap: choose b only when R is true but irrelevant to A. Here R is the mechanism itself, not a side fact.", "traps": {"b": "This would mean the circuit's functions are true but unrelated to OCD; in fact error detection, habit formation and inhibitory control are exactly what fail in obsessions and compulsions, so R does explain A.", "c": "R is not false: error detection (orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex), habit formation (striatum) and inhibitory control are well-established functions of this loop.", "d": "A is not false: imaging since the late 1980s has repeatedly linked OCD to the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate, caudate and thalamus, the parts of the CSTC loop."}}, {"q": "Which following finding would most strongly support a genetic explanation for schizophrenia based on adoption studies?", "concept": "The answer is a: the strongest genetic evidence from adoption studies is that schizophrenia follows the biological parents, who passed on the genes, rather than the adoptive parents, who provided the upbringing. Family members share both genes and environment, so a disorder that runs in families could be inherited or learnt. An adoption study pulls the two apart. A child adopted away at birth gets genes from the biological parents and rearing from the adoptive parents. If the child's risk tracks the biological parent's diagnosis, genes are implicated; if it tracks the adoptive home, environment is. Leonard Heston (1966) followed children of mothers with schizophrenia who had been adopted away soon after birth. About 1 in 10 developed schizophrenia, while none of the control adoptees, whose biological mothers were well, did. The Danish adoption studies of Seymour Kety, David Rosenthal and Paul Wender (from 1968) found schizophrenia spectrum disorders concentrated in the biological, not the adoptive, relatives of adoptees with schizophrenia. Cross-fostering data added that children of healthy biological parents raised by a parent with schizophrenia did not show a raised risk. Later, Pekka Tienari and colleagues (2004) in Finland found that high-risk adoptees became ill most often when raised in disturbed adoptive families, a gene-environment interaction. Example: Meera was born to a mother with schizophrenia and adopted as a newborn by a healthy family. Kabir was born to healthy parents and raised by an adoptive father who developed schizophrenia. If children like Meera show higher rates than children like Kabir, the risk travelled with the genes. Option a is right because it is the exact comparison that separates genetic from rearing influence and finds the genetic side stronger. Exam trap: option c sounds like real evidence, and stressful rearing does matter, but \"regardless of biological parent diagnosis\" makes it a purely environmental finding.", "traps": {"b": "Equal rates whether the affected parent was biological or adoptive would mean rearing matters as much as genes, so it gives no special support to a genetic account.", "c": "Higher rates in stressful adoptive homes regardless of biological risk is an environmental finding; the real Finnish data (Pekka Tienari and colleagues, 2004) showed that family stress mattered mainly for genetically at-risk adoptees, a gene-environment interaction.", "d": "No difference between adoptees of affected and unaffected biological parents would show no genetic effect at all; it is the result a purely environmental theory predicts."}}, {"q": "Fine motor control during skilled behaviors (e.g., writing or playing a musical instrument) primarily depends on:", "concept": "The answer is a: skilled fine movements of the fingers depend on the corticospinal tract, which carries commands from the primary motor cortex down to the alpha motor neurons that drive the muscles. The primary motor cortex lies in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) and is mapped as a motor homunculus in which the hand and fingers take up a very large area. Its axons, with those from premotor areas, form the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract. About 85 to 90% of the fibres cross at the pyramids of the medulla and descend as the lateral (dorsolateral) corticospinal tract to the ventral horn of the spinal cord. There they synapse on alpha motor neurons, in primates often directly, and these contract the muscle fibres. Direct cortex-to-motor-neuron connections are densest for the hand and allow fingers to move independently. Donald Lawrence and Hans Kuypers (1968) cut the pyramidal tracts in monkeys. The animals could still walk, climb and grasp with the whole hand, but they permanently lost the ability to move single fingers, for example to pick food out of a small hole. Other systems have other jobs. The ventromedial pathways (reticulospinal, vestibulospinal, tectospinal) control posture and the trunk and proximal limb muscles. The basal ganglia and cerebellum shape movement, mainly through loops back to the motor cortex. Example: Riya, a veena player, has a small stroke in the internal capsule, where corticospinal fibres are tightly packed. She can still lift her arm, but her fingers cannot pick out separate notes and her handwriting turns clumsy. Option a is right because the corticospinal route from primary motor cortex to alpha motor neurons is the pathway for fractionated, skilled hand movement. Exam trap: \"extrapyramidal\" tempts because the basal ganglia do matter in skilled movement, but they work through the cortex, never \"without cortical involvement\".", "traps": {"b": "The reticulospinal tract is a ventromedial pathway from the brainstem reticular formation that controls posture, locomotion and proximal limb muscles, not precise finger movements.", "c": "The basal ganglia help select and scale movements but act through the thalamus back onto the motor cortex, so 'without cortical involvement' is wrong; their disorders (Parkinson's, Huntington's) mainly disturb starting and stopping movement.", "d": "Intrafusal fibres inside muscle spindles are supplied by gamma motor neurons, which are somatic, not autonomic; they set spindle sensitivity and tone but do not produce skilled movement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best illustrates the permissive effect of hormones on behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is b: thyroid hormones enabling catecholamines to work normally is the textbook permissive effect, in which one hormone does not cause a response itself but must be present for another hormone to act fully. Hormones can interact in three main ways. In a synergistic effect, two hormones together produce more than the sum of each alone. In an antagonistic effect, one opposes the other, as insulin lowers blood glucose and glucagon raises it. In a permissive effect, hormone A has little visible effect on its own but prepares the target tissue so that hormone B can act. Thyroid hormones (thyroxine and triiodothyronine) increase the number of beta-adrenergic receptors on heart, muscle and fat cells. With enough receptors, adrenaline and noradrenaline produce their normal rise in heart rate, alertness and energy release. With too little thyroid hormone, the same catecholamines have a weak effect; with too much, tissues become over-responsive. Example: Meera develops hyperthyroidism. She feels jittery and anxious, with a racing heart and tremor, as if constantly over-aroused. Her catecholamine levels are not especially high; her tissues are simply over-sensitive to them. That is why her doctor gives propranolol, a beta-blocker, for quick relief while the thyroid itself is treated. Option b is right because the thyroid hormones here do not create arousal themselves; they enable catecholamines to regulate it. Exam trap: the other three options each describe a hormone directly producing a response, the opposite of permissiveness. Cortisol is in fact permissive for some actions of catecholamines, so its name may tempt, but option a says it directly initiates fight-or-flight. That response is launched by the sympathetic nervous system and adrenaline, as Walter Cannon (1915) described, while cortisol rises more slowly through the HPA axis.", "traps": {"a": "The fight-or-flight response is triggered within seconds by the sympathetic nervous system and adrenal medulla (Walter Cannon, 1915); cortisol, from the slower HPA axis, is not the trigger, and the option describes a direct effect anyway.", "c": "Oxytocin acting on uterine muscle to cause contractions is a direct effect of one hormone on its target, part of a positive feedback loop during labour, not a permissive one.", "d": "Testosterone's influence on behaviour is an activational effect that shifts the likelihood of aggression over time rather than producing it instantly; either way it is a direct effect, not a permissive one."}}, {"q": "'Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)' incorporates elements from:", "concept": "The answer is c: Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) draws mainly on Buddhist meditation, especially the insight (vipassana) and Zen traditions, taught in a secular, medical form. Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979) founded MBSR at the Stress Reduction Clinic of the University of Massachusetts Medical Center, for patients with chronic pain and stress-related illness whom usual care had not helped. He had trained in Zen and insight meditation and deliberately removed religious language so the practices could be taught in a hospital. The programme runs for eight weeks: a weekly group class of about two and a half hours, one full day of silent practice, and around 45 minutes of daily home practice. Its core exercises are the body scan, sitting meditation on the breath, gentle mindful hatha yoga and mindful everyday activities. Kabat-Zinn (1994) defined mindfulness as paying attention in a particular way: on purpose, in the present moment and non-judgementally. The idea is that automatic reactions and rumination magnify stress; watching sensations and thoughts without fighting them breaks the cycle. MBSR later inspired mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002), which reduces relapse in recurrent depression. Example: Arjun, an IT worker in Bengaluru with chronic back pain, joins an MBSR group. During the body scan he learns to notice the ache as shifting sensations rather than a catastrophe, and his distress falls even though the pain does not vanish. Option c is right because MBSR's mindfulness practices come straight from Buddhist meditation, even though they are taught without religious framing. Exam trap: MBSR includes yoga postures, so \"Integral Yoga\" tempts; but the yoga element is generic mindful hatha yoga, and the defining source of the programme is Buddhist mindfulness (sati). MBSR borrows Buddhist practices, not Buddhist beliefs.", "traps": {"a": "Sufism is the mystical tradition of Islam, with practices such as dhikr (repeated remembrance of God) and the whirling of the Mevlevi order; it is not a source of MBSR.", "b": "Integral Yoga is the spiritual system of Sri Aurobindo (1872 to 1950) and the name of a later school founded by Swami Satchidananda; MBSR uses some gentle hatha yoga postures, but its mindfulness core is Buddhist.", "d": "The Bhagavad Gita teaches karma yoga and nishkama karma (action without attachment to its fruits), ideas used in some Indian psychotherapy models, but MBSR does not draw on it."}}, {"q": "'Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)' is a third-wave behavioral therapy that emphasizes:", "concept": "The answer is c: Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims at psychological flexibility, the ability to stay in contact with the present moment, unwanted thoughts and feelings included, while acting in line with one's chosen values. ACT (said as the word \"act\") was set out by Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999), and Steven Hayes (2004) placed it in the \"third wave\" of behaviour therapy, after classical behaviour therapy and cognitive therapy. Its basic science is relational frame theory, an account of how language lets us link events to each other, which explains why a thought such as \"I am a failure\" can feel as real as an event. ACT holds that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance (trying to escape or control unwanted inner experiences) and cognitive fusion (treating thoughts as literal truths). Six core processes, often drawn as a \"hexaflex\", build flexibility: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. ACT does not try to change the content of thoughts; it changes the person's relationship to them. Example: Meera, a junior lawyer, avoids speaking in meetings because she thinks, \"I will sound stupid.\" Her ACT therapist does not dispute the thought. Meera practises saying, \"I notice I am having the thought that I will sound stupid,\" clarifies that she values becoming a strong advocate, and commits to speaking once in every meeting, anxiety and all. Option c is right because psychological flexibility and values-driven action are the stated goals of ACT. Exam trap: option a describes Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy and Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy, the second wave. ACT deliberately does not challenge irrational thoughts; it defuses from them and makes room for them.", "traps": {"a": "Directly disputing irrational thoughts is the second-wave method of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955) and Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1960s); ACT uses defusion and acceptance and leaves the content of the thought alone.", "b": "Changing behaviour through classical conditioning is first-wave behaviour therapy, such as Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation; ACT uses behavioural methods, but its target is flexibility and values, not conditioned responses.", "d": "Exploring early childhood experiences is the method of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy, from Sigmund Freud (1896 onwards); ACT is present-focused and works on current patterns of avoidance."}}, {"q": "'Psychoneuroimmunology' research has shown a strong link between chronic stress and:", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoneuroimmunology research shows that chronic stress suppresses the immune system, leaving people more prone to infection and slower to heal. Psychoneuroimmunology studies how the mind, nervous system, endocrine system and immune system influence each other. It began with Robert Ader and Nicholas Cohen (1975), who gave rats saccharin-flavoured water together with cyclophosphamide, a drug that suppresses immunity. Later, saccharin alone suppressed the rats' immune response: the immune system could be classically conditioned, so the brain must be talking to it. The main stress route runs through the HPA axis and the sympathetic nervous system. Prolonged stress keeps cortisol and catecholamines high, and these reduce the number and activity of lymphocytes and natural killer cells. Human studies show the effect. Janice Kiecolt-Glaser and colleagues (1984) found that medical students' immune measures fell during examinations, and later (1995) that caregivers of relatives with dementia took about nine days longer than controls to heal a small standard skin wound. Sheldon Cohen and colleagues (1991) gave volunteers cold viruses in nasal drops and found that the more stressed people were, the more likely they were to develop a cold. Suzanne Segerstrom and Gregory Miller (2004), in a meta-analysis of about 300 studies, concluded that brief stressors can briefly boost some first-line defences, but chronic stressors suppress immunity broadly. Example: Kabir works night shifts while caring for his father after a stroke. Over the year he catches every passing cold, and a cut on his hand takes weeks to close. Option b is right because immune suppression under chronic stress is the central, repeatedly replicated finding of the field. Exam trap: option a is a half-truth that holds only for short, acute stress. The stem says chronic, and chronic stress suppresses immunity.", "traps": {"a": "Enhanced immune function is seen only briefly with acute stressors, which can mobilise some natural immunity for minutes to hours; with chronic stress the effect turns into suppression.", "c": "Chronic stress reduces, not increases, neurogenesis: high glucocorticoids suppress the birth of new neurons in the hippocampus, as animal work by Elizabeth Gould and colleagues (1997) showed.", "d": "Chronic stress impairs cognition, especially hippocampal memory and prefrontal attention and flexibility; improved cognitive function is the reverse of the finding."}}, {"q": "The effectiveness of 'Parental mediation of Digital Usage' in mitigating negative impacts on children implicitly assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is d: parental mediation can only work if parents can actually shape how children use digital media and what effects that use has, so this is the assumption built into the idea. Parental mediation means the ways parents manage and interpret media for their children. It began in television research. Patti Valkenburg and colleagues (1999) described three styles: restrictive mediation (rules about time and content), instructive or active mediation (talking with the child about what they see, for example explaining adverts or violence) and social co-viewing (watching together without much comment). Sonia Livingstone and Ellen Helsper (2008) extended the work to the internet, adding technical restrictions (filters, parental controls) and monitoring (checking history or apps). The implicit premise is that the family is a powerful microsystem, in the terms of Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), through which outside influences reach the child, so what parents do can increase benefits and reduce risks. Research broadly supports this, with nuance. Active mediation is linked with better critical understanding of content and fewer negative effects such as aggression after violent content. Restrictive mediation reduces exposure and some online risks, but it also cuts online opportunities, and with adolescents heavy restriction can backfire. This mirrors the finding of Diana Baumrind (1966) that warm, explanatory, authoritative parenting tends to work better than control alone. Example: Arjun's mother lets eight-year-old Riya use a tablet in the living room, with a one-hour limit. Sometimes she watches with her and asks, \"Why do you think that advert says the toy will make you popular?\" Riya starts to spot persuasion on her own. Option d is right because, if parents had no real influence, none of these strategies could reduce harm. Exam trap: the wrong options are extreme claims. Mediation theory assumes media bring both risks and opportunities, and it aims to build children's own self-regulation, not to deny that they have any.", "traps": {"a": "Mediation does not assume children cannot self-regulate; active mediation aims to build self-regulation and critical skills, so its value rests on parents influencing that development, not on children lacking it.", "b": "Mediation exists precisely because content differs in suitability by age, which is why age ratings, filters and co-viewing are used at all; this option contradicts the idea.", "c": "Mediation assumes digital media bring both risks and opportunities (learning, social contact, creativity); if media were inherently harmful, the logical response would be total prohibition, not mediation."}}, {"q": "'Grounded Theory' as a qualitative research method emphasizes:", "concept": "The answer is b: grounded theory develops a theory from data that are systematically gathered and analysed, rather than testing a theory chosen in advance. Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967) set out the method in The Discovery of Grounded Theory, after their study of how hospital staff and dying patients managed awareness of dying. They argued that sociology had become too focused on testing a few grand theories, and that new theory should be \"grounded\" in what people actually do and say. The method is inductive and cyclical: data collection and analysis run together. The researcher codes data line by line (open coding), links codes into categories and relationships (axial coding, in the version of Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin, 1990), and then organises everything around a core category (selective coding). Constant comparison checks each new incident against earlier ones. Theoretical sampling chooses the next participants to test and develop the emerging categories, and sampling stops at theoretical saturation, when new data add nothing new. Memos record ideas as the theory forms. Versions differ: Glaser stressed letting theory emerge, Strauss and Corbin gave more structured procedures, and Kathy Charmaz (2006) developed a constructivist grounded theory in which researcher and participants co-construct the theory. Example: Meera interviews mothers caring for adult sons with schizophrenia in Kerala. Early codes include \"hiding the illness\" and \"waiting for marriage\". Comparing cases, she deliberately samples mothers from different communities and builds a theory of \"protective concealment\". Option b is right because generating theory from systematically gathered and analysed data is the defining aim of grounded theory. Exam trap: grounded theory ends in a theory, so students may link it with hypothesis testing; it moves in the opposite, inductive direction, from data to theory.", "traps": {"a": "Testing pre-existing hypotheses is the hypothetico-deductive logic of quantitative research (Karl Popper's falsification, 1934); grounded theory is inductive and generates hypotheses rather than testing them.", "c": "Quantifying social phenomena is the aim of quantitative methods such as experiments and structured surveys; grounded theory works with qualitative data such as interviews, observation notes and documents.", "d": "Large-scale surveys use big probability samples and standard questionnaires to estimate population values; grounded theory uses small, purposive and theoretical samples."}}, {"q": "The scenario which best demonstrates the unique advantage of invasive methods over noninvasive neuroimaging is using:", "concept": "The answer is c: single-cell recording of hippocampal place cells shows what only an invasive method can, the firing of one identified neuron, spike by spike, while the animal behaves. Invasive methods put something into brain tissue: a microelectrode, a lesion or a stimulating probe. Noninvasive methods (fMRI, PET, EEG, MEG) record from outside the skull and always see the pooled activity of very many neurons. In single-unit recording a fine microelectrode sits next to one neuron and picks up each action potential it fires, so it has the best spatial resolution (one cell) and the best temporal resolution (single spikes, in milliseconds) at the same time. The classic example is John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky (1971), who recorded from the rat hippocampus and found place cells, each firing only when the rat was in one particular spot; O'Keefe shared the 2014 Nobel Prize for this work. Suppose Meera, a neuroscience student, wants to know whether a single hippocampal neuron fires only when her rat reaches the left corner of a maze. No scanner can answer that. An fMRI voxel a few millimetres across holds hundreds of thousands of neurons, and the BOLD signal lags seconds behind their firing; EEG is fast but blurred across centimetres of cortex. Only an electrode inside the hippocampus can reveal one neuron's code. Option c is right because it is the only scenario that needs, and exploits, the cellular and real-time precision that only electrodes placed in the brain can give. Exam trap: two decoys borrow half of this advantage. PET involves an injection, so students call it invasive, but the tracer measures regional metabolism averaged over minutes. EEG has millisecond timing, so \"real time\" tempts, but its electrodes stay on the scalp and it cannot isolate a single neuron.", "traps": {"a": "fMRI is noninvasive and measures the BOLD signal, an indirect index of blood oxygenation that pools hundreds of thousands of neurons and lags their firing by seconds, so it cannot show what is unique to invasive methods.", "b": "PET traces glucose metabolism (usually with radioactive FDG) over minutes; the tracer is injected into a vein, but nothing enters brain tissue and the result is a regional average, not the activity of single neurons.", "d": "EEG event-related potentials come from scalp electrodes; they have millisecond timing but poor spatial resolution, summing millions of cortical neurons, so this is a noninvasive strength, not an invasive one."}}, {"q": "Damage to the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) most consistently leads to:", "concept": "The answer is d: damage to the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) most consistently impairs conflict monitoring and blunts the emotional, unpleasant side of pain. The ACC lies on the medial surface of the frontal lobe, curving around the front of the corpus callosum; James Papez (1937) placed the cingulate gyrus in his emotion circuit. It does two jobs that examiners like. First, it watches for conflict and errors. In the Stroop task, naming the ink colour of the word RED printed in green creates response conflict, and the ACC becomes active. The conflict monitoring theory of Matthew Botvinick and colleagues (2001) holds that the ACC detects such conflict and signals the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex to apply more control. Second, it codes how much pain hurts, not where it is. Pierre Rainville and colleagues (1997) used hypnosis to change how unpleasant hot water felt on the hand without changing its felt intensity; ACC activity tracked the unpleasantness, while somatosensory cortex did not. Patients given a cingulotomy for intractable pain often say the pain is still there but no longer bothers them. Picture Kabir after a bilateral cingulate stroke: he is slow and error-prone on the Stroop test, does not notice his own mistakes, and calls a pinprick \"sharp, but it does not upset me\". Large lesions can add apathy and even akinetic mutism. Option d is right because it names exactly these two ACC functions, detecting cognitive conflict and giving pain its affective sting. Exam trap: the cingulate belongs to the limbic system, so examiners pair it with limbic syndromes. Klüver-Bucy syndrome and loss of fear belong to the amygdala and anterior temporal lobes, and dense anterograde amnesia belongs to the hippocampus; none of these is the ACC's signature.", "traps": {"a": "Severe anterograde amnesia with spared implicit learning is the hippocampal and medial temporal lobe pattern, as in patient H.M., first described by William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957), who later improved at mirror drawing without remembering any practice.", "b": "Loss of autonomic fear responses follows amygdala damage, which makes animals tamer, not more aggressive; disinhibited aggression points to orbitofrontal damage. Neither is the classic result of an ACC lesion.", "c": "Hyperorality, hypersexuality and tameness make up Klüver-Bucy syndrome (Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy, 1937), caused by bilateral removal of the anterior temporal lobes including the amygdala, not by cingulate damage."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered the greatest methodological risk in computer-adaptive psychological testing compared to traditional paper-pencil testing?", "concept": "The answer is c: in computerised adaptive testing (CAT) the greatest methodological risk is item exposure, which compromises test security. A CAT rests on item response theory (IRT), associated with Georg Rasch (1960) and Frederic Lord (1980). Every item in a large bank has known parameters, such as difficulty and discrimination. The computer starts with a medium item, re-estimates the person's ability (theta) after each answer, and picks the next item that gives the most information at that level: right answers bring harder items, wrong answers easier ones. The test stops when the estimate is precise enough, often after far fewer items than a paper form needs, and every score lands on the same theta scale even though each person saw different items. The weakness comes from the same algorithm. Because the computer always seeks the most informative item, a small set of highly discriminating items is chosen again and again while much of the bank sits unused. Those overused items are seen by thousands of candidates, remembered, and passed on through coaching centres and websites. Imagine Riya sits an adaptive entrance test and recognises five items that a senior described to her last month: her score now reflects memory, not ability, and validity collapses. Test makers fight back with exposure control rules, very large and frequently refreshed banks, and item rotation, but the risk is built into the method. Option c is right because overexposure is created by the adaptive selection rule itself, whereas a fixed paper form can be sealed until the day and replaced by a fresh form at the next sitting. Exam trap: students list the familiar weaknesses of fixed tests (ceiling and floor effects, uneven standardisation, examiner bias), but CAT actually reduces all three; the risk peculiar to CAT is security, not precision.", "traps": {"a": "Ceiling and floor effects plague fixed paper forms; CAT reduces them by moving item difficulty up or down to match each examinee, provided the bank has items at the extremes.", "b": "A computer delivers identical instructions, timing and scoring, so standardisation is usually tighter, not looser; the different items each person receives are equated through the common IRT scale.", "d": "CAT scoring is automatic and algorithmic, which removes examiner bias; scorer bias is a concern of individually administered and open-ended tests such as the Rorschach or Wechsler verbal subtests."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Conversion Disorder symptoms are intentionally produced for external incentives. Reason (R): Because patients with conversion disorder consciously feign symptoms to obtain tangible rewards.", "concept": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, and d is the only option that rejects it. Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5-TR (2022), means real symptoms of altered movement or sensation (weakness, non-epileptic seizures, blindness, loss of speech) that do not fit any recognised neurological disease, often shown by positive signs such as Hoover's sign. The person is not pretending. Step 1, the Assertion: false. Conversion symptoms are not intentionally produced. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the old formal criterion requiring the clinician to rule out feigning, because feigning is so hard to prove, but the disorder is still understood as involuntary. Psychodynamic writers spoke of primary gain (relief from an unconscious conflict) and secondary gain (attention, escape from duties), yet neither is conscious faking. Step 2, the Reason: also false. Consciously feigning symptoms to obtain tangible rewards is malingering, which DSM-5-TR lists as a V/Z code, a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention, not a mental disorder. Intentional production with no external reward, only the wish to occupy the sick role, is factitious disorder (the Munchausen syndrome named by Richard Asher, 1951). Step 3, whether R explains A, does not arise. Compare Meera, whose legs give way the week her engagement is broken off and who is frightened that she cannot walk, with Arjun, who fakes back pain to win an insurance claim: Meera has conversion disorder, Arjun is malingering. Option d is right because it is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested. Exam trap: examiners swap these three terms; ask two questions, is it intentional, and is there an external reward? Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no \"both false\" option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question for exactly this reason, so no one was marked on it.", "traps": {"a": "This needs both statements true with R explaining A; it fails at the first step, because conversion symptoms are not intentional, and R describes malingering rather than conversion disorder.", "b": "This needs both statements true; it fails because the Assertion is false (conversion symptoms are involuntary) and the Reason is false too, since conscious feigning for rewards defines malingering.", "c": "This accepts the Assertion, which is exactly the error: intentional production for external incentives is malingering, a V/Z code, not conversion disorder."}}, {"q": "When employing 'Structural Equation Modeling (SEM)' in research, a fundamental assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is d: structural equation modelling (SEM) assumes that latent, unobserved variables can be measured indirectly through observed indicators. A latent variable is a construct you cannot see directly, such as depression, intelligence or social support; you only see its indicators, such as questionnaire scores, ratings and test results. SEM joins two older tools. The measurement model is a confirmatory factor analysis: each latent variable is defined by several indicators, and each indicator gets a loading plus its own error term. The structural model is a path analysis, the method Sewall Wright (1921) devised for testing a diagram of directional links, now drawn between the latent variables. Karl Jöreskog (1973) set out the general estimation method, and his LISREL program made SEM practical. Suppose Kabir studies whether social support protects college students against depression. He measures support with three scales and depression with the Beck Depression Inventory, a clinician rating and a sleep questionnaire. SEM treats support and depression as latent factors, separates true construct variance from measurement error in each indicator, estimates the path between the factors, and then asks whether the whole model reproduces the observed covariance matrix, judged by fit indices such as the comparative fit index (CFI) and the root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA). Option d is right because the latent variable and indicator measurement model is what sets SEM apart from ordinary regression or path analysis on raw scores. Exam trap: SEM is often called \"causal modelling\", so option b tempts. The causal arrows come from theory and design, not from the data; with cross-sectional correlations several different models can fit equally well, so a good fit shows that the data are consistent with the model, not that the causes are proven.", "traps": {"a": "If every variable were directly observable, ordinary multiple regression or Sewall Wright's path analysis would do; the distinctive contribution of SEM is modelling latent constructs.", "b": "SEM tests whether correlational data are consistent with a causal model drawn from theory; it cannot by itself prove causation, because equivalent models with reversed arrows can fit just as well.", "c": "SEM explicitly includes error terms for every indicator and disturbance terms for every predicted variable, and fit is judged approximately (CFI, RMSEA); perfect fit is neither assumed nor expected."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) is particularly effective for Borderline Personality Disorder. Reason (R): Because DBT balances acceptance strategies with change-oriented interventions.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the balance of acceptance and change is exactly why dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) works for borderline personality disorder (BPD). Step 1, the Assertion: true. Marsha Linehan (1993) built DBT for chronically suicidal women, most with BPD. In her first randomised trial (1991) DBT clients made fewer and less dangerous self-harm attempts, spent fewer days in hospital and dropped out less than those given treatment as usual, and a 2006 trial against expert community therapists still halved suicide attempts. Step 2, the Reason: true. The word dialectical means holding two opposites and finding a synthesis. DBT's acceptance side (validation, core mindfulness drawn from Zen practice, radical acceptance) is paired with a change side from behaviour therapy (chain analysis of each self-harm episode, skills training, problem solving). Step 3, the link: R explains A. Linehan found that standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients, who then quit, while acceptance alone left the self-harm untouched. Her biosocial theory sees BPD as emotion dysregulation arising when a biologically sensitive child meets an invalidating environment, so a treatment that first validates and then teaches regulation targets the disorder's very roots. Take Riya, 22, who cuts herself after fights with her partner. Her therapist says her pain makes sense given what happened (acceptance), then walks through the chain of events and coaches her in distress tolerance skills (change). Option a is right because the acceptance and change dialectic is the mechanism Linehan designed to keep BPD clients in treatment and to reduce self-harm. Exam trap: option b tempts students who think DBT works simply because it teaches four skills modules; the skills are the change half of the dialectic, not a separate reason.", "traps": {"b": "This says R does not explain A; it fails because the acceptance and change balance is precisely what Linehan designed to stop BPD clients feeling invalidated and dropping out, so R is the explanation.", "c": "This calls R false; but balancing acceptance (validation, mindfulness) with change (chain analysis, skills training) is the very definition of DBT's dialectic.", "d": "This calls A false; but DBT has the strongest randomised trial evidence of any psychotherapy for BPD, cutting self-harm, suicide attempts and hospital days."}}, {"q": "The 'Semantic Differential Scale' measures:", "concept": "The answer is b: the semantic differential measures attitudes, and the meaning a concept has for a person, through ratings along bipolar adjective pairs. Charles Osgood, George Suci and Percy Tannenbaum introduced it in The Measurement of Meaning (1957). The respondent sees a concept at the top of the page, such as \"mental hospital\", and rates it on a row of seven-point scales whose ends are opposite adjectives: good to bad, strong to weak, active to passive, clean to dirty. Factor analysis of thousands of such ratings across many concepts and cultures kept finding the same three dimensions of connotative meaning, known by the initials EPA: evaluation (good to bad), potency (strong to weak) and activity (fast to slow). Evaluation carries most of the variance and is the attitude component proper, so a person's attitude score is usually the mean of the evaluative scales. The method is quick, works for any concept, and can compare concepts on the same profile. For example, Arjun, a psychology student, gives the semantic differential to two groups of college students before and after an anti-stigma talk. They rate \"person with schizophrenia\" on good to bad, safe to dangerous and strong to weak, and the talk shifts the evaluative ratings towards the positive pole. Option b is right because rating a concept between pairs of opposite adjectives is the defining format of the semantic differential. Exam trap: option a describes the Likert scale of Rensis Likert (1932), where people agree or disagree with statements; both look like rows of numbered boxes, but Likert scales rate statements while the semantic differential rates a concept between two adjectives. Remember: no statements, only adjective pairs.", "traps": {"a": "Agreement or disagreement with attitude statements is the summated rating scale of Rensis Likert (1932); Louis Thurstone's (1928) equal-appearing interval scale also uses statements. Neither uses bipolar adjectives.", "c": "Self-report personality inventories such as the MMPI or the 16PF measure traits through statements about oneself; the semantic differential measures the connotative meaning of, and attitude towards, a concept.", "d": "Intelligence through problem-solving tasks is the domain of ability tests such as the Wechsler scales or Raven's Progressive Matrices, not an attitude scale."}}, {"q": "The 'Nature-Nurture controversy' in behavioral genetics, particularly when using twin studies, assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is d: the twin design rests on comparing genetic similarity with resemblance, and identical twins reared apart are its sharpest test. Monozygotic (MZ) twins come from one fertilised egg and share all their genes; dizygotic (DZ) twins share about 50 percent of the genes that vary, like ordinary siblings. If MZ pairs resemble each other more than DZ pairs on a trait, genes are implicated; Douglas Falconer (1960) turned this into a rough heritability estimate, twice the difference between the MZ and DZ correlations. Twins reared together, however, share a home, so their likeness could be environmental. MZ twins separated early and raised apart remove that shared home: any similarity between them cannot come from a common upbringing, so it is attributed to their identical genes. From 1979 Thomas Bouchard and colleagues ran the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart; their 1990 report in Science found that MZ twins raised in different families were about as alike in intelligence and personality as MZ twins raised together. Imagine identical twins Meera and Kavya, adopted at birth by families in Kochi and Delhi: at 30 they have similar IQs and similar levels of anxiety, which points to genes. Whatever differences remain between them must come from their different environments. Option d is right because it is the only option about the reared-apart identical twin design, which isolates genetic influence. Exam trap: twin studies never assume that environment is negligible; models split variance into genes, shared environment and non-shared environment. Key note: as printed, option d says \"differences\" between identical twins reared apart are due to genes, which is backwards (identical twins share all their genes, so their differences must be environmental); the setter clearly meant the similarities of identical twins reared apart, and d is the only option about that design, so d is used.", "traps": {"a": "The whole point of studying identical twins reared apart is that they do NOT share an environment; the equal environments assumption applies to twins reared together, where MZ and DZ pairs are assumed to share environments equally.", "b": "Twin studies estimate environmental influence rather than assume it away; heritability estimates for most behavioural traits are well below 100 percent, leaving a large share to environment.", "c": "Fraternal twins share only about half their segregating genes, so differences between them are partly genetic, not solely environmental; only differences between identical twins can be pinned on environment."}}, {"q": "'Behavior Modification' techniques, rooted in operant conditioning, implicitly assume that:", "concept": "The answer is b: behaviour modification assumes that behaviour is learned, and kept going or weakened, by its consequences. The idea goes back to the puzzle-box experiments of Edward Thorndike (1898) and his law of effect: responses followed by satisfying results are stamped in, those followed by discomfort are stamped out. B. F. Skinner (1938) recast this as operant conditioning. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, and both increase a behaviour; positive punishment adds something unpleasant and negative punishment removes something pleasant, and both decrease it; extinction, withholding the reinforcer that maintained a behaviour, makes it fade. Behaviour modification applies these principles to real problems. It starts with a functional analysis of antecedents, behaviour and consequences, then changes the consequences. The assumption underneath is environmental: maladaptive behaviour is learned in the same lawful way as adaptive behaviour, so it can be unlearned without digging for hidden causes. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) showed this on psychiatric wards with the token economy, where patients earned tokens for self-care and work and exchanged them for privileges. Take Kabir, 7, who throws tantrums at homework time. His parents stop giving in to the tantrums (extinction) and give him a star for every completed page, exchangeable for cricket time (token reinforcement); within weeks the tantrums drop. Option b is right because reinforcement and punishment, the consequences of behaviour, are the core of operant theory and of every behaviour modification technique. Exam trap: option c tempts because of \"cognitive behaviour modification\", the term Donald Meichenbaum (1977) used for self-instructional training; even that approach treats thoughts as one influence among many, never the sole determinant, and classic behaviour modification sets cognition aside.", "traps": {"a": "Unconscious conflict is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic model; behaviour modification treats the behaviour itself as the problem and does not infer hidden conflicts behind it.", "c": "Treating cognition as the sole determinant exaggerates even the cognitive therapies of Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis; operant approaches locate the causes of behaviour in environmental consequences.", "d": "Innate drives as prime movers belong to instinct theories such as William McDougall's (1908) and to Freud's drive theory; operant theory explains behaviour by its learned consequences, not inborn urges."}}, {"q": "'Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT)' assumes that psychological distress is primarily caused by:", "concept": "The answer is b: rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) holds that psychological distress comes mainly from irrational beliefs, not from events themselves. Albert Ellis (1955) began rational therapy after psychoanalysis disappointed him; it became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. Its core is the ABC model. A is the activating event, B the person's beliefs about A, and C the emotional and behavioural consequences. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis says A contributes, but B is the main cause. An irrational belief is a rigid, absolute demand that does not fit reality: musts and shoulds (demandingness), awfulising (\"this is the worst thing possible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global rating of self or others (\"I am worthless\"). A rational belief is a flexible preference. Therapy adds D, vigorous disputing of the irrational belief, which produces E, an effective new belief. Take Riya, who fails a mock entrance test (A). If she thinks \"I must always score at the top, or I am a failure\" (B), she becomes depressed and stops studying (C). Her therapist disputes the belief: where is the evidence that one mark makes a whole person worthless? She arrives at \"I would prefer to do well, and one mock does not decide my future\" (E), feels disappointed rather than depressed, and returns to her books. Option b is right because Ellis placed the cause of disturbance in the B of the ABC model, the person's irrational beliefs. Exam trap: \"cognitive distortions\" (catastrophising, overgeneralisation and so on) is strictly the language of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1967); Ellis spoke of irrational beliefs. Examiners mix the two vocabularies, but both point to the same cognitive cause, so b still fits.", "traps": {"a": "Early childhood trauma as the main cause is the psychoanalytic and trauma-focused view; Ellis held that people keep re-indoctrinating themselves with irrational beliefs in the present, so REBT works on current beliefs, not the past.", "c": "Unmet needs for self-actualisation is the humanistic view of Abraham Maslow (1943) and Carl Rogers; REBT does not locate distress there.", "d": "Biological imbalance is the medical model; Ellis did think humans have a biological tendency to think irrationally, but therapy targets the beliefs, not brain chemistry."}}, {"q": "According to John Holland's RIASEC model, a mismatch between personality type and occupational environment is most likely to result in:", "concept": "The answer is d: in John Holland's theory a poor fit between personality type and work environment predicts lower vocational satisfaction and more career instability. John L. Holland (1959; set out fully in 1973) proposed that both people and work environments can be described by six types, known by the initials RIASEC: Realistic (hands-on, mechanical), Investigative (scientific, analytical), Artistic (creative, unstructured), Social (helping, teaching), Enterprising (persuading, leading) and Conventional (orderly, data-keeping). Each person gets a three-letter code from tests such as his Self-Directed Search (1971), and each occupation gets a code from the people who work in it and the tasks it demands. The types sit on a hexagon: neighbours are alike (Realistic and Investigative), opposites least alike (Realistic and Social). The central prediction is congruence. People seek environments that let them use their skills and express their values, and the closer the match between their type and their environment, the greater their satisfaction, stability and achievement. A mismatch, called incongruence, brings dissatisfaction, poorer performance and a pull towards changing jobs or fields until a better fit is found. Take Arjun, whose code is Artistic-Social, working in a bank's accounts department, a Conventional environment of rules and ledgers. He is bored, keeps applying elsewhere, and finally retrains as an art teacher, where he settles. Research supports the prediction, though the link between congruence and satisfaction is real but modest. Option d is right because lower satisfaction and job instability are exactly what Holland's congruence principle predicts from a poor fit between person and environment. Exam trap: option c borrows the language of career development theories (adaptability, growth through challenge), but Holland's model is a matching model: misfit produces strain and change, not enhanced adaptability.", "traps": {"a": "Crystallised and fluid intelligence are Raymond Cattell's (1963) ability concepts; Holland's theory concerns interests and personality fit and makes no prediction about intelligence.", "b": "Socioeconomic status shapes access to occupations, but Holland's theory predicts measurable effects of fit; claiming no effect contradicts its central congruence hypothesis.", "c": "Holland predicts strain, dissatisfaction and job change from misfit, not enhanced adaptability; growth through forced diversification is not part of the RIASEC model."}}, {"q": "The statement best illustrates the distinction between directive and nondirective counselling approaches:", "concept": "The answer is c: directive counselling is counsellor-led, built on diagnosis, interpretation and advice, while nondirective counselling lets the client explore and decide, trusting the client's capacity for growth. Directive counselling grew from the trait-factor approach of Frank Parsons (1909), who taught vocational guidance as knowing yourself, knowing the world of work and matching the two. Edmund G. Williamson (1939) of the University of Minnesota turned it into a method in How to Counsel Students. The counsellor collects data, often test scores, and moves through six steps: analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. The counsellor is the expert who interprets the evidence and recommends a course of action, and the focus is intellectual problem solving. Nondirective counselling came from Carl Rogers (1942) in Counseling and Psychotherapy. The counsellor does not advise, interpret or steer; he listens, reflects feelings and accepts the client, believing that a person who feels understood will find his own direction. Responsibility for the choice stays with the client, and the focus is on feelings and self-understanding. Rogers later renamed the approach client-centred, and Frederick Thorne (1950) argued for an eclectic middle path. Consider Meera, a Class 12 student torn between engineering and design. A directive counsellor reviews her aptitude and interest scores and advises design. A nondirective counsellor reflects her excitement when she talks about sketching and her fear of disappointing her father, until she reaches her own decision. Option c is right because it captures the core contrast: who leads the session, with interpretation and advice on one side and self-exploration and growth on the other. Exam trap: options a and b take the right ideas and swap them between the two schools; autonomy and feelings belong to Rogers, problem solving and counsellor control to Williamson.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the emphases: Williamson's directive counselling is intellectual problem solving, while Rogers's nondirective counselling attends to the release and clarification of feelings.", "b": "This reverses the definitions: client autonomy is the hallmark of the nondirective approach, while the directive counsellor takes charge of diagnosis and advice.", "d": "Wrong origins: directive counselling grew from Frank Parsons's trait-factor guidance and Williamson's Minnesota school, nondirective counselling from Carl Rogers's humanistic work, which rejected psychoanalytic interpretation; existential therapy (Rollo May, Viktor Frankl) is the source of neither."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In Person-Centered Therapy, the therapist's unconditional positive regard fosters self-actualization. Reason (R): Because clients experience reduced incongruence when accepted without judgment.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and reduced incongruence is the route by which unconditional positive regard frees self-actualisation. Carl Rogers (1951, 1959) held that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow and fulfil potential. Growth is blocked by conditions of worth: as children we learn that we are loved only when we think, feel or act in approved ways, so we deny or distort experiences that clash with the approved self-concept. The resulting mismatch between self-concept and actual experience is incongruence, which Rogers saw as the root of anxiety and maladjustment. Step 1, the Assertion: true. In his 1957 paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change, Rogers named three core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (UPR) and empathy. UPR, a term coined by Stanley Standal (1954), means valuing the client without conditions, and Rogers held that it frees the actualising tendency. Step 2, the Reason: true. When feelings meet acceptance rather than judgement, the client no longer needs to defend against them, so denied experiences can enter awareness and the self-concept widens to include them; incongruence falls. Step 3, the link: R explains A. Falling incongruence is the mechanism that lets the client move towards being a fully functioning person. Take Kabir, who believes \"a good son never feels angry with his father\". In therapy his anger meets warm acceptance, not shock. He admits it, comes to see himself as a loving son who is sometimes angry, and stops spending energy hiding it. Option a is right because Rogers explicitly links acceptance, falling incongruence and renewed growth in one causal chain. Exam trap: \"congruence\" names both a therapist condition (genuineness) and a client state (self matching experience); examiners blur the two to make b look tempting.", "traps": {"b": "This says R does not explain A; it fails because in Rogers's theory reduced incongruence is the very process through which unconditional positive regard releases the actualising tendency.", "c": "This calls R false; but Rogers held that acceptance without conditions of worth lets denied experience into awareness, so incongruence shrinks.", "d": "This calls A false; but unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's three core conditions (1957), and its stated purpose is to free the client's growth towards self-actualisation."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is equally effective across all cultural contexts. Reason (R): Because maladaptive cognitive schemas are universal and unaffected by cultural variations.", "concept": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, and d is the only option that rejects it. Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) has a large evidence base, but most of its trials were run with Western, educated, industrialised, rich and democratic samples, the WEIRD populations described by Joseph Henrich, Steven Heine and Ara Norenzayan (2010). Step 1, the Assertion: false. CBT is not equally effective in every cultural context. Meta-analyses of culturally adapted interventions, such as that of Derek Griner and Timothy Smith (2006), find that treatments adapted in language, metaphors, content and goals do better for minority and non-Western clients than unadapted ones. The culturally adapted CBT trials led by Farooq Naeem in Pakistan, which fitted the therapy's language, examples and family involvement to local culture, are a well-known example. Step 2, the Reason: also false. Aaron Beck (1967) described schemas as core beliefs formed from early experience, and that experience is cultural. What counts as an irrational belief, how much weight family obligations carry, whether the self is defined individually or collectively: all of these vary across cultures. Step 3, the link, does not arise. Consider Arjun, a 24-year-old in Kochi, who believes \"I must follow my parents' choice of career\". An unadapted therapist might dispute this as a rigid \"should\"; in his joint family it is a shared value, and attacking it alienates him. An adapted therapist works with the family's goals instead. Option d is right because it is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is what the item tests. Exam trap: \"evidence-based\" does not mean \"universally effective\"; strong efficacy in one population does not transfer automatically to another. Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no \"both false\" option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question, so no one was marked on it.", "traps": {"a": "This needs both statements true with R explaining A; it fails at the first step, since effectiveness varies with cultural fit, and R is false because schema content is shaped by culture.", "b": "This needs both statements true; it fails because adapted CBT outperforms unadapted CBT in non-Western groups (A false) and schemas are culturally shaped (R false).", "c": "This accepts the Assertion; but equal effectiveness everywhere is not supported, since the evidence base is largely Western and cultural adaptation improves outcomes."}}, {"q": "'Transactional Analysis' as a psychotherapy approach focuses on:", "concept": "The answer is b: transactional analysis (TA) analyses the ego states Parent, Adult and Child, the transactions between them, and the life scripts people follow. Eric Berne (1961) founded TA in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and popularised it in Games People Play (1964). Berne, trained as a psychoanalyst, borrowed the term ego state from psychoanalytic ego psychology but made it observable: an ego state is a consistent pattern of feeling and behaviour that you can see and hear. The Parent holds attitudes copied from parents and parental figures, critical or nurturing. The Adult processes present reality objectively. The Child holds feelings and responses from childhood, free or adapted. TA works at four levels. Structural analysis identifies which ego state is active. Transactional analysis proper studies exchanges: complementary transactions run in parallel and talk flows, crossed ones stop it. Game analysis looks at repeated ulterior transactions with a predictable bad-feeling payoff, such as \"Why Don't You, Yes But\". Script analysis uncovers the life script, an unconscious life plan decided in childhood under parental messages such as \"Don't be close\". People also need strokes, units of recognition, and prefer negative strokes to none. Picture Meera asking her husband Rahul, \"Where are my keys?\" (Adult to Adult). He replies, \"Why do you always blame me?\" (Child to Parent). The transaction is crossed and an argument begins; a TA therapist helps both notice which ego state is speaking. Option b is right because ego states and scripts are the defining units of analysis in TA. Exam trap: the three ego states loosely echo the superego, ego and id of Sigmund Freud (1923), so option a tempts; but Berne studied observable social transactions, not unconscious conflict and defences.", "traps": {"a": "Unconscious conflicts and defence mechanisms are the focus of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis; Berne moved away from it to analyse observable transactions, even though his ego states loosely echo superego, ego and id.", "c": "Empathy and unconditional positive regard are the core conditions of Carl Rogers's person-centred therapy (1957), not of TA.", "d": "Challenging irrational beliefs is the disputing step of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955), not TA."}}, {"q": "When using 'Single-Subject Designs' (e.g., ABAB reversal design) in research, a key assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is b: an ABAB reversal design assumes that the target behaviour will return towards baseline when the intervention is withdrawn. Single-subject (single-case) designs study one person, or a few, measured repeatedly over time. Murray Sidman (1960) set out their logic in Tactics of Scientific Research, and Alan Kazdin (1982) systematised them for clinical work. Phase A is the baseline: the behaviour is recorded, by frequency, duration or time intervals, until it is stable, which predicts what would happen without treatment. Phase B introduces the treatment. In the second A the treatment is withdrawn, and in the second B it is reinstated. The logic is replication within one person. If the behaviour improves each time the treatment starts and worsens each time it stops, coincidence, maturation or outside events become very unlikely explanations, and the treatment is shown to control the behaviour. That logic only works if the behaviour can reverse. Take Arjun, 8, who hits classmates about 12 times a day at baseline. With token reinforcement for calm play, hitting falls to 3 a day; when the tokens stop it climbs back to 10; when they return it drops to 2. That rise and fall is the evidence. If the treatment had taught a lasting skill, such as reading, the behaviour would not reverse and the design would fail, and withdrawing an effective treatment for self-injury would be unethical. In such cases the multiple-baseline design, which starts treatment at staggered times across behaviours, settings or people, is used instead. Option b is right because the return to baseline during withdrawal is the very event that demonstrates experimental control. Exam trap: option a states the reverse of the assumption; an irreversible effect is exactly the situation in which ABAB cannot be used.", "traps": {"a": "An irreversible effect would defeat the design, because nothing would change during withdrawal; when effects cannot reverse (a learned skill), the multiple-baseline design is used instead.", "c": "Single-case designs aim first at internal validity within one person; generality is built later through direct and systematic replication across cases, as Murray Sidman argued.", "d": "One participant is enough: control comes from repeated measurement and planned phase changes, judged mainly by visual inspection of graphs rather than group statistics."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'Neuroplasticity' in the context of recovery from brain injury implicitly assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is a: the idea of neuroplasticity in recovery assumes that the brain can reorganise itself and compensate for damage. Neuroplasticity is the nervous system's capacity to change its structure and function with experience, learning and injury. After a stroke or head injury some early recovery has simpler causes: swelling subsides, blood flow returns to tissue around the lesion, and distant areas stunned by the sudden loss of their inputs start working again (diaschisis, a term of Constantin von Monakow, 1914). Longer-term recovery depends on plasticity proper. Surviving axons send branches into vacated synapses (collateral sprouting), silent connections are unmasked, and neighbouring cortex, or the matching area in the other hemisphere, takes over lost territory (cortical reorganisation). Randolph Nudo and colleagues (1996) showed in monkeys that after a small motor cortex lesion, retraining the hand preserved, and sometimes enlarged, the hand area in nearby cortex. Constraint-induced movement therapy, developed by Edward Taub (1993), restrains a stroke patient's good arm so that the weak arm must be used intensively, enlarging its cortical map. Take Kabir, 45, whose left-hemisphere stroke weakened his right hand. Weeks of daily training with his left hand in a mitt improve his grip and writing, and scans show more activity in motor areas around the damage. Dead neurons are rarely replaced, but function can be rerouted. Recovery is better with small lesions, intensive practice and high cognitive reserve. Option a is right because every rehabilitation programme rests on the premise that the brain can rewire and compensate. Exam trap: option d echoes the Kennard principle of Margaret Kennard (1936), that early lesions are better tolerated; youth helps, but adult brains are plastic too, as stroke rehabilitation shows.", "traps": {"b": "Saying the brain cannot recover any lost function is the old view of a fixed adult nervous system, refuted by everyday stroke recovery and by rehabilitation research.", "c": "Many lost neurons are indeed not replaced, but function can return through sprouting and reorganisation; calling all damage permanent and irreversible denies plasticity itself.", "d": "Plasticity is greatest early in life (the Kennard principle), but adults show it too, as in stroke rehabilitation and the enlarged posterior hippocampi of London taxi drivers (Eleanor Maguire, 2000)."}}, {"q": "In hypothesis testing, which situation best illustrates a Type II error?", "concept": "The answer is d: a Type II error is concluding that a therapy does not work when it actually does, that is, failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) framed hypothesis testing as a decision with two possible errors. The null hypothesis (H0) says there is no effect; the alternative says there is one. A Type I error is rejecting a true H0, a false alarm; its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets, usually .05. A Type II error is retaining a false H0, a miss; its probability is beta. Power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of detecting a real effect, and Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested aiming for at least .80. Type II errors become likely when the sample is small, the true effect is small, measurement is noisy, or alpha is very strict, because a stricter alpha lowers Type I risk at the cost of raising Type II risk. Take Riya, who tests a new mindfulness programme for exam anxiety with only 12 students. The programme genuinely helps, but with so few participants her p value is .20, so she retains H0 and reports that the programme is ineffective. That is a Type II error, caused by low power; with 100 students the same effect would probably have reached significance. Option d is right because it describes a real effect being missed, a false H0 that was not rejected. Exam trap: option b borrows the phrase \"failing to reject the null hypothesis\" from the definition of a Type II error but adds \"when the null is actually true\", which turns it into a correct decision. Ask two questions every time: what was decided, and what is the truth? Type I is a false positive, Type II a false negative.", "traps": {"a": "Concluding that a therapy works when it does not is a Type I error (a false positive): rejecting a true null, with probability alpha.", "b": "Failing to reject a true null is a correct decision, with probability 1 minus alpha; a Type II error requires the null to be false.", "c": "This muddles alpha, which is itself the chosen Type I error rate; the statement describes neither error, and certainly not a Type II error."}}, {"q": "A test item shows a high difficulty index (p = 0.85) and a negative discrimination index. What is the most likely interpretation?", "concept": "The answer is d: p = 0.85 means the item is easy, and a negative discrimination index means high scorers fail it more often than low scorers, which usually signals a miskeyed or flawed item. The difficulty index, p, is simply the proportion of examinees who answer the item correctly. It is really an easiness index: the higher the p, the easier the item, so at p = .85, 85 percent passed. Items of middle difficulty (around .50, a little higher for multiple-choice items because of guessing) spread scores best. The discrimination index, D, compares the top and bottom groups on the total test, classically the upper and lower 27 percent recommended by Truman Kelley (1939): D is the proportion correct in the upper group minus the proportion correct in the lower group. D runs from minus 1 to plus 1. A positive D, ideally .30 or more, means the item separates strong from weak candidates; a D near zero means it does not discriminate. A negative D means weaker students do better than stronger ones, the reverse of what a good item does. The usual causes are a scoring error (the wrong option marked as correct), an ambiguous stem, two defensible answers, or a trick that misleads students who know the subject in depth. Suppose Meera analyses her class test. On item 12, 70 percent of the top group but 95 percent of the bottom group answered correctly, so D = .70 minus .95 = minus .25. She checks and finds that the scoring sheet says b when the correct answer is c. Option d is right because it reads both numbers correctly: an easy item with reversed discrimination, so check the scoring and revise or drop it. Exam trap: \"high difficulty index\" sounds like \"very difficult\", but p counts passes, so a high p means easy.", "traps": {"a": "It is right that the item is too easy, but a negative D means it discriminates in the wrong direction, not better; such an item lowers the validity of the whole test.", "b": "This reads p backwards: p = .85 means 85 percent passed, so the item is easy, and an item with negative discrimination should be revised or dropped, not retained.", "c": "p = .85 is easy, not moderate (moderate is around .50), and a negative D is the poorest possible discrimination, not excellent."}}, {"q": "Which feature most differentiates military selection tests from civilian aptitude tests?", "concept": "The answer is b: what most sets military selection tests apart is their reliance on group-administered, timed tests that can screen huge numbers of recruits quickly. Group testing itself was born of military need. When the United States entered the First World War, Robert Mearns Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, led a committee that built the Army Alpha and Army Beta. Alpha was a verbal, paper-and-pencil test for literate recruits, with items on following directions, arithmetic, analogies and general information. Beta was a pictorial, non-verbal test for recruits who were illiterate or did not speak English, explained by mime and demonstration. About 1.75 million men were tested, many in large halls at once, with strict time limits and quick scoring stencils. The results sorted recruits into ranks, training streams and officer candidates within days, something no individually given Binet-style test could do. The same logic continues in the Armed Services Vocational Aptitude Battery (ASVAB) used by the US military today. Civilian aptitude tests usually serve guidance and selection for education or particular jobs, where a detailed profile of the individual matters more than speed of deployment. Imagine Rohan sitting a services entrance test in a hall with 400 other candidates: identical printed booklets, a stopwatch, machine-scored answer sheets and results within days. Option b is right because mass, fast, standardised group screening is the defining feature that military testing introduced and still emphasises. Exam trap: Indian students know that the Services Selection Board (SSB) uses a picture-story test and word association for officer candidates, so option c can tempt; those projective-style tests come after group screening and are not what distinguishes military testing as a whole.", "traps": {"a": "Crystallised intelligence (Raymond Cattell, 1963) is not the military hallmark; Army tests stressed quick screening of general ability, and civilian scholastic tests draw just as heavily on acquired knowledge.", "c": "Projective techniques such as the Rorschach and the TAT are slow to give and score individually, so they cannot drive mass screening; where they are used, as in later SSB stages, they only supplement group tests.", "d": "Interest inventories such as the Strong Vocational Interest Blank (Edward Strong, 1927) belong mainly to civilian vocational guidance; military selection prioritises general cognitive ability."}}, {"q": "During the propagation of an action potential, which event ensures the unidirectional flow of the neural impulse along the axon?", "concept": "The answer is a: the absolute refractory period, caused by inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels, ensures that an action potential travels only one way along the axon. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out the ionic basis of the action potential from the squid giant axon. When the membrane at one point reaches threshold, voltage-gated Na+ channels open and sodium rushes in, driving the inside positive. Within about a millisecond these channels inactivate: an inactivation gate plugs them, and they cannot reopen until the membrane has repolarised. For roughly 1 to 2 milliseconds after a spike begins, no stimulus, however strong, can fire that patch again. This is the absolute refractory period. Now follow the impulse. Current from the active patch spreads passively in both directions. Ahead lies resting membrane, which is pushed to threshold and fires. Behind lies the patch that has just fired, whose Na+ channels are still inactivated, so the spreading current cannot trigger it. The spike therefore moves forward only, from axon hillock to terminal buttons, like a flame along a Diwali fuse that cannot burn back over the part already burnt. The same period also limits how fast a neuron can fire. If an axon is stimulated artificially in the middle, spikes run in both directions, which shows that direction depends on where firing starts and on refractoriness, not on any one-way property of the axon itself. Option a is right because sodium channel inactivation makes the membrane just behind the spike temporarily unexcitable. Exam trap: the relative refractory period also follows the spike, so option b tempts; but during it a strong enough stimulus can still fire the membrane, so it cannot guarantee one-way travel, and it reflects open potassium channels rather than inactivated sodium channels.", "traps": {"b": "The relative refractory period, when K+ channels are still open and the membrane is hyperpolarised, only raises the threshold; a strong stimulus can still fire, so it limits firing frequency rather than fixing direction.", "c": "Saltatory conduction, the node-to-node jumping of the impulse in myelinated axons, increases speed; its direction is still set by the refractory node just behind, and unmyelinated axons conduct one way too.", "d": "Temporal summation of EPSPs at the axon hillock decides whether an action potential starts, by adding inputs over time; it says nothing about which way the spike travels once it has begun."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims to increase psychological flexibility. Reason (R): Because ACT focuses on eliminating distressing thoughts through direct cognitive restructuring.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false, because acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) does not try to eliminate distressing thoughts or restructure them. Step 1, the Assertion: true. Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999) developed ACT, a third-wave behaviour therapy, and its stated aim is psychological flexibility: staying in contact with the present moment and acting on one's values whatever thoughts and feelings are present. It builds this through six processes, drawn as the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment contact, self-as-context, values and committed action. Step 2, the Reason: false. ACT rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of language, and argues that the struggle to get rid of unwanted thoughts and feelings, called experiential avoidance, is itself the problem. Thought suppression backfires, as the white bear studies of Daniel Wegner (1987) showed: people told not to think of a white bear kept thinking of it, and thought of it even more afterwards. So ACT changes the person's relationship to a thought, not its content. Defusion teaches clients to see a thought as just words, for instance by saying \"I am having the thought that I will fail\". Direct cognitive restructuring, examining the evidence for a thought and replacing it with a more balanced one, belongs to the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1979). Step 3, the link, does not arise because R is false. Take Meera, who thinks \"I will embarrass myself\" before every seminar. A cognitive therapist would test the evidence for that prediction; an ACT therapist helps her notice the thought, make room for the anxiety, and present anyway because teaching matters to her. Option c is right because A states ACT's real aim while R wrongly gives ACT the method of Beck's therapy. Exam trap: both therapies sit under the CBT umbrella, so examiners swap their techniques; elimination and disputing belong to the second wave, acceptance and defusion to the third.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R true and explaining A; it fails at R, because ACT does not aim to eliminate or restructure thoughts but to accept them and defuse from them.", "b": "This needs both statements true; it fails because R is false: direct cognitive restructuring is the method of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, not ACT.", "d": "This calls A false; but increasing psychological flexibility is the stated central aim of ACT and the hub of its hexaflex."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In a repeated measures ANOVA, violation of the sphericity assumption can bias results. Reason (R): Because violation of sphericity inflates the Type I error rate.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and the inflated Type I error rate is exactly how a sphericity violation biases a repeated measures ANOVA. In a repeated measures design the same people are tested under three or more conditions, and the univariate F test assumes sphericity: the variances of the difference scores between every pair of conditions are equal. Step 1, the Assertion: true. When sphericity fails, the F ratio no longer follows the F distribution with the usual degrees of freedom, so its p values are wrong. John Mauchly (1940) gave the standard test for it. Step 2, the Reason: true. The F test becomes too liberal: with alpha set at .05, the real chance of a false positive can be noticeably higher. Step 3, the link: R explains A. The bias the Assertion speaks of is precisely this positive bias in F and the extra Type I errors it causes. That is why corrections shrink the degrees of freedom by an estimate called epsilon: the Greenhouse-Geisser correction (Samuel Greenhouse and Seymour Geisser, 1959), which is more conservative, and the Huynh-Feldt correction (Huynh Huynh and Leonard Feldt, 1976), which is less so. A multivariate approach (MANOVA), which does not assume sphericity, is another route. Take Arjun, who measures anxiety in the same clients at baseline, week 4, week 8 and month 6. Differences between time points far apart vary more than differences between adjacent ones, so Mauchly's test is significant. His uncorrected p is .04, but after the Greenhouse-Geisser correction it is .09; reporting the first would risk a false positive. Option a is right because Type I inflation is the mechanism behind the bias. Exam trap: with only two conditions there is just one set of difference scores, so sphericity is automatically met; and Bartlett's test of sphericity (Maurice Bartlett, 1950), used before factor analysis, is a different test altogether.", "traps": {"b": "This says R does not explain A; it fails because the bias in A is exactly the positively biased F ratio that inflates Type I error, so R is the explanation.", "c": "This calls R false; but a violation makes the F test liberal, pushing the actual Type I error rate above the nominal alpha, which is why the Greenhouse-Geisser and Huynh-Feldt corrections exist.", "d": "This calls A false; but sphericity is a core assumption of the univariate repeated measures ANOVA, and Mauchly's test is routinely checked before F is interpreted."}}, {"q": "Which mechanism best explains synaptic facilitation observed during high-frequency stimulation?", "concept": "The answer is a: synaptic facilitation during high-frequency stimulation is explained by residual calcium in the presynaptic terminal, which raises the probability that vesicles will be released. Ordinary transmission starts when an action potential reaches the terminal button and opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; calcium entry makes vesicles fuse with the membrane and release transmitter (exocytosis). Release is probabilistic: each spike releases only some of the vesicles that are ready. After the spike, calcium is pumped out and buffered, but that takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds. If a second spike arrives before clearance is complete, its fresh calcium adds to what is left over, so the calcium level near the release sites is higher than after the first spike, and more vesicles are released. Bernard Katz and Ricardo Miledi (1968) proposed this residual calcium hypothesis from work at the frog neuromuscular junction. With two stimuli close together the effect is called paired-pulse facilitation; during a high-frequency train it builds up and is joined by augmentation and post-tetanic potentiation, which last seconds to minutes. This is short-term plasticity: presynaptic, fast and transient. Suppose Riya, in a physiology practical, stimulates a nerve twice, 20 milliseconds apart. The second postsynaptic potential is clearly larger than the first, though nothing has changed postsynaptically; that is facilitation. At synapses that already release most of their ready vesicles, the opposite, depression from vesicle depletion, can dominate instead. Option a is right because the extra release comes from accumulated presynaptic calcium, not from any change in receptors. Exam trap: option b names long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973), which also follows high-frequency stimulation; but LTP lasts hours to weeks and depends mainly on postsynaptic NMDA receptor activation and added AMPA receptors, whereas facilitation fades within about a second.", "traps": {"b": "Long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973) is a lasting change, hours to weeks, driven largely by NMDA receptors and insertion of postsynaptic AMPA receptors; facilitation is presynaptic and lasts milliseconds.", "c": "Autoreceptor activation (for example presynaptic D2 or alpha-2 receptors) reduces transmitter release, a negative feedback that would produce depression, not facilitation.", "d": "Desensitisation makes postsynaptic receptors less responsive to repeated transmitter, so it weakens the response; it cannot produce hyperexcitability or facilitation."}}, {"q": "A key limitation of retrospective cohort studies compared to prospective cohort studies is:", "concept": "The answer is b: retrospective cohort studies rely on records made in the past, often for other purposes, so data may be incomplete or inconsistent, and any exposure information gathered from memory brings recall bias. A cohort study classifies people who do not yet have the outcome as exposed or unexposed and compares how many in each group develop it, which yields incidence and relative risk. In a prospective cohort the researcher identifies the groups now and follows them into the future, measuring exposure in a planned, uniform way. The British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill (1951) and the Framingham Heart Study (from 1948) are classics. In a retrospective (historical) cohort the researcher starts today but uses existing records to identify who was exposed years ago, then traces what happened up to the present. The reasoning still runs from exposure to outcome; only the data collection looks back. The price is data quality. The researcher cannot choose what was measured, so key variables and confounders may be missing, recorded differently by different staff, or lost; and if exposure has to be filled in by interviewing people, their memories may be coloured by what has since happened to them. Take Kabir, who uses a hospital's 2010 to 2015 neonatal records to compare children who had severe newborn jaundice with those who did not, then checks school records for learning difficulties. Many files lack birth weight or treatment details, and parents asked to recall feeding problems may remember selectively. Option b is right because incomplete records and recall bias are the characteristic weaknesses of looking back. Exam trap: recall bias is classically tied to case-control studies, so students hesitate; it also threatens retrospective cohorts whenever exposure data come from memory.", "traps": {"a": "Cohort designs are actually the design of choice for rare exposures, such as an occupational cohort of asbestos workers; it is case-control studies that suit rare outcomes.", "c": "Because a retrospective cohort still starts from exposure and counts new cases, it can compute incidence and relative risk; it is the case-control design that cannot measure incidence.", "d": "This is reversed: retrospective cohorts are cheaper and quicker because the outcomes have already occurred, while prospective cohorts demand years of follow-up and large budgets."}}, {"q": "According to the Geneplore Model (Finke, Ward, & Smith), the creative process involves:", "concept": "The answer is d: in the Geneplore model, creative thinking cycles between a generative phase that builds rough mental 'pre-inventive structures' and an exploratory phase that interprets and refines them. Ronald Finke, Thomas Ward and Steven Smith (1992) set out the model in their book Creative Cognition. Its name joins 'generate' and 'explore'. In the generative phase a person retrieves, associates, combines and transforms ideas to form a pre-inventive structure: a half-formed shape, a mental blueprint, an odd word combination or a category example that is not yet an invention. In the exploratory phase the person studies that structure, finds new attributes in it, imagines what it could be used for, shifts it into new contexts and tests its limits. If the result fails, the person goes back and generates again, so the two phases alternate until a product emerges. Constraints (for example, 'it must be furniture') can be added at either phase. In Ronald Finke's creative imagery experiments, people mentally combined a few simple shapes into a form and only then interpreted it as an invention; forms interpreted this way were often judged more creative than ones built with the purpose fixed in advance. Example: Kabir, a design student, mentally joins a cylinder, a hook and a flat disc, then explores the shape and sees a hanging lamp. When it looks unstable, he goes back and builds a new form. Option d is right because it names the model's own two phases and its central term, pre-inventive structures. Exam trap: option a describes the divergent and convergent thinking of Joy Paul Guilford (1950), which sounds like 'generate, then evaluate' but uses Guilford's terms, not the Geneplore model's. Geneplore is a cognitive account, so brain-only and unconscious-only explanations do not fit it.", "traps": {"a": "This is Joy Paul Guilford's (1950) contrast between divergent thinking (producing many ideas) and convergent thinking (narrowing to one best answer). It resembles a generate and evaluate cycle, but Geneplore speaks of generative and exploratory phases acting on pre-inventive structures, and exploring means interpreting, not just judging.", "b": "This reflects the popular 'right brain is creative' myth. Creative thinking draws on networks spread across both hemispheres, and Geneplore is a cognitive process model that says nothing about one hemisphere working alone.", "c": "This echoes romantic and psychoanalytic views of inspiration welling up from the unconscious. Geneplore is a creative cognition model in which every step, from combining to interpreting, is an ordinary cognitive process, so 'without cognitive mediation' contradicts it."}}, {"q": "Which observation most specifically reflects a thought process abnormality rather than a thought content abnormality in MSE?", "concept": "The answer is c: tangential speech is a disorder of thought process (the form or stream of thought), because it shows how ideas are linked, not what they are about. The mental status examination (MSE) splits thinking into two parts. Thought process, also called the form or stream of thought, describes how ideas connect and flow: their speed, their logical links and whether they reach a goal. Its disorders include flight of ideas, loosening of associations, tangentiality, circumstantiality, thought block and perseveration. Thought content describes what the person is thinking about: delusions, obsessions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations, suicidal ideas and themes such as guilt or worthlessness. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, made the split between form and content a basic tool of descriptive psychopathology. Nancy Andreasen (1979) later defined tangentiality as replying to a question in an oblique or irrelevant way, so the answer drifts off and never returns to the point. It differs from circumstantiality, in which the speaker takes long detours but finally reaches the goal. Example: Meera is asked, 'Where do you live?' She replies, 'Near the beach; the sea reminds me of my school trip; the teachers were very strict then; discipline is important for children...' and never names her town. The clinician records this under thought process, whatever the topic happens to be. Option c is right because tangentiality is defined by the broken path between ideas, not by any belief or theme. Exam trap: hallucinations look like a 'thought' symptom but belong to perception, while delusions and depressive ideas belong to thought content; examiners also swap tangentiality (never reaches the goal) with circumstantiality (gets there slowly).", "traps": {"a": "Hearing voices that command action is a command auditory hallucination, a disorder of perception recorded under perceptual disturbances in the MSE. It is neither a thought process nor a thought content abnormality.", "b": "A fixed false belief that neighbours are plotting is a persecutory delusion, the classic thought content abnormality. It tells us what the patient thinks, not how the ideas are linked.", "d": "Persistent ideas of worthlessness and guilt are depressive thought content (they can become mood-congruent delusions if held with fixed conviction). The abnormality lies in the theme, not in the flow of thinking."}}, {"q": "According to Rowe and Kahn's model of successful aging, which of the following best captures the three central criteria?", "concept": "The answer is a: John Rowe and Robert Kahn (1997) defined successful ageing by three criteria: low probability of disease and disability, high cognitive and physical functioning, and active engagement with life. Rowe, a physician, and Kahn, a social psychologist, led the MacArthur Foundation research network on ageing. In 1987, in the journal Science, they argued that research had studied only 'usual' ageing and wrongly treated common losses as inevitable. In 1997 they set out the full model: successful ageing needs (1) avoiding disease and disease-related disability, (2) maintaining high mental and physical function, and (3) active engagement with life, meaning close relationships with others and productive activity, paid or unpaid. The three overlap, and successful ageing lies where all three meet. They stressed that lifestyle factors such as exercise, diet and social ties shape late life more than people assume, so much decline is not fixed by age alone. Critics replied that the model labels most people with chronic illness as 'unsuccessful', ignores what older people themselves value and overlooks poverty and social inequality. Paul Baltes and Margret Baltes (1990) offered a process alternative, selective optimisation with compensation. Example: Arjun's grandfather, 74, has no diabetes or heart disease, walks five kilometres daily, solves crosswords and teaches village children twice a week. He meets all three criteria. His neighbour, equally active and cheerful but living with arthritis, fails the first criterion, which is exactly the critics' point. Option a is right because it lists Rowe and Kahn's three components in their own order and wording. Exam trap: examiners mix in Erik Erikson's (1950) integrity, Lars Tornstam's (1989) gerotranscendence or the Baltes model, all real ideas about ageing that are not Rowe and Kahn's criteria.", "traps": {"b": "Accepting decline, spiritual fulfilment and links between generations echo Erik Erikson's (1950) integrity and generativity and Lars Tornstam's (1989) gerotranscendence. Rowe and Kahn's model is about avoiding decline, not accepting it.", "c": "Genetic resilience, plasticity and enriched environments sound like the biology of ageing and Paul Baltes's work on cognitive plasticity. Rowe and Kahn in fact played down genes, and none of these is one of their three criteria.", "d": "Economic independence, vitality and low distress make a plausible list of good outcomes, but money and mood are not Rowe and Kahn's criteria, and 'active engagement with life' is missing."}}, {"q": "According to Murray Bowen's Family Systems Theory, which of the following best represents the concept of differentiation of self?", "concept": "The answer is a: differentiation of self is the capacity to keep one's own thinking, feelings and identity while staying emotionally connected to the family. Murray Bowen (1913 to 1990) built family systems theory from his 1950s work at the National Institute of Mental Health, where whole families of patients with schizophrenia lived on the ward. He saw the family as an emotional unit in which anxiety passes from member to member. Differentiation of self, his master concept, has two sides. Inside the person, it is the ability to separate thinking from feeling, so that one can choose a response instead of reacting automatically. Between people, it is the ability to stay oneself while in close contact: neither fused with the family (absorbing its anxiety and opinions) nor cut off from it (cold distance). Bowen pictured a scale from low to high differentiation; people low on it are reactive, seek approval and pull others into triangles under stress. His other concepts include triangles, the nuclear family emotional process, the family projection process, multigenerational transmission, emotional cutoff, sibling position and societal emotional process. A Bowenian therapist stays calm, avoids being drawn into triangles, coaches each member to take 'I-positions' and maps three generations in a genogram. Example: Riya, 25, wants to study psychology while her parents want medicine. She explains her view calmly, listens to their worries and keeps visiting as usual; she neither storms out nor gives in to keep the peace. That is high differentiation. Option a is right because it captures both halves of the concept: autonomy and continued connection. Exam trap: emotional distance looks like independence but is emotional cutoff, a sign of poor differentiation, and 'enmeshed boundaries' is a term from the structural family therapy of Salvador Minuchin (1974), not Bowen.", "traps": {"b": "Projecting parents' unresolved anxiety onto a child is Bowen's family projection process, a separate one of his eight concepts. It describes how low differentiation is passed down, not what differentiation is.", "c": "Marital stress spilling into a child's symptoms is one pattern of Bowen's nuclear family emotional process (and of triangulation), again a different concept from differentiation of self.", "d": "Enmeshment and boundaries come from Salvador Minuchin's (1974) structural family therapy. Rigid enmeshment would mean low differentiation in Bowen's terms, the opposite of the concept asked."}}, {"q": "'Item Response Theory (IRT)' in psychological testing is an advanced approach that aims to:", "concept": "The answer is b: item response theory models how a person's level on a latent trait (ability) relates to the probability of answering each item correctly. Classical test theory (CTT), going back to Charles Spearman (1904), treats an observed score as true score plus error (X = T + E) and describes items by indices such as difficulty (p, the proportion passing) that depend on the sample tested. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, models each item instead. Frederic Lord (1952) laid its foundations, and Georg Rasch (1960) published the one-parameter Rasch model. The core tool is the item characteristic curve: an S-shaped curve plotting the probability of a correct response against ability, written θ (theta). The one-parameter model uses item difficulty (b, the ability at which the chance of success is 0.50); the two-parameter model adds discrimination (a, the steepness of the curve); the three-parameter model adds guessing (c, the lower floor of the curve). Because item parameters are, in principle, independent of the sample, and ability estimates independent of the particular items, IRT supports test equating, item banks, checks for item bias and computerised adaptive testing. Example: in an adaptive test, Meera answers a medium item correctly, so the computer gives her a harder one; she fails it, so the next is slightly easier. After about 15 items her θ is estimated about as precisely as a much longer fixed test would manage. Option b is right because modelling the link between ability and the probability of success on each item is the definition of IRT. Exam trap: examiners offer CTT ideas (a total score, Cronbach's alpha, norms) as if they were IRT aims; IRT does yield a score, but its aim is the item-level model.", "traps": {"a": "Producing a single total score is what classical test theory does by adding item scores. IRT also estimates a score (θ), but its defining aim is to model how each item behaves across ability levels.", "c": "Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is a classical test theory index of internal consistency. IRT replaces a single reliability figure with a test information function that shows precision at each ability level.", "d": "Building norms from a representative standardisation sample belongs to norm-referenced test construction. It places a score against a group, which is not what IRT models."}}, {"q": "When conducting a 'Phenomenological Study', the researcher's 'bracketing' or 'epoche' is crucial because it assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is b: bracketing (epoché) means the researcher sets aside their own assumptions so that participants' lived experience can be understood as they live it. Phenomenology studies how a phenomenon appears in a person's own experience: what it is like to grieve, to hear voices or to care for a parent with dementia. Edmund Husserl (1913) gave the method its key step, the epoché, from the Greek for suspension or holding back. The researcher first becomes aware of their beliefs, theories and personal history about the topic, then suspends them, like putting them in brackets in an equation, so that the description is not forced into a ready-made frame. Clark Moustakas (1994) turned this into a research procedure: the researcher writes out their own experience of the topic, collects participants' accounts, treats every statement as equally important at first (horizontalisation), groups statements into themes and writes a textural description (what was experienced) and a structural description (how it was experienced) that lead to the essence. Martin Heidegger (1927) replied that no one can fully step out of their own world, so interpretative versions such as Jonathan Smith's interpretative phenomenological analysis (1996) ask instead for honest reflexivity about the researcher's influence. Example: Riya, whose father died when she was 18, studies young widows in Kerala. She notes in a journal her belief that grief is mostly sadness, holds it aside in interviews, and hears relief, anger and money fears she might otherwise have missed. Option b is right because suspending one's own biases in order to grasp participants' lived experience is exactly what bracketing is for. Exam trap: option d also sounds like 'setting aside', but bracketing does not say the researcher's experience is irrelevant; it says that experience is influential enough to be named and held in check.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses bracketing. Phenomenology treats preconceptions as a threat to faithful description, so they are suspended, not used to 'enhance objectivity'.", "c": "Imposing the researcher's interpretations is exactly what the epoché guards against. Even interpretative phenomenology asks the researcher to stay close to the participant's meanings, not to override them.", "d": "Bracketing assumes the researcher's experience matters a great deal, which is why it must be made explicit and suspended. If it were irrelevant, bracketing would be pointless."}}, {"q": "The 'Social Readjustment Rating Scale' (Holmes & Rahe) is a tool used to measure:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Social Readjustment Rating Scale measures life stress, as the amount of life change a person has faced, and was built to predict later illness. Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe (1967) started from the idea that any major change, pleasant or unpleasant, demands readjustment, and that heavy readjustment strains the body. They asked hundreds of people to rate 43 life events against marriage, which was fixed at 50. Each event received a value in life change units (LCU): death of a spouse 100, divorce 73, marriage 50, change in residence 20, holiday 13 and minor violations of the law 11. A person ticks the events of the past year and the units are summed. Later work by Holmes and colleagues linked totals of about 300 or more with a much higher chance of illness in the following year or two, and Rahe found similar links in studies of naval crews, though the correlations were modest. Critics noted that the scale mixes good and bad events, ignores how a person appraises an event (the transactional model of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman, 1984), relies on memory, and confuses causes with consequences, since illness can itself cause events such as job loss. Allen Kanner, Lazarus and colleagues (1981) found that daily hassles predicted symptoms better than major life events. Example: in one year Kabir marries (50), moves house (20) and takes a holiday (13), a total of 83 LCU, even though two of these events made him happy. Option b is right because the SRRS quantifies stressful life change in order to estimate its health impact. Exam trap: the SRRS counts events, not personality, support or coping, and because change itself is the stressor, positive events such as marriage and holidays carry scores too.", "traps": {"a": "Personality traits are measured by inventories such as Raymond Cattell's (1949) 16PF or the NEO Personality Inventory of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985). The SRRS asks what has happened to you, not what you are like.", "c": "Social support is measured by tools such as Irwin Sarason's (1983) Social Support Questionnaire. Support buffers stress in later models, but the SRRS does not measure it.", "d": "Emotional intelligence, the ability to perceive, use, understand and manage emotions (Peter Salovey and John Mayer, 1990), is assessed by ability tests and questionnaires, not by counting life events."}}, {"q": "The 'Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)' approach to treating anxiety disorders implicitly assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is b: CBT for anxiety assumes that anxious thoughts (overestimating danger, underestimating coping) and avoidance behaviours keep anxiety going. Aaron Beck, with Gary Emery and Ruth Greenberg (1985), described anxiety as a response to perceived threat: the anxious person overestimates the probability and cost of danger and underestimates their ability to cope. David Clark (1986) applied this to panic disorder: the person misreads normal bodily sensations, such as a racing heart, as signs of catastrophe, such as a heart attack, which raises fear and the sensations further in a vicious circle. Behaviour then keeps the cycle alive. O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory explained that fear is learnt by classical conditioning and maintained by avoidance, because escape brings relief (negative reinforcement). Paul Salkovskis (1991) added that subtle safety behaviours, such as gripping a chair 'to stop fainting', stop the person from discovering that the feared outcome would not have happened. CBT therefore targets both: cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments test the thoughts, and graded exposure ends avoidance until fear falls and the feared prediction is disconfirmed. Example: Meera fears she will faint during a seminar. She thinks 'my pounding heart means I will collapse', holds the desk, avoids eye contact and skips seminars when she can. Her therapist helps her weigh the evidence and then present without holding the desk; she does not faint, and her belief weakens. Option b is right because the maintaining role of maladaptive thoughts and avoidance is the central working assumption of CBT for anxiety. Exam trap: absolute words ('solely', 'purely', 'always') mark the distractors, and option d reverses CBT's logic, since avoidance is the problem and exposure the cure.", "traps": {"a": "Unconscious conflict as the source of anxiety is the psychodynamic account of Sigmund Freud (1926), in which anxiety signals a threatening impulse. CBT works with conscious, testable thoughts and behaviour, and 'solely' rules this option out anyway.", "c": "CBT does not treat anxiety as purely a thought running alongside the body. Its model links thoughts, emotions, bodily sensations and behaviour in a two-way cycle; separate parallel tracks sound more like the Cannon-Bard theory of emotion (Walter Cannon, 1927).", "d": "This is the opposite of CBT. Avoidance maintains anxiety, so planned, graded exposure to feared situations is one of its main tools."}}, {"q": "'Motivational Interviewing' is a client-centered counseling style for eliciting behavior change by helping clients to explore and resolve:", "concept": "The answer is d: motivational interviewing helps clients explore and resolve ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting to change at the same time. William Miller (1983) first described motivational interviewing (MI) for problem drinkers and developed it with Stephen Rollnick in their book Motivational Interviewing (1991). Its starting point is that most people stuck in a harmful habit are not simply 'in denial'; they hold two sets of reasons at once. When a counsellor argues for change (the 'righting reflex'), the client defends the other side and talks themselves further into staying the same. MI instead draws out the client's own reasons. The counsellor uses OARS skills (open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries), develops discrepancy between present behaviour and the client's deeper goals, rolls with resistance and supports self-efficacy. The aim is to strengthen change talk (desire, ability, reasons and need to change, then commitment) and soften sustain talk. Its spirit is partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation. MI fits the stages of change of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983), especially for people in precontemplation and contemplation, and is widely used for alcohol, drugs, treatment adherence and health behaviour. Example: Arjun says, 'I know drinking is hurting my liver, but it is the only way I relax after work.' The counsellor reflects both sides: 'Part of you needs a way to unwind, and part of you is worried about your health.' Arjun then lists his own reasons to cut down, which carry more weight than any lecture. Option d is right because resolving ambivalence is the defining purpose of MI in Miller and Rollnick's own description. Exam trap: MI is client-centred yet directive, and it belongs to Miller and Rollnick, not to Prochaska and DiClemente, whose model only describes the stages of readiness.", "traps": {"a": "Exploring unconscious conflicts is the work of psychodynamic therapy from Sigmund Freud (1900) onwards. MI works with conscious, spoken reasons for and against change.", "b": "Disputing irrational beliefs is the core of Albert Ellis's (1955) rational emotive behaviour therapy. MI avoids disputing and arguing, because arguing increases sustain talk.", "c": "Processing past traumas is the aim of trauma-focused therapies such as prolonged exposure or Francine Shapiro's (1989) EMDR. MI focuses on the present and future of one specific behaviour change."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A non-significant result in hypothesis testing always means that the null hypothesis is true. Reason (R): Because the absence of statistical significance indicates absolute absence of an effect in the population.", "concept": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false, because a non-significant result only means we failed to reject the null hypothesis (H0), not that H0 is true. Step 1, the Assertion on its own: a test asks how likely data this extreme would be if H0 were true. If p is above alpha (say .05), the data are compatible with H0, so we 'fail to reject' or 'retain' it. That is not proof. Ronald Fisher (1935) wrote that the null hypothesis is never proved or established, but is possibly disproved. A real effect can be missed because the sample is small, the measure is noisy or the effect is modest: this is a Type II error, with probability beta. Jacob Cohen (1962) showed that the power (1 minus beta) of many published psychology studies was low. So A is false. Step 2, the Reason on its own: 'absence of significance indicates absolute absence of an effect' repeats the same error. Douglas Altman and Martin Bland (1995) put it simply: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. To claim no meaningful effect, a researcher needs a confidence interval narrow enough to exclude important effects, or an equivalence test. So R is also false. Step 3: since neither is true, the question of explanation does not arise. Example: Kabir tests a yoga programme on 12 students and gets p = .20; the confidence interval runs from a small harm to a large benefit, so the study cannot tell. Option d is right because it is the only option that rejects the Assertion, which is the point being tested. Exam trap: 'retain H0' is easily misread as 'accept H0 as true'. Key note: strictly both statements are false and the paper offers no 'both false' option; d is the only option that correctly rejects the Assertion, so d is used here. LBS's revised key for this paper (18 Oct 2025) deleted this question for exactly this reason, so no one was marked on it.", "traps": {"a": "This needs both statements true and linked. The Assertion fails, because non-significance never proves H0, so the whole option collapses.", "b": "This also claims both statements are true. Neither is: the Assertion confuses failing to reject H0 with proving it, and the Reason repeats that confusion.", "c": "This accepts the Assertion as true, which is the central error being tested; a non-significant p can come from low power and a Type II error."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Factor analysis helps in identifying underlying latent variables from a large set of observed variables. Reason (R): Because it maximizes within-factor variance and minimizes between-factor variance.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, because factor analysis does uncover latent variables from many observed ones, but the Reason misdescribes how it works. Step 1, the Assertion on its own: true. Factor analysis starts from the correlations among many measured variables and asks how few underlying factors could produce them. Charles Spearman (1904) invented it to argue for a general intelligence factor, g, and Louis Thurstone (1931) extended it to several factors, aiming at simple structure, in which each variable loads highly on one factor and near zero on the rest. A factor loading is a variable's correlation with a factor; a factor's eigenvalue is the variance it explains. Factor analysis models the shared (common) variance among variables, so variables that correlate highly end up on the same factor. Step 2, the Reason on its own: false. 'Maximising within-factor variance and minimising between-factor variance' borrows the language of separating groups of cases, as in cluster analysis or ANOVA-style comparisons (and even there the aim runs the other way: small spread within groups, large differences between them). Factor analysis does not sort people into groups. The confusion probably comes from varimax rotation (Henry Kaiser, 1958), which maximises the variance of the squared loadings on each factor to sharpen simple structure; that is a rotation rule, not the reason factors are found. Step 3: since R is false, it cannot explain A. Example: Riya gives a 20-item anxiety scale to 400 students. Racing heart, sweating and trembling correlate with one another and load on a 'somatic' factor, while worry and dread items load on a 'cognitive' factor. Option c is right because A is true and R is false. Exam trap: examiners borrow cluster and ANOVA vocabulary to make a wrong reason sound technical.", "traps": {"a": "This requires R to be true and to explain A. R describes group-separation logic, not how factor analysis extracts common variance, so it explains nothing.", "b": "This claims R is true. R is false: factor analysis groups correlated variables by their shared variance; it does not maximise within-factor and minimise between-factor variance.", "d": "This rejects A, yet A is a textbook definition of factor analysis; and R, the part this option accepts, is the false statement."}}, {"q": "The 'Biological Basis of Motivation' (e.g., hunger, thirst) implicitly assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is b: biological theories of motivation assume that physiological needs and homeostatic mechanisms drive certain basic behaviours such as eating and drinking. Claude Bernard (1813 to 1878) described the milieu intérieur, the stable internal environment of the body. Walter Cannon (1926) named the process that keeps it stable homeostasis, and explained it in The Wisdom of the Body (1932): like a thermostat, the body detects a departure from a set point (temperature, blood glucose, fluid balance) and triggers corrections. Clark Hull (1943) built drive-reduction theory on this idea: a tissue need creates a drive, an unpleasant state of arousal that pushes the organism to act, and any behaviour that reduces the drive is reinforced. Primary drives such as hunger and thirst come from such needs; secondary drives, such as learned fear, are acquired. Hunger is regulated by the hypothalamus, which reads signals such as blood glucose, ghrelin from the stomach and leptin from fat cells. Thirst has two triggers: osmotic thirst, when salt concentration rises and osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus respond, and hypovolaemic thirst, when blood volume falls, as after sweating or bleeding. The word 'certain' matters: the biological view does not claim that all motives are biological, and learning, culture and thought still shape what, when and how much we eat. Example: after a cricket match in the Kozhikode heat, Arjun has lost fluid through sweating. Rising salt concentration and falling blood volume trigger thirst; he drinks, the drive falls and drinking is reinforced. Option b is right because it states the homeostatic assumption without overclaiming. Exam trap: the distractors use 'all', 'sole' and 'entirely'; an absolute statement about motivation is almost always wrong.", "traps": {"a": "This is the extreme environmentalist view of early behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913). Conditioning does create secondary drives and food preferences, but hunger and thirst themselves are not learned.", "c": "Cognitive theories, such as Bernard Weiner's (1985) attributional theory of motivation, add beliefs and expectations, but none claims cognition is the sole determinant, least of all for hunger and thirst.", "d": "This contradicts the homeostatic idea itself: hunger and thirst are triggered precisely by bodily states such as low glucose or high blood salt."}}, {"q": "When conducting a meta-analysis, a crucial assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is c: meta-analysis rests on the idea that there is a true underlying effect (or a mean of true effects) that combining many studies can estimate more precisely than any single study. Gene Glass (1976) coined the term meta-analysis for the statistical pooling of results from many studies. Each study's result is converted to a common effect size, such as Cohen's d or a correlation, each is weighted (usually by the inverse of its variance, so larger and more precise studies count more), and a pooled estimate with a confidence interval is computed. Mary Lee Smith and Gene Glass (1977) pooled 375 psychotherapy studies and found an average effect size of about 0.68. The fixed-effect model assumes one true effect size shared by all studies, so they differ only by sampling error. The random-effects model of Rebecca DerSimonian and Nan Laird (1986) relaxes this: true effects vary from study to study, and the analysis estimates the mean of their distribution and the spread between studies. Heterogeneity is checked with Cochran's Q and the I-squared statistic. Either way, the logic assumes there is a meaningful effect to estimate. Example: Meera pools 12 trials of CBT for social anxiety in college students. If all the trials estimate one common effect, a fixed-effect model suits; if populations and formats differ, a random-effects model estimates the average effect and how much it varies. Option c is right because estimating a true underlying effect by combining studies is the premise that defines meta-analysis. Exam trap: publication bias and unequal study quality are known problems that meta-analysts test for, not assumptions they make. Key note: b, independence of the effect sizes (for example, no sample counted twice), is also a real technical assumption of standard meta-analysis; c is the conceptual premise examiners mean, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Studies are not assumed to be of equal quality; quality varies and is rated with risk-of-bias tools and examined in sensitivity analyses. Hans Eysenck's (1978) 'garbage in, garbage out' attack on meta-analysis targeted exactly this problem.", "b": "Independence of effect sizes is a genuine technical requirement (one sample should not feed two pooled effects), but it is a statistical condition, not the premise that makes pooling meaningful.", "d": "Publication bias certainly exists; Robert Rosenthal (1979) called its extreme form the file-drawer problem, and meta-analysts look for it with funnel plots and related tests rather than assuming it away."}}, {"q": "The 'Phi Coefficient' is a measure of association used when:", "concept": "The answer is b: the phi coefficient measures the association between two dichotomous variables, each with only two categories. Phi (φ) is simply the product-moment correlation of Karl Pearson (1896) applied to two variables coded 0 and 1. The data fit a 2 by 2 table with cells a, b, c and d, and φ = (ad minus bc) divided by the square root of the product of the four marginal totals. It is also tied to chi-square: φ equals the square root of chi-square divided by N. Like r, it runs from minus 1 to plus 1 and is read the same way, though its maximum shrinks when the two variables have very different splits. Phi is meant for true (natural) dichotomies, such as alive or dead, smoker or non-smoker. The right coefficient depends on the type of each variable: Pearson's r for two continuous variables; point-biserial for one true dichotomy and one continuous variable; biserial for an artificial dichotomy (pass or fail cut from marks) with a continuous variable; tetrachoric for two artificial dichotomies; and Cramér's V (Harald Cramér, 1946) for larger nominal tables. Example: Kabir records 100 patients as smokers or non-smokers and as relapsed or not relapsed after treatment. Both are natural two-category variables, so he computes phi from the 2 by 2 table. Option b is right because phi is defined for the case where both variables are dichotomous. Exam trap: the tetrachoric coefficient also uses two dichotomies, but artificial ones with an assumed continuous, normal variable underneath; and the point-biserial, with one dichotomous and one continuous variable, is the classic near-miss.", "traps": {"a": "Two continuous variables call for Pearson's product-moment r (or Spearman's rho if the data are ranks). Phi is the special case of r when both variables have only two values.", "c": "One dichotomous and one continuous variable is the territory of the point-biserial correlation (true dichotomy) or the biserial correlation (artificial dichotomy), not phi.", "d": "Phi is not defined for an ordinal with a nominal variable. Ordinal pairs use rank coefficients such as Spearman's rho or Kendall's tau, and nominal tables larger than 2 by 2 use Cramér's V."}}, {"q": "'Neuroplasticity' is most evident in which of the following scenarios?", "concept": "The answer is b: neuroplasticity is seen most clearly when the brain recovers function after injury or rewires itself to fit new experience. Neuroplasticity is the brain's ability to change its structure and function throughout life in response to experience, learning and damage. For much of the twentieth century the adult brain was thought to be fixed; Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1852 to 1934) described adult nerve pathways as fixed and unable to regenerate. Evidence overturned this. Donald Hebb (1949) proposed that connections between neurons strengthen when the neurons are active together, and Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) found such long-term potentiation in the hippocampus. Mark Rosenzweig and colleagues in the 1960s showed that rats reared in enriched cages grew a thicker cortex. Eleanor Maguire and colleagues (2000) found that London taxi drivers had a larger posterior hippocampus, related to their years of navigating. Peter Eriksson and colleagues (1998) confirmed that new neurons form in the adult human hippocampus. After injury, neighbouring axons sprout into vacant synapses and nearby cortex takes over lost functions. Plasticity is greatest in childhood critical periods, as David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1963) showed in kittens deprived of sight in one eye, but it never switches off. Example: Arjun, 56, has a stroke that weakens his right arm. In constraint-induced movement therapy (Edward Taub, 1993) his good arm is restrained and the weak arm is trained for hours daily; its area in the motor cortex expands and use returns. Option b is right because recovery and adaptation are the observable signs of plasticity. Exam trap: critical periods mean plasticity is greater early in life, not that it is absent later; options a, c and d all restate the outdated fixed-brain view.", "traps": {"a": "A fixed adult brain is the old dogma linked with Santiago Ramón y Cajal's view of the adult nervous system. Enriched-environment, taxi-driver and stroke-recovery findings show the adult brain keeps changing.", "c": "This confuses critical periods (heightened plasticity early in life, shown by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel) with no plasticity at all; adults keep forming synapses and even some new hippocampal neurons.", "d": "Unchanging pathways would make learning impossible. Every new memory involves synaptic change such as long-term potentiation, so neural pathways change throughout life."}}, {"q": "Which of the following datasets has the highest coefficient of variation (CV)? Dataset A: Mean = 50, Standard Deviation = 5. Dataset B: Mean = 20, Standard Deviation = 8. Dataset C: Mean = 100, Standard Deviation = 10. Dataset D: Mean = 10, Standard Deviation = 2.", "concept": "The answer is b: Dataset B has the highest coefficient of variation, 40 percent. The coefficient of variation (CV), introduced by Karl Pearson (1896), expresses the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD / mean) x 100. Because it removes both the units and the size of the mean, it compares relative spread across groups with very different means or units, such as reaction time in milliseconds and body weight in kilograms. It needs ratio-scale data with a true zero. The working: Dataset A = 5 / 50 x 100 = 10 percent. Dataset B = 8 / 20 x 100 = 40 percent. Dataset C = 10 / 100 x 100 = 10 percent. Dataset D = 2 / 10 x 100 = 20 percent. Option b is right because 40 percent is the largest relative spread: B's standard deviation is two fifths of its mean. Exam trap: Dataset C has the largest raw SD (10), which tempts anyone who compares SDs directly, but its mean is also the largest, so its relative spread is only 10 percent.", "traps": {"a": "A gives 5 / 50 = 10 percent, tied with C for the lowest relative spread of the four.", "c": "C has the largest standard deviation (10), but against a mean of 100 that is only 10 percent; this option catches those who compare raw SDs.", "d": "D gives 2 / 10 = 20 percent, the second highest; a small mean raises the CV, but B's SD is a larger share of its mean."}}, {"q": "When conducting 'Ethnography' as a research method, a core assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is b: ethnography assumes that a culture or group is best understood from the inside, through prolonged immersion and observation in its natural setting. Ethnography means 'writing about a people' and grew from anthropology. Bronislaw Malinowski (1884 to 1942) lived in the Trobriand Islands from 1915 to 1918, learned the language and joined daily life; his Argonauts of the Western Pacific (1922) set the goal of grasping 'the native's point of view'. The main method is participant observation: the researcher lives or works alongside the group for months or years, takes part in everyday life, keeps detailed field notes and talks with key informants. Interviews, documents and objects add to this. Kenneth Pike (1954) contrasted emic categories (insiders' own meanings) with etic ones (an outsider's comparative categories); ethnography starts from the emic. Clifford Geertz (1973) called its aim thick description: recording behaviour together with the context that gives it meaning, so that a wink can be told from a twitch. Ethnographers accept that their presence changes what they see (reactivity), so they stay long enough for people to get used to them and reflect openly on their own role (reflexivity). Example: Riya spends a year in a fishing village near Kozhikode to understand how families talk about alcohol and 'tension'. Only after months do the women tell her things they never say to visiting doctors. Option b is right because prolonged immersion in the natural setting is the defining assumption of the method. Exam trap: complete detachment is the 'complete observer' role in the typology of Raymond Gold (1958), not the ethnographic ideal, and ethnographers never assume their presence has no effect.", "traps": {"a": "Complete detachment is the 'complete observer' role in Raymond Gold's (1958) typology of field roles. Ethnography leans towards participation, because insider understanding needs closeness.", "c": "Treating quantitative data as always superior is the positivist position; ethnography is a core qualitative tradition that values meaning and context over measurement.", "d": "Ethnographers explicitly accept reactivity, since people behave differently when watched. They manage it through a long stay and reflexivity, not by assuming it away."}}, {"q": "When using 'ANCOVA' (Analysis of Covariance) in research, a key assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is d: ANCOVA assumes that the covariate is linearly related to the dependent variable and is not itself affected by the treatment. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA), developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), blends ANOVA with regression. It compares group means on a dependent variable (DV) after statistically removing the influence of a continuous covariate, such as a pre-test score. This adjusts the group means for chance differences at baseline and shrinks error variance, so the test of the treatment gains power. Beyond the usual ANOVA assumptions (normality, equal variances, independent observations), ANCOVA adds several of its own. First, the covariate must be linearly related to the DV, because the adjustment is a straight-line regression. Second, homogeneity of regression slopes: the covariate-DV slope must be the same in every group, so there is no covariate by treatment interaction. Third, the covariate must be independent of the treatment: measured before the treatment, or at least not changed by it; otherwise adjusting for it removes part of the real treatment effect. The covariate should also be measured reliably. Example: Kabir compares three ways of teaching statistics to postgraduate students. Because they start with different maths ability, he uses a pre-course maths score as the covariate. It predicts the final exam score in a straight line, the slope is similar in all three groups, and it was measured before teaching began, so ANCOVA suits his data. Option d is right because linearity with the DV and independence from the treatment effect are core ANCOVA assumptions. Exam trap: option a reverses the logic; the covariate should correlate with the DV, not with the independent variable, because a covariate tied to the treatment confounds the comparison.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the requirement. A covariate strongly related to the independent variable confounds the groups, and adjusting for it strips away part of the treatment effect; the covariate should relate to the DV.", "b": "Unequal sample sizes are not an assumption of any test; ANCOVA can handle them, though equal groups make it more robust when other assumptions are bent.", "c": "ANCOVA needs a continuous (interval or ratio) DV. A categorical outcome calls for methods such as logistic regression or chi-square."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a 'Non-parametric test' used for comparing more than two independent groups when the data is ordinal?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Kruskal-Wallis H test is the non-parametric test for comparing three or more independent groups on ordinal data. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) designed it as a one-way ANOVA by ranks. All scores from all groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest, and the ranks are summed for each group. If the groups come from the same population, their mean ranks should be similar; the H statistic grows as the mean ranks pull apart. With reasonable group sizes, H is read against the chi-square distribution with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. Because it works on ranks, it needs no assumption of normality and suits ordinal data such as ratings or badly skewed scores, though it still assumes independent observations, and similarly shaped distributions if the result is to be read as a difference in medians. A significant H says only that at least one group differs; follow-up pairwise comparisons, such as Dunn's test (Olive Jean Dunn, 1964), show which. Example: Meera compares nurses' ratings of patient agitation (1 = calm to 5 = very agitated) across three wards. The ratings are ordinal and each ward holds different patients, so she uses the Kruskal-Wallis H test. Option b is right because it is the rank-based test built for more than two independent groups. Exam trap: match the test to two questions, how many groups and whether they are independent or related: Mann-Whitney U covers two independent groups, Wilcoxon signed-rank two related samples, Friedman three or more related samples, and ANOVA is the parametric version.", "traps": {"a": "One-way ANOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher (1925), does compare more than two independent groups, but it is parametric: it assumes interval data, normality and equal variances.", "c": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) is the rank test for only two independent groups; Kruskal-Wallis extends that logic to three or more.", "d": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) compares two related samples, such as the same people before and after treatment, not several independent groups."}}, {"q": "When conducting 'Case Studies' in research, a primary assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is b: a case study assumes that in-depth investigation of one individual, group or event can yield rich, detailed insights that other methods would miss. A case study is an intensive, usually long-term study of a single case, drawing on several sources: interviews, observation, records, tests and reports from others. Gordon Allport (1937) called this the idiographic approach, the study of the unique individual, as opposed to the nomothetic search for general laws across many people. Case studies have shaped psychology far beyond their size. Sigmund Freud's (1909) account of Little Hans fed his theory of the Oedipus complex. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) described patient H.M., whose severe amnesia after removal of both medial temporal lobes showed that these structures are needed to form new long-term memories. Phineas Gage's change in personality after his 1848 injury pointed to the role of the frontal lobes. Case studies suit rare conditions, new hypotheses and the detailed study of process. Their weaknesses are just as clear: no control, possible researcher bias, reliance on memory and limited generalisation. Robert Yin (1984) argued that a case generalises to theory (analytic generalisation), not to a population (statistical generalisation). Example: Arjun, a clinical psychology trainee, writes a case study of a 9-year-old girl with selective mutism, combining parent interviews, school reports, play observation and test results to trace how her silence developed. Option b is right because depth and detail on one case is the assumption that justifies the method. Exam trap: generalisability is the classic weakness of case studies, so option a turns their main limitation into an assumption.", "traps": {"a": "Direct generalisation to a whole population is the main limitation of case studies, not their assumption. Robert Yin (1984) argued that cases generalise to theory, not to populations.", "c": "Case studies are mainly qualitative, though they often include test scores, so they cannot assume quantitative data is always better; that is the stance of large-sample positivist research.", "d": "Objectivity matters a great deal: researcher bias is a known risk (Freud built the Little Hans case largely from the father's reports), so good case studies use multiple sources and transparent records."}}, {"q": "When interpreting the results of a 'Cross-Cultural Study' on psychological phenomena, a crucial assumption for avoiding ethnocentrism is that:", "concept": "The answer is b: avoiding ethnocentrism in cross-cultural research rests on the assumption that culture shapes how psychological phenomena are expressed and what they mean, so researchers must stay sensitive to those differences. Ethnocentrism, a term William Graham Sumner (1906) defined in Folkways, means judging other groups by the standards of one's own. In research it appears when a theory, test or category built in one culture is applied elsewhere as if it were universal. Kenneth Pike (1954) contrasted emic concepts (meanings inside a culture) with etic ones (categories used to compare cultures). John Berry (1969) warned against an 'imposed etic': taking a Western concept and treating it as a universal yardstick. Joseph Henrich, Steven Heine and Ara Norenzayan (2010) showed that most psychology samples are WEIRD (Western, educated, industrialised, rich, democratic) and often unusual by world standards. Indian work makes the point: Joan Miller (1984) found that American adults explained behaviour mainly by personality, while Hindu adults in Mysore gave more situational explanations, and the gap grew with age, so attribution style is learned from culture. Good cross-cultural studies therefore check construct, linguistic and measurement equivalence and use careful back-translation. Example: Meera adapts a Western depression scale for women in rural Kerala. Focus groups show that distress is often voiced as 'tension', tiredness or a sinking heart, so she adds these local idioms before comparing scores. Option b is right because cultural sensitivity is the safeguard against judging all cultures by one culture's norms. Exam trap: universalism in cross-cultural psychology means shared basic processes with culturally varied expression; it does not mean identical experience everywhere, which is the absolutist assumption in option a.", "traps": {"a": "Assuming constructs are defined and experienced identically everywhere is the absolutist position, and applying it uncritically is John Berry's (1969) imposed etic, the very route to ethnocentric conclusions.", "c": "Treating Western theories as universally valid is ethnocentrism itself, and the WEIRD sample problem shows why it fails.", "d": "Calling cultural differences superficial dismisses findings such as Joan Miller's (1984) Indian and American attribution study, in which culture changed how people explain behaviour."}}, {"q": "The 'Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)' measures intelligence through:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale measures intelligence through part scores: Verbal and Performance scales in its earlier editions and, from the WAIS-IV, four index scores (Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed). David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale because the Stanford-Binet suited children and leaned heavily on verbal tasks. He added a Performance scale of non-verbal tasks beside the Verbal scale and replaced the mental-age ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15 within each age group). The WAIS (1955), WAIS-R (1981) and WAIS-III (1997) gave a Verbal IQ, a Performance IQ and a Full Scale IQ. The WAIS-IV (2008; ages 16 to 90) dropped the Verbal and Performance IQs and reports a Full Scale IQ from 10 core subtests plus four indexes: Verbal Comprehension (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information), Perceptual Reasoning (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), Working Memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic) and Processing Speed (Symbol Search, Coding). Wechsler defined intelligence as a global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment, yet he measured it through many different tasks because a profile tells more than one number. Example: after a road accident, Kabir, 30, scores 108 on Verbal Comprehension but 78 on Processing Speed. His Full Scale IQ alone would hide the slowed processing that matters for his rehabilitation. Option d is right because it names both the classic Verbal and Performance structure and the modern index scores. Exam trap: the Verbal and Performance IQs belong to the WAIS up to the WAIS-III, and 'Perceptual Organisation' was the WAIS-III index name; the WAIS-IV term is Perceptual Reasoning.", "traps": {"a": "A Full Scale IQ exists, but the WAIS is built to give part scores and a profile; a single overall result is closer to the mental-age level of the early scales of Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905).", "b": "Creativity and divergent thinking are measured by tests such as Ellis Paul Torrance's (1966) Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, following Joy Paul Guilford (1950); the WAIS does not assess them.", "c": "Emotional intelligence is assessed by tools built on the model of Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990), such as the Mayer-Salovey-Caruso Emotional Intelligence Test; the WAIS has no emotional intelligence scale."}}, {"q": "When using 'Repeated Measures Design' in experimental research, a key assumption is that:", "concept": "The answer is a: a repeated measures design assumes that the order of treatments does not distort the results, or that order effects are controlled by counterbalancing. In a repeated measures (within-subjects) design every participant receives every condition and serves as their own control. Stable individual differences in ability or personality drop out of the error term, so fewer participants are needed and the test is more powerful. The price is order effects. Practice effects make later conditions look better, fatigue or boredom effects make them look worse, and carry-over effects occur when one condition leaves something behind, such as a drug still acting or a strategy still in use. If order is confounded with condition, any difference could be due to order rather than treatment. Researchers therefore counterbalance: half the participants do A then B and half B then A (or ABBA within each person), and with several conditions a Latin square places each condition once in every position. Where carry-over is severe, a washout period or a between-subjects design is used instead. Statistically, scores from the same person are correlated, and the analysis must respect this; repeated measures ANOVA also assumes sphericity, checked by John Mauchly's (1940) test and corrected by the adjustment of Samuel Greenhouse and Seymour Geisser (1959). Example: Riya tests recall of word lists in silence, with music and with traffic noise. Each of her 24 students does all three, with orders rotated by a Latin square, so no condition always comes first. Option a is right because the design is valid only if order effects are absent or balanced. Exam trap: random assignment to separate groups and one condition per person describe between-subjects designs, and 'independent measurements' is the opposite of what repeated measures produce.", "traps": {"b": "Random assignment of participants to different treatment groups defines a between-subjects design. In repeated measures everyone gets every treatment; only the order may be randomised.", "c": "Receiving only one treatment condition is the between-subjects arrangement, the opposite of a repeated measures design.", "d": "Measurements from the same people are correlated, not independent; that is why paired t-tests and repeated measures ANOVA, which model this dependence, are used."}}, {"q": "'Observational Learning' (Bandura) involves which of the following processes?", "concept": "The answer is a: Albert Bandura's observational learning works through four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) showed that people learn by watching others, called models, without being reinforced themselves. In the Bobo doll study of Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the model's specific actions and words, while children who saw a calm model did not. In a 1965 follow-up, children who saw the model punished imitated less, until they were offered a reward for showing what the model had done, when they reproduced it as well as the others. This separated learning from performance. The four processes are attention (noticing the model; we attend more to models who are similar, competent or high in status), retention (storing the behaviour as images and verbal codes), motor reproduction (being physically able to turn the memory into action, improving with practice and feedback) and motivation (a reason to perform, from external, vicarious or self-reinforcement). The first three govern learning; the fourth governs whether the behaviour is shown. Bandura later widened this into social cognitive theory (1986). Example: Kabir watches his elder sister roll chapatis. He watches her hands closely (attention), remembers 'press from the centre, turn a quarter' (retention), tries it with lumpy results at first (reproduction) and keeps practising because his mother praises him (motivation). Option a is right because these are Bandura's four named processes, in his order. Exam trap: 'Encoding, Storage, Retrieval' sounds close because retention involves memory, but it is the three-stage model of memory, not Bandura's model of modelling.", "traps": {"b": "Encoding, storage and retrieval are the three stages of memory in the information-processing approach (developed in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin, 1968). Retention overlaps with storage, but the list leaves out attention, reproduction and motivation.", "c": "Stimulus, response and consequence form the three-term contingency of the operant conditioning of Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1938), in which the learner's own behaviour is reinforced; Bandura showed learning without direct reinforcement.", "d": "Id, ego and superego are the parts of personality in Sigmund Freud's (1923) structural model, unrelated to learning by observation."}}, {"q": "Match the following. a. Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test, b. Kruskal-Wallis Test, c. Eta-squared, d. Friedman Test. 1. Non-parametric equivalent of Repeated Measures ANOVA, 2. Effect size in ANOVA, 3. Non-parametric equivalent of One-way ANOVA, 4. Non-parametric test for two related samples", "concept": "The answer is a: the correct matches are Wilcoxon signed-rank with two related samples (a-4), Kruskal-Wallis with one-way ANOVA (b-3), eta-squared with effect size in ANOVA (c-2) and Friedman with repeated measures ANOVA (d-1). Non-parametric tests replace raw scores with ranks, so they need no normality assumption and suit ordinal or skewed data. Each has a parametric partner. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) is for two related samples, such as the same patients before and after therapy. It ranks the size of each person's difference and reattaches the signs, so it uses both direction and magnitude; it is the rank-based partner of the paired t-test. The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) pools and ranks the scores of three or more independent groups and compares their mean ranks, the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA. The Friedman test, devised by the economist Milton Friedman (1937), ranks each person's scores across three or more conditions and compares the rank totals, the non-parametric equivalent of repeated measures ANOVA. Eta-squared is not a test at all: it is an effect size for ANOVA, the proportion of total variance explained by the factor (sum of squares between groups divided by the total sum of squares). Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested about .01 as small, .06 as medium and .14 as large. Example: Riya rates anxiety in the same 15 patients at weeks 0, 4 and 8 on an ordinal scale, so she uses the Friedman test. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairs correct. Exam trap: a-4 appears in both a and d, so decide on b: Kruskal-Wallis is the independent-groups (one-way) equivalent, which rules out d, where it is wrongly matched to repeated measures.", "traps": {"b": "This matches Wilcoxon to one-way ANOVA, Kruskal-Wallis to effect size and eta-squared to two related samples; only the Friedman pairing (d-1) is right.", "c": "This swaps the roles: Wilcoxon is not the repeated measures equivalent (that is Friedman), Kruskal-Wallis is not a two related samples test (that is Wilcoxon) and Friedman is not the one-way equivalent (that is Kruskal-Wallis). Only c-2 is right.", "d": "Wilcoxon (a-4) is right, but Kruskal-Wallis is for independent groups, not repeated measures; eta-squared is an effect size, not a test; and Friedman is not an effect size."}}]});
